# Cyrius — Language Features
# How to use specific Cyrius language features: types, syntax, derives, directives.
# Last full refresh 2026-06-09 at v6.1.18; v6.1.x feature additions (TS/TSX→JS
# emitter, --target=js, #if NAME>=VALUE numeric preprocessor, PIE/.gnu.hash,
# Windows directory-listing) documented below.
# v6.3.x closeout (2026-07-03, v6.3.45): +4 entries for the "language
# trio" minor — closures_v637_v638, generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310,
# async_await_futures_v6311, and derive_str_fields refreshed for f64.
# v6.4.0 (2026-07-03): CYRIUS_MONOMORPH DEFAULT-ON flip — generics no
# longer gated (CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0 opts out); generics_monomorphization
# entry updated. async/await + closures unchanged.
# v6.5.0 (2026-07-29): +1 entry visibility_public_private_v650 — the first
# enforced encapsulation boundary in the language (file-scoped `private`,
# per-item `public`, covering fns AND global vars; private items are also
# dropped from the exported symbol table).
# v6.6.5 (2026-09-18): +1 entry struct_storage_class_v665 — a struct declared inside a
# fn is a PER-CALL, PER-THREAD frame object (a literal used to compile as a global,
# shared across recursion, threads and files), and inline-vs-pointer is now RECORDED at
# the declaration rather than inferred from a neighbouring stack slot.
# v6.6.10 (2026-09-28): +1 entry compile_time_assert_v6610 — #assert (and array sizes)
# take enum constants, bare or `Enum.NAME`; sizeof is a whole word; no cascade error.
# v6.6.11 (2026-09-29): +1 entry string_literal_escapes_and_line_accounting_v6611 —
# an unknown escape is a lex error, `\`+newline keeps its bytes, a newline inside a
# string counts as a line (diagnostics were one line high per newline; CVE-55).
# Updated: enum_namespacing (the qualifier must name the variant's own enum — it was
# ignored), sizeof_operator (whole type names; mulh64 whole word; `return sizeof(..)`),
# compile_time_assert_v6610 (no-`;` assert ends at its line; failing asserts report all).
# +1 entry struct_value_copies_and_result_type_checks_v6611 — `q = b.v` copies whole, a
# struct result is type-checked at every size (incl. the leading declaration block), a
# <= 8 B typed `self` is by value, `var y: Q = x` from a by-value param copies. Updated:
# unions (the field caps fire after a union), derive_str_fields (a re-derived struct with a
# different layout is refused).
# Updated: f64_named_type_and_operators — `+= -= *= /=` on an f64/f32 GLOBAL and in a `for`
# step are float ops (a step takes all ten compound operators), f32 `+ - * /` warns on a
# non-f32 right operand (kind 5), `var t = p.y` copies the float type; its stale "an intrinsic
# cannot flank `*`" gotcha (fixed v6.4.83) is corrected.
# Updated: async_await_futures_v6311 — the reactor resumes a coroutine whose `await` did not
# park (it used to finish it with 0; the "force it yourself" advice is withdrawn).
# v6.6.12 (2026-09-30): +7 entries — typed_array_subscripts_v6612 (`a[i]`, `a[i] = v`,
# `a[i] OP= v` on an integer `var a: T[N]`; the language had no subscript before),
# struct_literals_generic_heads_and_nested_values_v6612 (`Box<Pt> { p, 5 }`, a whole struct
# value for a nested field, nested leaves written at their own width),
# field_chains_any_depth_and_call_result_fields_v6612 (`n.v.v.x`, `mk(3).v.y`),
# struct_field_arguments_and_toplevel_copy_init_v6612 (`take(r.v)` copies, `var B: Pt = A;`
# at top level; the source must be declared ABOVE), unterminated_string_literal_is_an_error_v6612,
# aarch64_xattr_statx_rlimit_fchown_wrappers_and_native_region_exemption_v6612 (the 1000+N
# alias band again; no raw-literal warning inside an aarch64-only region; the 3-arg kill arity
# warning is back on x86-Linux), cx_tail_calls_and_cxvm_guest_traps_v6612 (CVE-58).
# Updated: multi_width_types (a narrow variable is written and read at its width on the `for`
# step and compound-operator paths too; the zero-initialised narrow-global clobber is still
# open), generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310 (the generic struct literal and the bare
# explicit-generic statement `id<i32>(4);`).
# v6.6.17 (2026-10-05): +5 entries — pointer_fields_returns_and_linked_lists_v6617 (`next: *Node`,
# `fn f(): *Node`, dot syntax on `*Struct` locals / globals / results, chains through pointer fields),
# typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617 (`p + n` = sizeof(T) at every site; `q - p` counts elements),
# method_chains_v6617, nested_closure_capture_v6617 (the "captured closures are flat" rule is gone),
# nested_generic_type_arguments_v6617 (`Vec<Box<i64>>`, `sizeof(Box<i32>)`, `(Box<i64>, i64)`).
# v6.4.4 (2026-07-05): +1 entry f32v4_packed_single_simd — SIMD arc
# Phase 1 (128-bit packed single, structured-descriptor encoding, x86).
# v6.4.5 (2026-07-05): SIMD arc Phase 2 — f32v_fmadd/f32v_dot matmul ops
# + f32v4_fmadd/_dot wrappers folded into f32v4_packed_single_simd.
# v6.4.6 (2026-07-05): +1 entry integer_vector_simd — SIMD arc Phase 3a
# (i8/i16/i32/i64 vectors + iv_add/sub/mul, x86).
# v6.4.7 (2026-07-05): SIMD arc Phase 3b — value-form typed int ops
# (i32v4_add(a,b) etc.) + iv_dp8 (u8·i8→i32 widening dot, b1.58 GEMM)
# folded into integer_vector_simd. Integer-SIMD arc closes.
# v6.4.8 (2026-07-05): +1 entry f32v8_avx2_256bit_simd — SIMD arc Phase 4
# R1: f32v8 256-bit AVX2 elementwise (first VEX/AVX) + CPUID runtime fallback.
# v6.4.9 (2026-07-05): SIMD arc Phase 4 R2 — f32v8_fma (vfmadd231ps, first
# 3-byte VEX) + f32v8_dot (8-lane vextractf128 reduce) folded into
# f32v8_avx2_256bit_simd. Phase 4 CLOSES (f32 SIMD complete on x86).
# v6.4.82 (2026-07-27) — v6.4.x CLOSEOUT sweep. Two entries were
# ACTIVELY WRONG, not merely stale, and were rewritten:
#   * f64_named_type_and_operators said "f32 is type-only ... no f32
#     arithmetic". WRONG since v6.4.56 — f32 scalar + - * / and
#     comparisons ship on x86/PE/Mach-O/aarch64-NEON/cx. Also folded in
#     v6.4.55 (scalar f64 as a fn RETURN type, xmm0, sentinel -9; f32 is
#     NOT a legal return type) and v6.4.57 (float params + compound
#     assign + the WARN-only float typecheck).
#   * cap_raises_v51118_v51119 published the retired 262144-byte
#     identifier cap as current. It is 524288 B (512 KB) since v6.4.76,
#     and the fn_table warning now measures against the 32768 hard
#     CEILING (v6.4.75) rather than the doubling live cap.
# +2 entries: reserved_word_class_toknames (the 67-name class +
# the v6.4.77 naming diagnostic), diagnostics_column_caret_multierror
# (v6.4.60 column/caret, v6.4.62 multi-error + no-output-on-error +
# anti-hang, v6.4.78 EOF clamp).
# v6.5.10 (2026-08-07) doc-currency sweep for .6-.10:
#   * arena_lifecycle_v51114 rewritten — the v6.5.9 growable arena +
#     ARENA_FULL_* exhaustion policy (one policy field, not three
#     features). The old entry described a FIXED arena whose only
#     exhaustion behaviour was a 0 return, which is now the default
#     rather than the whole story.
#   * +1 entry threads_detached_and_is_done_v658 — thread_create_detached
#     / thread_is_done, and why sys_exit_group (v6.5.6) is the correct
#     epilogue once any thread exists.
# v6.6.16 (2026-10-05): +4 entries — chan_blocking_capability_v6616 (CHAN_BLOCKING; the
# channel is MPMC and blocks on arm64 macOS + Windows), indirect_calls_at_top_level_since_6_6_16
# (top-level callptr / fncallN on a capturing closure), struct_assignment_handle_and_value_v6616
# (handle <-> value assignment copies), struct_parameter_is_a_copy_v6616 (a struct parameter is
# the callee's copy). REWRITTEN: byte_array_literal (`var X: T[N] = {..}` is N elements of T,
# baked into the image; the old body was wrong on three counts), sizeof_operator (every type
# name — f64, u8, bool, vectors, enums; `sizeof(T)` with T = f64 read 9). Updated:
# struct_field_widths (bool fields), struct_storage_class_v665 (`d = c` copies),
# threads_concurrent_capability_v6544 (Windows is 1; a gate can enforce an under-report),
# stack_enum_unboxed_payload_variants_v6555 (three RULES bullets stale since 6.6.0; generic and
# method spellings), the ATTRIBUTES block (#deprecated on every call path), the 6.6.11 / 6.6.12
# struct-parameter entries (the copy is the callee's now).
# 87 entries (DERIVED at 6.6.16 with `grep -c '^\[\[entries\]\]'` — this line read 66 while the
# file held 83: nobody re-derived it from v6.5.10 to 6.6.15).

[[entries]]
name = "byte_array_literal"
description = "var X: T[N] = { .. } — N elements of T baked into the image; the bare var b[N] = { .. } is a byte list (v5.11.51; typed and baked 6.6.16)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX (module scope):
    enum Op { ADD = 1; SUB = 2; }
    var table: u16[4] = {0xFFFF, Op.SUB, 1 << 8, -1};   # 0xFFFF, 2, 256, 0xFFFF
    var scale: f64[2] = {1.5, -0.25};                   # exact binary64 bits
    var half:  f32[2] = {0.1, 3.4};                     # rounded to f32, ties to even
    var wide:  u128[1] = {0xFF};                        # low 8 bytes; high 8 are 0
    var guid[2] = { 0x8B, 0xE4, 0xDF, 0x61, 0x93, 0xCA, 0x11, 0xD2,
                    0xAA, 0x0D, 0x00, 0xE0, 0x98, 0x03, 0x2B, 0x8C };   # the BARE form: bytes

SEMANTICS (6.6.16):
    `var X: T[N] = { e, .. }` is N elements of T. An element is an integer
    literal, an enum constant (`A` or `E.A`, declared on either side), or a
    constant expression of them (`1 << 4`, `E.A * 2`, `-5`); an f64 / f32
    element also takes a float literal, optionally negated. Elements past
    the list are 0. More than N elements is refused, naming N.
    RANGE: one window per width, -2^(8w-1) .. 2^(8w)-1 — `0xFF` into an i8
    and `-1` into a u8 are the same bits; a value that would be truncated is
    refused. i64 / u64 / bool / an enum / *T take any 64-bit value.
    The BARE `var b[N] = { .. }` is a list of BYTES in [0, 255], at most
    N * 8 of them at top level (its historical meaning — a bare top-level
    `var b[N]` is N * 8 bytes), and takes enum constants and constant
    expressions too. A shorter list was always legal; the rest is 0.
    REFUSED BY NAME: a call, variable or string element ("must be
    constants"); a float literal in an integer array; a struct, union,
    vector, slice, cstring, Str, Result / Option / Tagged or Vec element
    type ("store the elements explicitly").
    POSITION: the same declaration after the first top-level statement means
    the same thing. Inside a top-level block (while / if / elif / else / for
    and its init clause / a switch case or default arm / match / @unsafe / a
    bare { }) a list is refused — a baked list would hold its values once,
    where a scalar's initializer runs every time the block does.
    A fn-local array takes no list.
    REDECLARATION: a list is a constant initializer (6.6.6's value rule).
    Over an earlier `= 0` or computed initializer of the name it is the value
    from program start — `var a = f(); var a[1] = {0, 4};` runs f() and reads
    0x400; over an earlier scalar constant it keeps the bytes it does not list
    (`var a = 0x0506; var a[1] = {9};` reads 0x0509); two lists overlay in
    order; a constant scalar after a list wins whole.

CODEGEN (6.6.16):
    NONE on an image target. Pass 1 (PARSE_GVAR_ARR) checks the element type
    and the count; the replay (EMIT_GVAR_INITS -> _gai_replay) evaluates each
    element with the constant folder (_CF_TRY, enum arm on) into the slot's
    blob (_vgai_base); every image writer copies the blob into the var area
    (_EMIT_GVAR_STATIC_INITS -> _gai_bake), x86 ELF / aarch64 / PE / EFI /
    Mach-O x86 and arm64 alike. So the values hold from the first
    instruction — in a `kernel;` build too, where the old run-time stores ran
    after a top-level program that never returns (B read 0). cx too: a .cyx
    carries its var data after the bytecode and cxvm copies it into the
    guest, so main_cx.cyr bakes the same blob there. An initializer that runs
    earlier — even one reading the array before its declaration — sees the
    list. (Compiler side: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
    `a_guard_in_the_loop_implies_a_second_pass_somebody_has_to_write`.)

⚠ UNTIL 6.6.16 the list was a byte list WHATEVER T was:
    `var t: i64[3] = {1, 2, 42};` read t[0] == 0x2A0201, t[1] == t[2] == 0
    on every target; 256, -3, 1.5 and an enum constant were refused, and
    Pt[2] / bool[2] / i8v16[2] lists compiled silently into bytes. The old
    entry here also said "element count must equal N" (never true) and
    described a store8 sequence per byte (removed by the bake).

CONSUMER:
    gnoboot (sovereign UEFI bootloader, `kernel;` + CYRIUS_TARGET_EFI=1):
    29 bare lists — EFI GUIDs and UTF-16LE strings. Same bytes since 6.6.16,
    now in the image (the binary lost 2 KB of stores).

[[entries]]
name = "efi_main_entry_convention"
description = "fn efi_main(handle, st) — UEFI Application entry convention (v5.11.52)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    fn efi_main(handle, st): i64 {
        # handle = EFI_HANDLE (UEFI ImageHandle)
        # st     = *EFI_SYSTEM_TABLE
        # Return: EFI_STATUS (0 = EFI_SUCCESS).
        return 0;
    }

WHEN IT'S USED:
    Only when `CYRIUS_TARGET_EFI=1` is set AND the program
    declares a `fn efi_main(handle, st)`. cyrius emits a
    trampoline that:
      1. Reads firmware args saved at entry (R14 ← RCX =
         ImageHandle; R15 ← RDX = SystemTable).
      2. Allocates the MS x64 shadow space + 8-byte stack
         alignment.
      3. Calls efi_main(handle, st) directly.
      4. Restores stack; EEXIT under EFI mode emits a single
         `ret` — firmware reads rax as EFI_STATUS.

ERGONOMICS:
    Pre-v5.11.52, gnoboot had to hand-write the trampoline +
    grab args from registers manually. Post-.52, the entry
    convention is implicit: just declare `fn efi_main(handle,
    st)` and the compiler wires it up.

GOTCHA (resolved at v5.11.53): MR-form `mov r/m64, r64`
(opcode `89 /r`) puts dst in r/m field, src in reg field. To
extend dst to r14/r15 needs REX.B; v5.11.52's first cut set
REX.R instead — gnoboot saw `mov rsi, r9` decoded bytes
instead of `mov r14, rcx`. v5.11.53 hotfix corrected the REX
prefix `0x4C → 0x49` in `src/main.cyr:1273` save emit.
Restore (line ~1369) was correct as-is.

CROSS-REF: see field_notes/kernel.cyml for the full UEFI
Application emit arc (v5.11.47-.49 + .52-.53).

[[entries]]
name = "multi_width_types"
description = "Multi-width types (i8, i16, i32, i64, u128) with width-correct codegen"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    var x: i8 = 42;       # 1-byte variable
    var y: i16 = 30000;   # 2-byte variable
    var z: i32 = 100000;  # 4-byte variable
    var w: i64 = 0;       # explicit 8-byte (same as untyped)
    var u: u128 = 0;      # 16-byte (storage only, arithmetic via lib/u128.cyr)

SEMANTICS:
    Type annotations control BOTH storage size AND load/store width.
    var x: i8 = 256; → stores 0 (truncated to 1 byte).
    Reading x back gives 0 (movzx zero-extends to rax).

    Globals: var_sizes[idx] = 1/2/4/8/16 (actual byte allocation in data section).
    Locals: 8-byte stack slots, but loads/stores use correct width within the slot.

CODEGEN:
    EVLOAD_W(S, idx, width) / EVSTORE_W(S, idx, width) for globals.
    EFLLOAD_W(S, idx, width) / EFLSTORE_W(S, idx, width) for locals.
    Width 1: movzx rax, byte [addr] / mov byte [addr], al
    Width 2: movzx eax, word [addr] / mov word [addr], ax
    Width 4: mov eax, [addr]        / mov dword [addr], eax
    Width 8: mov rax, [addr]        / mov qword [addr], rax (default)

GOTCHA (resolved at v5.8.16 §8 layout-flip): pre-v5.8.16,
`var x: u128 = 0;` at fn scope only allocated an 8-byte slot —
type-annotated locals carried size on globals but not locals.
Two adjacent fn-local 16-byte vars (slices, u128) silently
shared a stack slot, with `slice_set` / `store64(&x+8, ...)`
overflowing into saved-rbp territory. Post-v5.8.16:
scalar_type=16 fn-locals reserve TWO 8-byte slots, with the
canonical (named) slot at the LOWER stack address — `&x + 8`
correctly addresses the high half. The pre-v5.8.16 workaround
(`var buf[16]; pass as pointer`) still works but is no longer
needed — `var x: u128 = 0;` is fully load-bearing today. See
slice_type_v58x for the same fix on slices.

⚠ EVERY STORE PATH IS WIDTH-CORRECT SINCE 6.6.12 — two were not. Narrow
GLOBALS are PACKED (`var a: u8 = 1; var b: u8 = 2;` puts `b` at `a + 1`),
so an 8-byte store to `a` writes over the next seven bytes of globals:
  - the classic-`for` STEP kept its own copy of the assignment store and
    it was always 8 bytes. On a u8 global,
    `for (a = 250; a != 252; a = a + 1)` zeroed the packed globals after
    `a` (a neighbour set to 7 read 0), and a step `a += 1` wrapping 255
    carried into the next global (7 became 8). On a u8 LOCAL stepped
    past 255 the 8-byte slot held 256 — the narrow read showed 0, so the
    loop looked right — until a compound op read the whole slot:
    `i >>= 1` gave 128, not 0;
  - a COMPOUND operator's load read 8 bytes and did not sign-extend:
    with packed u8 globals `a = 4` and `b = 1`, `a >>= 1` shifted b's byte
    into `a` (130, not 2), and `c /= 2` on an i8 local -8 gave 124, not -4.
Both now go through the statement's store (`_asg_store_slot`) and the
expression read's width-and-sign load, on x86, aarch64, PE, both Mach-O
backends and cx (whose narrow store/load emitters were 8 bytes wide as
well). The `for` step also draws the statement's narrowing warning now.
Every shape above was measured wrong on 6.6.11 and right on 6.6.12 (x86;
aarch64 under qemu and cx under cxvm right on 6.6.12 too). A plain
statement `a += 1` was already right: the statement arm always stored
at the slot's width.

⚠ STILL OPEN at 6.6.12 (backlog, not fixed by the above): in the leading
declaration block, a narrow global initialised to ZERO clobbers the narrow
global after it — `var a: u8 = 0; var b: u8 = 7;` reads `b` as 0 (the u8
pair on x86, aarch64 under qemu and PE under wine, and an i32 pair on x86;
cx reads 7; 6.6.11 the same). A non-zero initialiser,
the reverse order, or the pair declared after the first top-level
statement reads 7. Until it is fixed, give such a global a non-zero
initialiser or set it at run time.

BACKWARD COMPATIBLE: Untyped variables remain i64. The compiler
source uses no type annotations.

[[entries]]
name = "sizeof_operator"
description = "sizeof(Type) compile-time constant — every type name, matched whole (6.6.16)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    var n = sizeof(i8);      # 1      sizeof(u8)  1
    var n = sizeof(i16);     # 2      sizeof(u16) 2
    var n = sizeof(i32);     # 4      sizeof(u32) 4
    var n = sizeof(i64);     # 8      sizeof(u64) 8
    var n = sizeof(u128);    # 16     (also in #assert since 6.6.16)
    var n = sizeof(f32);     # 4      sizeof(f64) 8
    var n = sizeof(bool);    # 8      cstring, Result, Option, Tagged, Vec, an enum: 8
    var n = sizeof(f64v2);   # 16     every 128-bit vector 16; f64v4 / f32v8 32
    var n = sizeof(Point);   # STRUCTSZ — unpadded, recursive
    fn g<T>(p: T): i64 { var k = sizeof(T); return k; }   # T's argument's size; the i64 base 8

IMPLEMENTATION (6.6.16):
    PARSE_FACTOR spots the IDENT `sizeof` (`_is_sizeof_word`, whole word —
    NOT a lexer keyword); `#assert` reads the same through
    `_EVAL_CONST_ATOM`. Both call `_tn_sizeof` (src/frontend/parse_types.cyr):
    a bound type parameter is its argument's size, else the one type-name
    resolver `_tn_resolve` (struct lookup FIRST, then the builtin names,
    matched WHOLE), and `_tn_bytes` gives the size — exactly what
    `var a: T[N]` reserves per element, so the array is N * sizeof(T) by
    construction. The vocabulary: language/core.cyml `type_names_v6616`.

⚠ UNTIL 6.6.16 sizeof knew only i8..i64, u128 and structs: `sizeof(f64)`,
    `sizeof(u8)`, `sizeof(bool)`, `sizeof(cstring)`, an enum and every vector
    type were `sizeof: unknown type`, and `#assert sizeof(u128)` was
    `expected ')', got u128`. A generic's `sizeof(T)` with T = f64 read 9
    (the f64 return code -9 taken as a width); under CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0 an
    unbound T was `unknown type`.

⚠ A NAME THAT IS NO TYPE is refused naming it: `unknown type 'i8zz' in
    sizeof` / `... in #assert sizeof` (plus `note: 'i8zz' is not 'i8' - a type
    name must match whole`), and an #assert whose operand was refused does
    not add a second `#assert failed`. `ptr` is not a cyrius type (`*T` is the
    pointer spelling). History: the word was matched by a 6-byte prefix until
    6.6.10 (`sizeofzz(P)` was sizeof; `mulh64x(a, b)` was the intrinsic until
    6.6.11); the TYPE by a prefix until 6.6.11 (`sizeof(i16v8)` was 2 — it is
    16 now, a vector).

⚠ `return sizeof(T);` AND `return mulh64(a, b);` WORK SINCE 6.6.11.
    Both are IDENT-spelled intrinsics lowered inline; the tail-call path
    used to jump to a fn of that name (`_is_ident_intrinsic` diverts it).

[[entries]]
name = "unions"
description = "union keyword — all fields at offset 0, size = max field"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    union Value {
        as_int;
        as_ptr;
    }

SEMANTICS:
    All fields share offset 0. sizeof(Value) = max(sizeof(field)).
    Field access: v.as_int and v.as_ptr read from the same memory.
    Init: var v = Value { 42, 42 }; (all fields, like struct init).

IMPLEMENTATION:
    Token 101 (UNION). Parsed by PARSE_UNION_DEF — same as
    PARSE_STRUCT_DEF but sets high bit (bit 63) of struct_fcounts
    as union flag. ISUNION(S, si) checks bit 63. FIELDOFF returns
    0 for all union fields. STRUCTSZ returns max field size
    instead of sum.

    ⚠ The flag rides in the field COUNT, so every reader of that
    count must mask it. Until 6.6.11 ADDFIELD / ADDFIELDTYPED /
    ADDFIELDFK used it raw: the count was negative, so the
    256-fields-per-struct cap never fired for a union (a 300-field
    union compiled rc 0), and the flag leaked into the field-pool
    top, so after ANY union the 8192-entry pool cap stopped firing
    for every later struct too (40 structs of 250 fields wrote past
    the pool and failed with an unrelated "passing integer literal
    8 to 'alloc'"). Both caps fire now ("too many struct fields
    (max 256)"); compiled output is unchanged.

[[entries]]
name = "bitfield_builtins"
description = "Bit manipulation builtins — bit_get/set/clear, popcount, ctz/clz"
category = "cyrius"
---
Bit-level operations as compiler builtins:

    bit_get(value, n)        — extract bit n (returns 0 or 1)
    bit_set(value, n)        — set bit n
    bit_clear(value, n)      — clear bit n
    bit_toggle(value, n)     — flip bit n
    popcount(value)          — count set bits (POPCNT instruction)
    ctz(value)               — count trailing zeros (BSF / TZCNT)
    clz(value)               — count leading zeros (BSR / LZCNT)

These compile to single x86 instructions where available (POPCNT
/ BSF / BSR via SSE4.2 / BMI1). Fallback paths for older CPUs
emit equivalent unrolled loops.

[[entries]]
name = "struct_field_widths"
description = "Per-field type annotations on structs — size and alignment correctness"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    struct Header {
        magic:   i32;       # 4 bytes
        version: i16;       # 2 bytes
        flags:   i8;        # 1 byte
        _pad:    i8;        # 1 byte (manual padding)
        size:    i64;       # 8 bytes
    }

SEMANTICS:
    Field types control individual field widths. STRUCTSZ sums them.
    FIELDOFF returns the byte offset of each field considering prior
    fields' widths.

    Unannotated fields default to i64 (8 bytes). Mix is fine:

    struct Mixed { tag: i32; payload; }   # 4 + 8 = 12 bytes

    Programmer is responsible for alignment-aware ordering. The
    compiler does NOT auto-pad — write padding fields explicitly
    if a downstream consumer needs C ABI compatibility.

⚠ 6.6.16: `bool` is a field type — 8 bytes, a plain word like u8..u64 (it was
    refused here while an array element took it). The field vocabulary is the
    one type-name resolver's (`_tn_resolve`, language/core.cyml
    `type_names_v6616`); the refusal note lists bool, Result, Option and
    Tagged, and a misspelt prefix gets the `'i8x' is not 'i8'` hint.

[[entries]]
name = "struct_storage_class_v665"
description = "Where a struct lives: inside a fn it is a PER-CALL, PER-THREAD frame object; at top level it is one shared object; `var p: T = <expr>` yielding an address is a POINTER to someone else's struct (v6.6.5)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX AND MEANING:
    fn f(n) {
        var a = P { n, n * 2 };   # per call, per thread
        var b: P;                 # per call, per thread
        var c: P = mk();          # a POINTER to what mk() returned — not a copy
        var d: P = a;             # a COPY of a (all STRUCTSZ bytes)
        d = c;                    # since 6.6.16: a COPY of what c points at (c is a pointer)
        return a.x + c.y;
    }
    var G = P { 1, 2 };           # ONE shared object in the data section

WHAT CHANGED AT v6.6.5:
    A struct LITERAL written inside a fn used to be compiled as a GLOBAL —
    shared across recursion, shared across threads, and visible to every
    later reference of that name in ANY file. `var cfg = P { 1, 2 };` inside
    a library fn captured a consumer's top-level `var cfg = 5;`, silently.
    It is a frame object now, like `var p: T;` has always been.

    The compiler also RECORDS, at the declaration, whether a variable is the
    struct or a pointer to one. Before v6.6.5 it inferred that from the shape
    of the neighbouring stack slot and got it wrong for any pointer-mode
    struct declared after a closed `{ ... }` block or after a `callptr` /
    `bitset` / SIMD expression.

WHAT FOLLOWS FROM IT:
  - Operator overloading (`P_add`, `P_mul`, …) dispatches for a struct VALUE
    wherever it lives — literal local, `var p: P;`, `var p: P = 1`, global —
    and does NOT dispatch for a pointer-mode local, where `+` is pointer
    arithmetic. Before v6.6.5 a struct LOCAL silently did an integer add.
  - A closure capturing an enclosing struct or array copies it BY VALUE,
    including a stack array referenced as `&buf`.
  - `fn mk(): P { var p = P { 7, 8 }; return p; }` returns both fields.
  - A multi-word struct local inside a SUSPENDING `async fn` is refused with
    a diagnostic: a coroutine's heap frame lays its slots out in the opposite
    direction to the stack, so `&p + off` would address a different local.
    Single-word locals are fine.

IF YOU RELIED ON THE OLD BEHAVIOUR (a literal that persisted across calls),
declare it at top level — that is what it actually was.

[[entries]]
name = "expression_type_propagation"
description = "Type annotations propagate through expressions for width-correct codegen"
category = "cyrius"
---
When operands have known widths, the compiler picks the correct
load/store size at codegen time. Mixing widths warns:

    var x: i32 = 100000;
    var y: i64 = 5;
    var z = x + y;        # warning: i32 + i64 — narrowing

    var x: i8 = 200;
    var n = x;            # n is i64; movzx zero-extends to 8 bytes

    var p: *i32 = ...;
    *p = 42;              # 4-byte store via mov dword [p], 42

NOTES:
- No implicit signed-vs-unsigned conversions; all integer types
  are signed under the `iN` annotation.
- Pointer arithmetic on typed pointers (`*i32`) scales by element
  size — `p + 1` advances 4 bytes for `*i32`, 8 bytes for `*i64`.

[[entries]]
name = "defer_statement"
description = "defer { body } — execute cleanup code at function return (LIFO)"
category = "cyrius"
---
defer { body } executes body when the enclosing function returns.
Multiple defers execute in LIFO order (last declared = first to run).
The WHOLE return convention survives the defer bodies (cycc 6.6.7): an
Ok/Err payload, a tuple / ret2 pair, a two-word struct, an f64, an
f64v2/f64v4 — on x86 rax/rdx/r8/xmm0/xmm1, on aarch64 x0/x2/x3/q0/q1,
on cx r0-r5. Before 6.6.7 only rax was kept, so a defer that called
anything could zero an Ok payload. Since 6.6.15 the 64-byte area those
registers are saved in is CLEARED before it is released (+74 B x86_64,
+16 B aarch64 per fn with a defer or secret var): it used to outlive a
`secret var` wipe, holding whatever the return registers held (CVE-69).

EVERY RETURN PATH (cycc 6.6.7):
  A defer (and every `secret var` zeroise) runs on every return, a
  tail-shaped `return f(x);` included — and `return Ok(v);` /
  `return Err(e);` are exactly that shape. So a fn with a defer or a
  secret var anywhere in its body NEVER tail-calls, and is never
  inlined (`#inline` warns and is ignored). Deep self-recursion in such
  a fn keeps a frame per level: loop instead, or move the defer into a
  wrapper around a defer-free helper. Before 6.6.7 the tail call jumped
  past every defer (a skipped unlock = a deadlock; a skipped secret
  zeroise = CVE-47).

WHERE IT MAY APPEAR (cycc 6.6.7 — each used to compile silently wrong):
  - only inside a fn (closure and async fn included); top-level defer
    is refused, like `secret var` / `stack var`
  - a defer body cannot leave its fn: return / `?` / ret2 inside it are
    refused, as is a break/continue jumping out of it (a loop opened
    inside the body is fine); a break/continue with nothing to leave in
    the same fn is refused too (it used to jump into another fn)
  - a defer inside a closure belongs to the CLOSURE and runs when the
    closure returns (it used to register on the enclosing fn — CVE-46
    for a closure's `secret var`)
  - in a coroutine async fn it runs once, at completion; an `await`
    inside a defer body is refused
  - not in a `#naked` fn (6.6.15, CVE-72): a naked fn has no epilogue,
    so the block never ran and a `secret var` was never zeroised —
    both now refused by name, as `return` always was. On cx `#naked` is
    inert and the defer runs.
  The five ways a `secret var` wipe has been skipped or copied around:
  field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
  `secret_var_zeroisation_skipped_four_ways_and_copied_around_once_enumerate_every_exit_and_every_epilogue_store`.

USAGE:
  fn process_file(path) {
      var fd = file_open(path, O_RDONLY, 0);
      defer { file_close(fd); }
      defer { file_unlock(fd); }
      file_lock(fd);
      # ... work with file ...
      return result;  # defers run: unlock, then close
  }

EXIT-PATH SAFETY:
  Per-defer runtime flags (hidden locals). Each defer sets a flag
  to 1 when reached. At epilogue, flag is checked before running
  the block. Unreached defers are skipped — early return BEFORE
  a defer statement won't trigger that defer's cleanup (closing
  the resource-double-free class).

IMPLEMENTATION:
  Token 106. Deferred blocks compiled inline (jumped over during
  normal flow). At function epilogue, emit jmp→block→jmp→block→
  epilogue chain. Each block's end-jmp patched to return to the
  chain.

Max 8 defer blocks per function. Zig/Odin parity.

[[entries]]
name = "ifplat_directive"
description = "#ifplat PLAT / #endplat — sugar for #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_<UPPER>"
category = "cyrius"
---
`#ifplat PLAT` / `#endplat` is a cleaner spelling for arch-gated
blocks. Strictly additive — the old `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86` /
`#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` forms keep working.

USAGE:

    #ifplat x86
    asm { ... x86 bytes ... }
    #endplat
    #ifplat aarch64
    asm { ... aarch64 bytes ... }
    #endplat

SUPPORTED PLATFORMS:
- x86      → resolves to CYRIUS_ARCH_X86
- aarch64  → resolves to CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64
- Unknown tokens (e.g. riscv64) fail closed — the body is
  skipped unconditionally.

SEMANTICS:
Identical to `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_<UPPER>`. The evaluator
hashes the token to the canonical compiler-builtin symbol
and looks it up in the same flag table as #ifdef. No separate
state; no new scopes.

NESTED BLOCKS work (skip_depth tracking mirrors #ifdef).
`#ifplat` can be nested inside `#ifdef` and vice versa.

WHEN TO USE:
- New code that gates on architecture: prefer `#ifplat`.
- Existing code using `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_*`: no migration
  required. Both forms are first-class.

FUTURE: the `(arch, format)` tuple form — e.g.
`#ifplat aarch64-macho`, `#ifplat x86-pe` — is reserved.

[[entries]]
name = "enum_namespacing"
description = "Enum.VARIANT syntax — qualified enum access in all expression contexts"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    enum Color { RED; GREEN; BLUE; }
    var c = Color.RED;
    if (c == Color.BLUE) { ... }
    fn paint(color) { return color == Color.GREEN; }

SEMANTICS:
    Enum.VARIANT works everywhere an expression is valid:
    assignments, comparisons, function arguments, return values,
    array indices — and, since 6.6.10, `#assert` operands and
    array sizes (`var buf[Sz.BUF]`).

    ⚠ THE QUALIFIER IS CHECKED (cycc 6.6.11). The base must name an
    enum, and VARIANT must be a member of an enum of THAT name:

        enum E1 { EA = 1; EB = 2; }
        enum E2 { EC = 3; }
        Foo.EB     # error: 'Foo' is not an enum
        E2.EB      # error: 'EB' is not a variant of 'E2'

    The same resolver (`_enum_qual_resolve`, parse.cyr) serves
    expressions, `#assert` and array sizes, and each mistake is
    reported once. An enum NAME may be declared more than once
    (every lib/syscalls*.cyr declares `Signal`); the variant is
    looked up across all enums of that name.

    Two enums may share a variant name, and the qualified form is
    how you pick one: with `enum A { X = 1; }` and
    `enum B { X = 2; }`, `A.X` is 1 and `B.X` is 2. The BARE `X`
    keeps "last definition wins" (2) and warns
    `duplicate symbol 'X' redefined with conflicting value`.

    BEFORE 6.6.11 THE QUALIFIER WAS IGNORED. The parser only asked
    whether the name after the dot was a global, so `Foo.EB` and
    `E2.EB` compiled to EB's value, `Nope.gz` loaded a plain global
    `gz`, and `A.X` read the LAST enum's X (A.X * 10 + B.X was 22,
    not 12). Code that compiled only because of that now fails —
    at the 6.6.11 cut, four consumers carried stale kavach
    spellings (`Backend.PROCESS` for the renamed `KavachBackend`,
    `KavachError.OK` for `KavachError.KAVACH_ERR_OK`, which had
    been reading whichever global named `OK` came last). Across
    1,655 ecosystem builds nothing else changed.

IMPLEMENTATION:
    Enum variants are still compiled as global constants; the
    qualifier is resolved through the enum table (`_enum_id_of`,
    `_enum_variant_in`) rather than by treating `Enum.` as noise.
    A base that is a VARIABLE is a field access, not an enum
    access (`p.x` never consults the enum table).

⚠ AN ENUM CONSTANT IS NOT AN LVALUE (cycc 6.7.3). With
    `enum E { A = 5; }`, these are refused by name:
    `A = 6;`, `A += 1;` (every OP=), `&A`, `E.A = 6;`, `E.A += 1;`,
    `&E.A`, and a for step that writes one.

        error: cannot assign to enum constant 'A' - an enum constant is
               a fixed value, not a variable (declare a `var` to change it)

    `&Ctor` of a payload variant is refused the same way (it was the
    variant's slot, never the constructor's address). The check uses
    the slot the name RESOLVES to, so a local `var A`, a closure's
    capture of one, and a later top-level `var A` (last definition
    wins) stay variables.
    BEFORE 6.7.3 the bare forms compiled silently. Every read folds
    to 5, but the write landed in the variant's 8-byte slot and `&A`
    returned that slot: `A = 6; A * 10 + load64(&A)` was 56. The
    qualified write was refused only as "undefined variable 'E'".
    Gate: tests/gates/frontend/enum_const_not_lvalue.sh.

[[entries]]
name = "ref_directive"
description = "#ref directive — compile-time TOML loading"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    #ref "config.toml"
    # If config.toml contains:
    #   width = 800
    #   height = 600
    # Then after preprocessing, the source contains:
    #   var width = 800;
    #   var height = 600;

SEMANTICS:
    #ref "file.toml" loads a TOML file at compile time and emits
    var declarations for each key-value pair. Skips comments (#),
    section headers ([section]), and blank lines. String values
    become string literals, numeric values become integer literals.

IMPLEMENTATION:
    PP_REF_PASS runs as a separate preprocessing pass BEFORE PP_PASS.
    This ordering is critical — #ref must expand before #ifdef and
    other preprocessor directives see the generated var declarations.

    ISREF() detects the #ref directive. The pass reads the TOML
    file, parses key = value lines, and emits `var key = value;`
    for each valid entry.

[[entries]]
name = "ret2_rethi"
description = "Return two values via rax:rdx register pair"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    fn divmod(a, b) {
        var q = a / b;
        var r = a % b;
        ret2(q, r);        # return two values: rax=q, rdx=r
    }
    var result = divmod(17, 5);   # rax = 3 (quotient)
    var remainder = rethi();      # rdx = 2 (remainder)

SEMANTICS:
    ret2(a, b) packs two values into the rax:rdx register pair and
    returns. The first argument goes to rax (normal return), the
    second to rdx. rethi() retrieves the second return value from
    rdx (mov rax, rdx).

    Follows the System V AMD64 ABI convention where functions can
    return two 64-bit values in rax and rdx.

ALTERNATIVE — NATIVE MULTI-RETURN (v3.7.2):
    fn divmod(a, b) { return (a / b, a % b); }   # PARENS REQUIRED
    var q, r = divmod(10, 3);                    # targets are NOT parenthesised

⚠ THE PARENS ARE MANDATORY ON THE RETURN AND FORBIDDEN ON THE BIND.
This entry documented `return a / b, a % b;` — WITHOUT parens — from
v3.7.2 until v6.5.21. That form is a parse error
(`expected ';', got ','`), and `var (q, r) = f();` is another
(`expected identifier, got '('`). The cost was not theoretical: an
abaco consumer filed a proposal in 2026-08 asking for multi-value
returns as a NEW feature, its "verified against 6.5.20" table listing
exactly these two spellings as unsupported — because vidya, the place
CLAUDE.md tells you to search before reimplementing, had the working
syntax written down wrong. The feature had shipped four majors earlier.

DECLARED FORM (v6.5.21) — arity and element types in the signature:
    fn two_product(a, b): (f64, f64) { return (p, err); }
    var prod, e = two_product(x, y);   # both bindings typed f64

Arity 2 or 3. Declaring the return type is what lets the compiler
arity-check a FORWARD call and type the bindings; an undeclared
`return (a, b);` still works and stays untyped.

Two values come back via rax + rdx (x0/x2 on aarch64, r0/r4 on cx);
a third uses r8 / x3 / r5. ret2/rethi remain the lower-level primitive
and share the same register pair.

IMPLEMENTATION:
    Tokens: ret2 = 97, rethi = 98. Handled in PARSE_SIMD_EXT.

    ret2(a, b):
      1. Evaluate b → rax
      2. mov rdx, rax    # second value in rdx
      3. Evaluate a → rax  # first value in rax
      4. Emit function return (epilogue + ret)

    rethi():
      1. mov rax, rdx    # move second return value to rax

[[entries]]
name = "continue_in_for_loops"
description = "continue statement with forward-patch mechanism for for/while-loops"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    for (var i = 0; i < 10; i = i + 1) {
        if (i == 5) { continue; }
        print_i64(i);
    }

SEMANTICS:
    continue skips the rest of the loop body and jumps to the step
    expression (i = i + 1) in for-loops, or to the condition check
    in while-loops.

IMPLEMENTATION:
    Forward-patch mechanism. Array of up to 8 continue patches per
    loop scope.

    For-loop compilation:
    1. Compile init (var i = 0)
    2. Emit condition check (i < 10)
    3. Compile body — any `continue` emits a jmp with placeholder
       and records the patch location in the array
    4. After body, record current position as the step expression start
    5. Compile step (i = i + 1)
    6. Patch all continue jmps to point to step expression

[[entries]]
name = "derive_str_fields"
description = "#derive(Serialize) — auto-generate JSON serializer/deserializer for structs (i8/i16/i32/i64/f64/Str/nested; f64 v6.3.40)"
category = "cyrius"
---
`#derive(Serialize)` before a struct auto-generates
`Name_to_json(ptr, sb)` that writes JSON into a str_builder.

REQUIRED INCLUDES (cyrius doesn't auto-pull these):
    include "lib/syscalls.cyr"
    include "lib/string.cyr"
    include "lib/str.cyr"
    include "lib/alloc.cyr"
    include "lib/fmt.cyr"
    # serializer-only ↑    ↓ deserializer (round-trip) needs these too:
    include "lib/vec.cyr"
    include "lib/hashmap.cyr"
    include "lib/bayan.cyr"   # 6.1.x: json/toml/base64 bundled in bayan

USAGE:
    #derive(Serialize)
    struct Agent { id: i64; name: Str; }

    var a = Agent { 42, str_from("alice") };
    var sb = str_builder_new();
    Agent_to_json(&a, sb);
    var s = str_builder_build(sb);
    # s is a `Str` (16-byte heap header {ptr, len}).
    # To print:
    str_println(s);
    # NOT `println(s)` — `println` expects a cstring and will
    # interpret the Str header bytes as garbage (low addr +
    # length). Use `str_println(s)` for the Str-typed case;
    # it's the canonical helper (lib/str.cyr line 72,
    # `str_print` + `\n`). Surfaced by agnosys 1.1.12 re-file
    # 2026-05-07 where the consumer used `println(out)` and
    # reported "garbage output"; root cause was API misuse,
    # not codegen.

SEMANTICS:
    Untyped scalar fields (no annotation) → bare JSON numbers: 42
    `: i8` / `: i16` / `: i32` / `: i64`  → bare JSON numbers via
                                            str_builder_add_int +
                                            width-correct loadN
                                            (v5.9.30 i64; v5.9.36
                                            i8/i16/i32 added)
    `: f64` fields                        → bare JSON numbers (v6.3.40;
                                            codec rewired to bayan Grisu2
                                            at v6.4.69):
                                            `_to_json` renders via
                                            bayan_f64_to_json (shortest
                                            round-trip-correct output);
                                            `_from_json`/`_from_json_str`
                                            parse via bayan_f64_from_json
                                            (correctly-rounded, DoS-safe).
                                            An f64-deriving struct MUST have
                                            `lib/bayan.cyr` in scope.
                                            Fractional values round-trip
                                            bit-exactly — e.g. 1/3 no longer
                                            truncates to 6 decimals.
    `: Str` fields                        → quoted JSON strings: "alice"
    Nested struct fields (typed by another
    `#derive(Serialize)` struct name)     → recursive _to_json call

    Detection: preprocessor checks the type cstring's first
    bytes. `Str\0` → string path. `i8\0` / `i16\0` / `i32\0`
    / `i64\0` → primitive integer path (v5.9.36; narrow widths
    paired with parser-side struct-literal width-correct
    initializer fix in the same slot). `f64\0` → the float path
    (v6.3.40 — before that an f64 field emitted an undefined
    `f64_to_json` / `f64_from_json`, a hard "reachable undefined
    function" build error, same class as the i64 gap fixed v5.9.35).

GLOBAL-SCOPE Str-FIELD FIX (v6.3.44):
    A `var g = T { .., <str> };` at GLOBAL scope, where T has a Str
    field, used to fail to PARSE (`unexpected '}'`) — the in-fn init
    was fine. Root cause: EMIT_GVAR_INITS's pass-2 replay of a global
    struct literal is a PARALLEL COPY of PARSE_STRUCT_INIT's positional
    loop that never got the v5.10.7 single-8-byte-slot Str handling, so
    a Str field fell into the nested-struct FLATTEN branch and PCMPE'd a
    second (nonexistent) `len` expression, hitting `}`. Fixed by
    mirroring the IS_STR_FIELD branch into EMIT_GVAR_INITS. (The bug is
    `#derive`-INDEPENDENT — a plain global struct literal with a Str
    field reproduced it.)

DESERIALIZER COMPANION:
    #derive(Serialize) ALSO generates `_from_json(pairs)` (the
    `lib/json.cyr` json_get-based form) and `_from_json_str(json)`
    (single-pass byte scanner). Round-trip works for `Str` and
    `i64` fields:

        var p = Agent_from_json_str(str_data(s));
        var id = load64(p + 0);   # 42
        # p + 8 holds a Str header for "alice"

    Pre-v5.9.35 the deserializer emitted `<typename>_from_json(...)`
    for every typed-non-Str field — including primitives — so
    `i64` fields hit `i64_from_json` (undefined). v5.9.35
    (agnosys 1.1.12 re-file close) added i64 detection: typed
    i64 fields take the `str_to_int(v)` + `store64(ptr+offset, ...)`
    path mirroring the serializer side.

NARROW INTEGER WIDTHS (i8, i16, i32):
    Supported as of v5.9.36. Two compounding bugs were paired
    in that slot:
    (1) PP_DERIVE codegen — `prim_load` byte-gate detection
    extended with per-width outer-byte checks ('8'/'1'/'3'/'6')
    in `_to_json`, `_from_json`, `_from_json_str` AND
    `PP_PARSE_STRUCT_DEF`'s offset-table cumul.
    (2) Parser-side struct-literal initializer made width-
    correct via new `EMIT_STRUCT_FIELD_W(S, vcnt, boff, width)`
    (picks ESTORE8/16/32/STOC by width); `PARSE_STRUCT_INIT`
    positional + named + `EMIT_GVAR_INITS` all advance `boff`
    by `FIELDSZ(ft)` per field. `STRUCTSZ` semantics
    unchanged — `sizeof(Packet) == 15` (1+2+4+8 packed) still
    asserts. The fix is at the literal-write side, not the
    size-reporting side.

PLATFORM CAVEAT (Mach-O / macOS arm64) — RESOLVED v5.9.38/39:
    Pre-v5.9.38 the auto-generated `_to_json` body SIGSEGV'd
    at runtime on real macOS arm64 (ecb host). Two compounding
    bugs surfaced via lldb crash log:
    (1) **v5.9.38 Bug A** — `lib/fnptr.cyr` had no
        `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS` branches in `fncall0..8`;
        on Mach-O builds `fncallN` returned 0 → `alloc_via`
        returned NULL → `str_builder_new` returned NULL →
        SIGSEGV at `load64(NULL+8)` inside `_sb_grow_a`. Fix:
        macOS branches with x86_64 + aarch64 asm copies of
        the Linux block content (SysV-identical).
    (2) **v5.9.39 Bug B** — `src/backend/aarch64/fixup.cyr`
        ftype==3 emitted a static MOVZ/MOVK chain encoding
        the unslid file VA. Apple Silicon enforces MH_PIE;
        dyld slides the image at load time → `blr xN` jumped
        to (file_VA, no slide), landing in `__data` / `__got`
        instead of `__text`. Fix: dispatch to ADRP+ADD
        relative addressing on `_TARGET_MACHO == 2`,
        mirroring how ftype==1 (string addrs) and ftype==0
        (var addrs) already worked.

    Both fixes verified via `_macho_derive_serialize_gate`
    in programs/check.cyr — runs `point_to_json(&p, sb)` on
    ecb and asserts the byte-count exit code. Cross-host
    parity with Linux aarch64 (pi) confirmed at v5.9.39 ship.
    The agnosys 1.1.12 verbatim repro hash
    (`6425355b6147d5a674078794310ae2c1`) now executes through
    the build path on Mach-O — the residual SIGSEGV at the
    `println(strlen(out))` shape is a TYPE-SYSTEM gap pinned
    for v5.10.x, not a Mach-O codegen bug.

ALSO AVAILABLE — #derive(accessors):
    #derive(accessors) struct Point { x; y; }
    Generates: Point_x(p), Point_set_x(p, v), Point_y(p),
    Point_set_y(p, v)

    A derived struct NAME declared twice (6.6.11): the parser keeps
    the FIRST layout, so a second `#derive`d definition whose field
    names, order or size differ is refused —
        #derive: struct 'A' is defined again with different field
        names, order or size -- the FIRST definition is the layout
        used, so these accessors would read and write the wrong bytes
    Before 6.6.11 it built with warnings only: a larger second
    definition's setters wrote past the first one's sizeof, and
    `{ x; y; }` redefined as `{ y; x; }` had every setter write the
    OTHER field. The same names at the same offsets still build —
    one struct vendored by two libraries, or field TYPES that differ
    at the same width (`f64` in one copy, `i64` in the other).

PP_DERIVE handles both — same preprocessor codegen pattern.

[[entries]]
name = "secret_var_compound_ops"
description = "Cyrius syntax extras — struct init, &local arch dispatch, #regalloc, secret var (an array zeroised on every return path — and what it does NOT cover: callee frames, vector registers, the heap, timing; the sigil 3.13.8 / CVE-68 and CVE-70 lessons, 6.6.15)"
category = "cyrius"
---
A grab-bag of syntactic additions consolidated for agent reference.

STRUCT INITIALIZER SYNTAX:
    var p = Point { x: 10, y: 20 };
Emits struct-shaped stores in declaration order. Pairs with
#derive(accessors) for accessor-fn generation.

NATIVE MULTI-RETURN:
    fn divmod(a, b) { return a / b, a % b; }
    var q, r = divmod(10, 3);
Two values come back via rax (ret1) + rdx (ret2). No tuple
wrapper; two `return` expressions, comma-separated.

&LOCAL ARCH DISPATCH:
`&local_var` — takes the address of a local variable.
Compiler dispatches per-arch in the emit: x86 uses
`lea rax, [rbp - N]`, aarch64 uses `sub x0, x29, #N`.

#regalloc DIRECTIVE:
    #regalloc 1
    fn hot_loop() { ... }
Opt-in per-fn flag. Routes the innermost loop's accumulator
local to rbx via a post-emit peephole with width-aware safety
abort (reverts to stack if it spots a width violation).
~12% speedup on nested accumulators.

SECRET VAR:
    secret var key[32];
    load_key(&key);
    if (ct_eq_bytes(&key, candidate, 32) == 1) { ... }
Declares a fn-local ARRAY that is ZEROISED on every return path —
the wipe is a synthetic `defer` the epilogue walker runs, so every
rule of `defer_statement` applies (owned by its closure, never
tail-called or inlined, refused at top level and, since 6.6.15, in a
`#naked` fn). Scalar form (`secret var x = 0;`) is rejected —
`secret var name[N]` only. The keyword does NOT make code
constant-time: comparisons go through `lib/ct.cyr` (below). Used
throughout sigil and by agnosys certpin.

WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER — the sigil 3.13.8 and native-TLS lessons
folded at cyrius 6.6.15. `secret var` wipes ITS array; key material
also lives in:
  - a CALLEE's dead frame. The HMAC_DRBG context sigil's ECDSA signer
    used sat ~43 KB down holding the nonce k (HMAC never wiped its
    context, and its final state IS the MAC); k plus one signature is
    the private key (CVE-68 part B). Wipe a context in the code that
    owns it; to scrub a deep call tree, burn the stack from a PLAIN
    wrapper AFTER the secret callee has returned, so the burn also
    covers that callee's epilogue (sigil burns 128 / 160 KB).
  - vector registers. SHA-NI / AES-NI leave state in xmm0..; an asm
    block computing on secrets clears the registers it used (a later
    `secret var` epilogue used to spill them to dead stack — CVE-69).
  - the heap. Native TLS kept every connection's ephemeral key and
    shared secret in ctx buffers until 6.6.15 (CVE-70): zero each at
    its last use AND on every exit of the driver.
  - TIMING. sigil's ECDSA signing (3.5.9 through 3.13.7) ran its
    ladder over field ops that branched on their operands — final
    subtracts, `if` carry fix-ups, early-exit compares, aarch64's
    software high-half multiply — a fixed-vs-random Welch t-test
    gave |t| = 6.3 at 400 samples, the Minerva / TPM-Fail lattice
    class (CVE-68). Constant time is a property of EVERY primitive
    under the secret, not of the top-level algorithm: two earlier
    fixes had removed only the coarse leaks and audits had filed the
    arithmetic as INFO. sigil 3.13.8's engine (src/ec_ct.cyr:
    branch-free Montgomery arithmetic, complete formulas, fixed
    window with a full-table masked lookup) was verified two ways:
    |t| = 0.7 at 1200, and EVERY conditional branch of the compiled
    engine, x86_64 and aarch64, accounted for by disassembly as a
    loop bound, public exponent bit, public size or public verdict.
    Not claimed: operand-value timing via power / frequency
    (Hertzbleed class).
The compiler-side holes in the wipe itself: field_notes/compiler/
gotchas.cyml `secret_var_zeroisation_skipped_four_ways_...`.

Stdlib-side helpers live in `lib/ct.cyr`:
  - `ct_select(cond, a, b)`             — branchless select (v3.x)
  - `ct_eq_bytes(a, b, n)`              — branchless byte-array
                                          equality, equal-length
                                          (v5.9.18; canonical XOR-
                                          accumulate)
  - `ct_eq_bytes_lens(a, alen, b, blen)` — dual-length variant;
                                           returns 0 on length
                                           mismatch then runs the
                                           CT compare on the equal-
                                           length common prefix
                                           (v5.9.20; additive
                                           stdlib helper).
The single-length form is the workhorse — use it for fixed-
length compares where lengths are equal-by-construction (MAC
tags, signature components, SHA-256 digests). The dual-length
variant exists for cstring or variable-length compares where
the call site genuinely needs a length-mismatch fast path.

**Sigil's `ct_eq` family**: sigil ships its own
`fn ct_eq(a, a_len, b, b_len)` and `fn ct_eq_32(a, b)` in
`sigil/src/ct.cyr` (vendored into cyrius `lib/sigil.cyr`).
Those are sigil-public symbols downstream consumers (kavach,
hoosh, libro, majra, plus 11+ vendored-copy holders) call
directly. They remain in sigil; the cyrius-side
`ct_eq_bytes_lens` is an additive helper, NOT a replacement.
A consolidation slot would require coordinated downstream
migration + a sigil major-version bump, since removing
`fn ct_eq` from sigil's bundle is a breaking public-surface
change across the ecosystem.

**agnosys 1.1.2+ certpin** uses `ct_eq_bytes` (the
single-length form). The cstring variant (`certpin_ct_streq`)
remains hand-rolled in agnosys pending a separate
cstring-helper slot. Compiler builtins in this neighborhood:
`mulh64(a, b)` for constant-time multiplication.

EXPRESSION-POSITION COMPARISONS:
    var flag = (x == y);
Comparisons return 0/1 and can live anywhere an integer
expression does. PARSER LIMITATION: comparisons inside function
CALL ARGUMENTS are rejected (parser ambiguity with generic/
templated forms). Work around by assigning first:
    var eq = (x == y); foo(eq);

OVERFLOW-EXPLICIT OPERATORS (lib/overflow.cyr):
    +%  -%  *%      # wrapping (alias for bare + - *; 2's complement wrap)
    +|  -|  *|      # saturating (clamp to i64::MIN / i64::MAX)
    +?  -?  *?      # checked (panic on overflow with exit code 57)

Wrapping ops are bytes-identical to the bare operator — they
document intent, no runtime cost. Saturating and checked
variants compile to calls into lib/overflow.cyr helpers.

ATTRIBUTES — #must_use, @unsafe, #deprecated:
    #must_use
    fn checked_op(x): i64 { return x * 2; }

    @unsafe { store64(some_raw_ptr, 0); }

    #deprecated("use sha256_init() — sha1 is collision-broken")
    fn sha1_init() { ... }

#must_use warns at expression-statement discard. @unsafe is
advisory only. #deprecated("why, and what to use instead")
warns ONCE per call, on that call's line, however the fn is
reached: a plain call, `return f(x);`, a call written BEFORE
the definition, `&f`, `o.m()` for a top-level `fn T_m`, an
overloaded operator, a struct-valued receive (`var q: P3 =
f()`), a generic instance (`g<i32>(..)`), or a call inside an
`#inline` / generic body (once, not once per expansion) — all
since 6.6.16; before, only three call paths warned. The
message is required: a bare `#deprecated` is refused by name.
It changes no code. Inside an `impl` body a `#deprecated` is
refused (deprecate the method with a top-level `fn T_m`).

[[entries]]
name = "slice_type_v58x"
description = "slice<T> / [T] type — bounds-aware indexing s[i], dot-syntax field access s.ptr / s.len"
category = "cyrius"
---
Slices are a first-class type with the syntactic ergonomics of
any other primitive — not just a 16-byte fat-pointer convention.

== TYPE ANNOTATIONS ==

Two equivalent forms accepted everywhere a type annotation goes
(var, fn param, field):

    var s: [u8] = 0;        # bracket form
    var s: slice<u8> = 0;   # ident form

Element type may be any scalar (u8/i8/u16/i16/u32/i32/u64/i64/
u128) or pointer (*T). Element width is captured at PARSE_VAR
time and drives sized loads in indexing.

== ALLOCATION + LAYOUT ==

A slice fn-local reserves TWO 8-byte slots:
  - canonical slot (lower address) — holds the .ptr field
  - high-half slot (higher address) — holds the .len field

Layout puts the canonical slot at the LOWER stack address so
`&slice + 8` correctly addresses the .len field. `var s: [T] = 0;`
zero-inits BOTH halves via address-based memory stores.

== HEAP-FALLOC INTEROP ==

A slice value is byte-identical to:
  - Str (lib/str.cyr) — heap-allocated 16 bytes, data@0, len@8
  - vec (lib/vec.cyr) first 16 bytes — same shape

Consequence: a Str pointer or vec value can be passed directly to
slice_ptr / slice_len / slice_eq / slice_is_empty / slice_is_null
without conversion. The only difference is lifetime — Str/vec
live in heap; fn-local slices are frame-scoped.

== SUBSCRIPT ==

    var d[5];  store8(&d, 65);  store8(&d+1, 66);  ...
    var s: [u8] = 0;
    slice_set(&s, &d, 5);
    var b = s[0];      # = 65 — bounds-checked u8 load
    var c = s[2];      # = 67
    # OOB / negative idx → stderr "slice bounds violation\n", exit 134

`s[i]` lowers at parse time to `_slice_idx_get_W(&s, i)` where
W is the element byte-width (1/2/4/8/16). Width-16 returns a
POINTER to the u128 slot (cyrius's ABI can't return u128 by value).

For perf hot paths inside already-bounds-checked loops, the
unchecked variants `slice_unchecked_get_W(&s, i)` skip the
bounds check.

== DOT-SYNTAX FIELD ACCESS ==

    var s: [u8] = 0;
    slice_set(&s, &d, 5);
    var p = s.ptr;            # = &d (lower-half slot)
    var n = s.len;            # = 5  (higher-half slot via &s + 8)
    s.len = 3;                # truncate the view
    s.ptr = &d + 1;           # rewrite view start

Only `.ptr` and `.len` are accepted; other field names hard-error
with "slice has only .ptr and .len fields".

== POINTER-TO-STRUCT DOT SYNTAX ==

    var s: Str = str_from("hello");
    var n = s.len;           # = 5  (pointer-deref first)
    var p = s.data;          # = pointer to "hello" bytes

PARSE_FIELD_LOAD/STORE auto-detects pointer-vs-inline by checking
the slot above the named slot for a sentinel name `-1`:
  - sentinel present → inline mode (lea, slot IS the struct)
  - sentinel absent → pointer mode (mov, slot HOLDS a pointer)

`fn foo(s: Str) { return s.len; }` also works.

== SLICE-TYPED WRAPPER HELPERS ==

    fn sys_read_slice(fd, &s)     — sys_read into s.data, up to s.len bytes
    fn slice_copy_bytes(&dst, &src) — memcpy with min(dst.len, src.len) cap
    fn slice_eq_bytes(&a, &b)     — content equality; length-mismatch is unequal

Additive — the canonical (ptr, len) primitives in lib/string.cyr
/ lib/syscalls.cyr remain the building blocks.

[[entries]]
name = "relaxed_fn_ordering"
description = "fn definitions can appear after top-level statements"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    var x = 42;
    print_i64(x);      # top-level statement
    fn helper(a) {     # fn defined AFTER top-level code
        return a * 2;
    }

SEMANTICS:
    fn definitions can be freely interleaved with top-level code.
    Forward calls already worked (two-pass compilation), so this
    only affected fn definitions appearing physically after
    executed statements.

IMPLEMENTATION:
    PARSE_PROG handles `fn` tokens by:
    1. Emitting a jmp instruction to jump over the function body
    2. Compiling the function body normally (same as before)
    3. Patching the jmp to land after the function body

    Same jmp-over pattern used for if/else blocks.

[[entries]]
name = "tagged_unions_v58x"
description = "enum sum types — `enum Result<T, E> { Ok(T), Err(E) }` with multi-arg constructors + nullary tagged variants. DEFAULT form is a heap box; `enum Name: stack` (v6.5.55) is the VALUE FORM returning (tag, payload) in a register pair with ZERO allocation — what the stdlib uses since v6.6.0"
category = "cyrius"
---
Tagged unions / sum types — first-class language feature shipped
across v5.8.21–v5.8.23 + v5.8.26. Replaces the hand-rolled
lib/tagged.cyr `tagged_new(tag, value)` pattern with
compiler-generated constructor fns.

== SYNTAX ==

The pinned canonical shape from the v5.8.21 sum-type slot:

    enum Result<T, E> {
        Ok(T),
        Err(E)
    }

All four pieces of this shape are independently optional:

    enum Maybe { Nothing; Just(v); }            # no generics, ;-sep
    enum Pair<T> { P(a, b); }                   # 1 type-param, multi-arg
    enum Tri<T, U, V> {
        Triple(a, b, c),                        # multi-arg
        Pair(x, y),                             # different arity OK
        Single(s),                              # single-arg
        Bare                                    # auto-incremented int
    }

Generic params (`<T, E>`) are syntactically accepted but not yet
bound semantically (mono-only, erasure semantics — full binding
arrives when monomorphization lands post-v5.8.x). Variant
separators are `;` OR `,` — mixed in same decl tolerated.

== CONSTRUCTOR CODEGEN ==

    var ok = Ok(42);
    # → alloc(8 + 8*1 = 16); store64(ptr, 0); store64(ptr+8, 42); return ptr

    var t = Triple(11, 22, 33);
    # → alloc(8 + 8*3 = 32); store64(ptr, 0); store64(ptr+8, 11);
    #   store64(ptr+16, 22); store64(ptr+24, 33); return ptr

Layout: tag at +0, payload[i] at +8 + 8*i. Frame size scales with
arity: `(arity + 1) * 16` bytes. Arity-1 produces byte-identical
output to the pre-v5.8.21 single-arg path (preserves the v5.5.2
single-arg corpus exactly).

== `: stack` — THE VALUE FORM (v6.5.55; the stdlib flipped at v6.6.0) ==

⚠ **Everything above describes the DEFAULT (boxed) form, and it is still correct
for it.** `: stack` is opt-in, and it is what `lib/result.cyr` and `lib/tagged.cyr`
now use:

    enum Result<T, E>: stack { Ok(v); Err(e); }
    enum Option: stack      { None(); Some(v); }

A **single-payload** variant of a `: stack` enum returns `(tag, payload)` in the
multi-return REGISTER PAIR (rax, rdx) — **no allocation at all**, so the boxed
codegen above is not emitted. Bind both halves:

    var t, v = Ok(42);      # t = 0 (the tag), v = 42

The measurement that forced the stdlib flip: 100 × `sock_send` grew the global
bump allocator by exactly 1600 B, unbounded — and that allocator's only reclaim
invalidates every pointer it ever returned, so a long-running server could never
call it. Now 0 B.

CONSEQUENCES that are easy to get wrong:
  * ⛔ rdx does NOT reach a parameter, so **no helper can take a pair in one
    argument.** This is why `payload()` was DELETED rather than re-implemented,
    and why `unwrap`/`result_unwrap`/`unwrap_or`/`err_code_of`/`*_print` all grew
    the tag as an argument. Tag-only predicates (`is_ok`, `is_none`, …) keep
    arity 1 because argument 1 receives rax, which IS the tag.
  * A variant with **two or more** payload fields cannot be a register pair, so it
    stays BOXED even under `: stack` — the layout above still applies to it.
  * ⛔ `return res;` on an Err path returns the payload ALONE — `Err(77)` then reads
    as tag=77 / is_err=0, an error that reads as SUCCESS. Return `(t, v)`. Mixed
    returns warn since v6.6.0.
  * Single-value consumption of a pair (`var r = f();`), `store64(&s, f())` and
    assignment of one are **hard errors** since v6.5.67 — they used to compile and
    silently drop the payload.

== EMPTY-PAREN NULLARY TAGGED VARIANTS (v5.8.23) ==

    enum Option { None(); Some(v); }
    var n = None();
    # → alloc(8); store64(ptr, 0); return ptr — TAG ONLY, no payload

Empty parens `Foo()` produces a true arity-0 nullary tagged
variant (8-byte alloc, tag at +0, no payload, no params consumed).
Pre-v5.8.23 it collapsed to arity-1 (16-byte alloc with garbage
payload) — a no-real-consumer landmine, fixed at v5.8.23.

== BARE-NAME VS PAREN'D ==

    enum Color { RED; GREEN; BLUE; }    # all bare
    var c = RED;                         # = 0 (auto-incremented int)

    enum Option { None(); Some(v); }    # all paren'd
    var n = None();                     # = 16-byte heap pointer, tag 0

In MIXED enums, bare variants stay int-typed and paren'd variants
heap-allocate — convention is paren-consistent. Bare-name
auto-tagging in mixed-enum decls (the v5.8.21 pin's
`enum Option { None; Some(v); }` prescriptive shape) was deferred;
the paren-consistent form works cleanly without it.

== STDLIB MIGRATION (v5.8.23, v5.8.26) ==

`lib/tagged.cyr` shipped 3 compiler-generated sum types
replacing 6 hand-rolled constructor fns:

    enum Option { None(); Some(v); }
    enum Result { Ok(v); Err(e); }
    enum Either { Left(v); Right(v); }

Helpers (`is_none`, `is_some`, `unwrap`, `unwrap_or`,
`result_unwrap`, `err_code_of`, `is_left`, `is_right`) reference
the lowercase variant names which fold via `enum_const_val` to
identical tag values. The primitives today: `is_tag(t, expected)`,
`option_print(t, v)` / `result_print(t, v)`. Re-aritied at v6.6.0
(value form). `payload` was deleted at 6.6.0 and `tag` at 6.6.2.
`tagged_new` is in lib/boxed.cyr.

`lib/hashmap.cyr` got a 6-site migration at v5.8.26:
`enum KeyType { KeyTypeCstr; KeyTypeStr; KeyTypeU64; }` replaces
raw 0/1/2 stored at map header offset 24.

== INTERNAL BOOKKEEPING ==

PARSE_ENUM_DEF in pass 1 writes 4 heap regions (added v5.8.22):
  - var_enum_id[8192] @ 0x204000 — per-variant parent eid
  - enum_count[8] @ 0x214000 — total enum decls (cap 1024)
  - enum_variant_count[1024] @ 0x214008 — variants per enum
  - enum_name[1024] @ 0x216008 — name offset for diagnostics

Enum cap: 1024 (bumped from 256 at v5.8.24 — stdlib alone has
299 enum decls, downstream multi-dep programs were 1-2 releases
from tripping). 8192 var-table cap is the variant-side cap;
no realistic consumer is anywhere near it.

[[entries]]
name = "exhaustive_match_v58x"
description = "exhaustive `match` coverage check — warns on missing variants + duplicate arms; opt out via `_ =>`"
category = "cyrius"
---
PARSE_MATCH coverage check shipped across v5.8.22 (coverage
diagnostic) + v5.8.25 (arm-tag dedup). **Warning**, not error,
per v5.8.x's incremental-adoption policy (same shape as
#must_use / #deprecated / #pure→#io). `cyrius lint --strict`
escalation reserved for a future slot.

== EXHAUSTIVE MATCH ==

    enum Status { PENDING; ACTIVE; DONE; }

    match s {
        PENDING => { ... }
        ACTIVE  => { ... }
        DONE    => { ... }
    }
    # → no warning (all 3 variants covered)

== NON-EXHAUSTIVE WITHOUT `_ =>` ==

    match s {
        PENDING => { ... }
        ACTIVE  => { ... }
    }
    # → warning:<file>:<line>: non-exhaustive match over enum
    #   'Status' — covers 2 of 3 variants; add `_ =>` to opt out

== `_ =>` OPT-OUT ==

    match s {
        PENDING => { ... }
        _       => { ... }
    }
    # → no warning (default arm present)

== MIXED-ENUM ARMS ==

    enum A { A1; A2; }
    enum B { B1; B2; }

    match A1 {
        A1 => { ... }
        B2 => { ... }   # arm references DIFFERENT enum
        _  => { ... }
    }
    # → warning:<file>:<line>: match arms span multiple enums
    #   (exhaustive-coverage check skipped)

== DUPLICATE ARM (v5.8.25) ==

    match s {
        PENDING => { ... }
        PENDING => { ... }
    }
    # → warning:<file>:<line>: duplicate match arm 'PENDING'
    # → warning:<file>:<line>: non-exhaustive match over enum
    #   'Status' — covers 1 of 3 variants; ...

Pre-v5.8.25, duplicate arms counted as 2-of-3 covered (false-
clean — adding a third arm made it 3-of-3 even though PENDING was
duplicated and the others were missing). v5.8.25's `seen_vcnt[256]`
stack-allocated dedup array fixes the math.

== CODEGEN UNCHANGED ==

The coverage check is metadata-only. Runtime cmp/jcc-skip cascade
still picks the FIRST matching arm — duplicate arms are dead code
at runtime (first wins). Tested in `tests/tcyr/match_dedup.tcyr`.

== WHAT FIRES THE CHECK ==

The check fires only when at least one arm references a variant
of an enum (PARSE_MATCH peeks the arm's first ident pre-PCMPE,
looks up `var_enum_id[arm_vi]`). Literal-int matches
(`match n { 0 => ..., 1 => ... }`) skip the check entirely —
treated as non-enum match. Non-variant idents (regular const
vars, complex expressions) also skip.

== INTEGRATION WITH PATTERN-PAYLOAD MATCHING ==

This slot ships the COVERAGE check only. Pattern destructuring
(`match opt { Some(v) => use(v) }` extracting the payload into
`v`) is NOT shipped — it would need:
  1. Auto-deref of the matchee from heap pointer to tag
  2. Variable binding from pattern slot offsets
That's a future slot. Today's idiom for tagged values:

    match load64(opt) {
        None => { ... }
        Some => { var v = load64(opt + 8); ... }
    }

Or use the `lib/tagged.cyr` helper API (`is_some`, `unwrap_or`,
etc.) which encapsulates the load64 pattern.

[[entries]]
name = "char_literals_v59x"
description = "Char literals — `'X'` syntax in lex.cyr emits NUMBER token (v5.9.37)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["lexer", "syntax", "v5.9.37", "char-literal"]
---
v5.9.37 added char-literal lexing. `'X'` produces a NUMBER
token (token type 1) carrying the byte value of the character
between the single quotes.

USAGE:
    str_builder_putc(sb, '[');           # 91
    str_builder_putc(sb, '\\n');          # 10
    if (c == 'a') { ... }                # c == 97
    var space = ' ';                      # 32

ESCAPES SUPPORTED (mirror string-literal escapes):
    \\n  → 10  (LF)
    \\t  → 9   (TAB)
    \\r  → 13  (CR)
    \\\\  → 92  (backslash itself)
    \\'  → 39  (single quote)
    \\0  → 0   (NUL)

PARSING SHAPE:
    `'` opens; one byte (or 2-char escape) follows; `'` closes.
    No multi-byte / unicode in char literals — single ASCII byte.
    Mismatched closing quote → lexer error at the line.

WHY IT LANDED:
    agnosys 1.1.12 verbatim repro at v5.9.37 ship used
    `str_builder_putc(sb, '[')` and `str_builder_putc(sb, ']')`.
    Pre-v5.9.37 cyrius had no char-literal token; consumers
    encoded byte values as `91` etc. with a comment naming
    the char. Adding `'X'` is consumer-ergonomic + standard
    lexer feature.

PAIRED ADDITION (lib/str.cyr):
    `str_builder_putc(sb, ch)` — append single byte to a
    str_builder. Mirrors the existing `str_builder_add`
    (Str), `str_builder_add_cstr` (cstring), `str_builder_add_int`
    helpers; pairs with the new char literal for byte-at-a-
    time append.

[[entries]]
name = "auto_call_main_v59x"
description = "Auto-call `main()` if defined — compiler emits ECALLTO at exit epilogue (v5.9.37)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "behavior", "v5.9.37", "auto-call-main"]
---
v5.9.37 added auto-call-main behavior. If a source defines
`fn main() { ... }` but doesn't explicitly call it via
`var ec = main(); syscall(60, ec);` (the long-standing
manual wiring), the compiler now walks the fn table for
the byte-match `"main\\0"` and emits `ECALLTO(_main_off)`
at the exit epilogue, AFTER the GLVAR-load.

ORDER MATTERS:
    Exit epilogue:
      1. `if (lv >= 0) { EVLOAD(S, lv); }`   ; load last expr
      2. **auto-call-main** (if `fn main` defined)            ← v5.9.37
      3. `EEXIT(S);`                                          ; ret syscall

    Auto-call AFTER GLVAR-load preserves the manual-wiring
    case: a source with explicit `var ec = main(); syscall(60, ec);`
    short-circuits at step 2 because syscall(60, ec) terminates
    the process before EEXIT runs. Auto-call only fires when
    the user didn't manually wire it.

DCE EXEMPTION:
    Cyrius's dead-code-elimination bitmap stubs unreferenced
    fns to `xor eax, eax; ret`. With auto-call-main, `main`
    is now reachable from the exit epilogue — but the DCE
    bitmap doesn't know that (the ECALLTO is emitted AFTER
    DCE marks the fn). Workaround: a name-byte-match
    exemption (`m`/`a`/`i`/`n`/`\\0` at start of name) sets
    `dce_is_main = 1` and bypasses the bitmap check.

CROSS-ARCH:
    Same shape in `src/main.cyr` and `src/main_aarch64.cyr`.
    aarch64 doesn't have a DCE pass so no exemption needed
    on that side.

WHY IT LANDED:
    agnosys 1.1.12 verbatim repro had `fn main() { ... }`
    without the manual `var ec = main(); syscall(60, ec);`
    wiring. Pre-v5.9.37 the compiler ran gvar inits + the
    parse-prog walk and exited with `rax=junk` — `main` was
    never invoked. Auto-call closes the consumer-friendliness
    gap. Long-standing `var ec = main(); syscall(60, ec);`
    pattern still works — it short-circuits the auto-call.

[[entries]]
name = "cx_phase2c_v59x"
description = "cyrius-x bytecode (cx) backend Phase 2c parity — sub-byte fields, struct-byval return, 7+-arg fns (v5.9.40)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cx-backend", "bytecode", "v5.9.40", "stdlib", "cxvm"]
---
v5.9.40 closed the three Phase 2c sites that v5.9.26 +
v5.9.27 narrowed but didn't fix in the cyrius-x (cx)
bytecode backend. End-to-end: a cyrius source exercising
struct-by-value return, sub-8-byte struct field loads, and
7+-arg fn calls now round-trips cleanly through cycc_cx →
.cyx bytecode → cxvm interpreter.

THREE COORDINATED CHANGES:

(1) Sub-byte struct field load — new per-backend
    `EFIELD_LOAD_W(S, width)` helper. cx version emits new
    cxvm `load16` (0x44) / `load32` (0x45) opcodes; aarch64
    version emits `ldrb` / `ldrh` / `ldr w0`; x86 emits
    `movzx rax, byte [rcx]` etc. parse_decl.cyr's
    `PARSE_FIELD_LOAD` switched from inline emit to the
    helper.

(2) Struct return by value — new per-backend
    `ESTRUCT_BYVAL_COPY(S, src_disp, stash_disp, aligned)`
    helper. cx emits an explicit byte-loop (load8 / store8 +
    add / sub + jnz) since cxvm has no `rep movsb` / post-
    incr semantics. aarch64 emits LDRB/STRB-with-post-incr.
    x86 emits `rep movsb` block.

(3) ESTORESTACKPARM — real impl in cx (was TODO stub).
    cxvm has 32 registers so 7+-arg fn calls just pop into
    r3..r9+ via the existing `ECALLPOPS` loop;
    `ESTORESTACKPARM` mirrors `ESTOREPARM`'s r-to-frame
    body shape using the call-site `disp` directly.

CXVM OPCODE EXTENSIONS:
    0x44 — load16 ra, [rb]   (zero-extend halfword)
    0x45 — load32 ra, [rb]   (zero-extend word)
    0x46 — store16 [ra], rb  (low halfword)
    0x47 — store32 [ra], rb  (low word)

    Composed from cyrius's native load8 / load64 + bit-shift
    primitives (the language has no native sub-8/sub-64
    multi-byte memory ops).

CX-SIDE ABI COMPLETION (required to make the chain compile):
    Pre-v5.9.40 these were stubs:
    - `EFLADDR(S, lli)` — was stub returning 0
    - `EFLADDR_X1(S, lli)` — didn't exist
    - `ESTOREREGPARM(S, pidx, disp)` — didn't exist
    Without them, cycc_cx SIGILL'd at compile-time on any
    sub-byte struct-field store or `fn f(): S` declaration.
    All three got real impls this slot mirroring the existing
    `ESTOREPARM` r-to-frame body shape.

CONSUMER NOTE:
    cx is x86-shaped (mostly) but has 32 registers vs SysV's
    8 GP-arg / 6 reg-arg convention. ECALLPOPS pops into
    r3..r9+ regardless of arg count — no separate
    "stack-arg" path is actually needed; cxvm's ESTOREPARM
    handles arbitrary arity via `r(3+pidx)`.

[[entries]]
name = "regression_module_v59x"
description = "lib/regression.cyr — testing-stdlib carved from programs/check.cyr at v5.9.42"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["stdlib", "testing", "v5.9.42", "regression"]
---
v5.9.42 carved out 22 reusable testing primitives from
`programs/check.cyr` into a new stdlib module
`lib/regression.cyr`. Downstream test runners (yantra,
agnosys/mabda smoke suites, future runners) can reach for
the same shapes via `include "lib/regression.cyr"` instead
of re-rolling fork/exec/SSH/timeout boilerplate.

PUBLIC SURFACE — 22 verbs across 5 groups:

  Display (4):
    regression_print(s)
    regression_section(name)
    regression_check(name, exit_code)
    REGRESSION_ANSI_RED / _GREEN / _RESET (3 const vars)

  Buffer scan (3):
    regression_buf_eq(p, s, n)
    regression_count_substr(buf, n, needle)
    regression_file_contains_substr(path, substr)

  Process (8):
    regression_pipe_to_bin / _capture
    regression_run_with_timeout (poll-based wall-clock)
    regression_exec_capture / _exec_run
    regression_exec_with_arg_capture / _both
    regression_exec_in_dir3 / _env

  Network (1):
    regression_network_probe(addr_ipv4, port, timeout_ms)
        non-blocking connect + poll(POLLOUT) + SO_ERROR
        readback. Replaces bash `/dev/tcp` trick.

  SSH cluster (6):
    regression_ssh_target / _ssh_skip_check / _scp_to /
    _ssh_remote_exit / _ssh_remote_exec_capture /
    _codesign_remote

CONVENTIONS:
- Every fork+exec verb takes explicit `envp` arg (no
  implicit env). Caller passes their own; pass `0`-
  terminated 1-elem array for empty.
- Exit-code convention: 0..127 = normal exit; 128+sig
  on signal; -1 on fork/exec/syscall failure; -2 for
  timeout (`_run_with_timeout` only).
- `regression_pipe_to_bin*` use 1 MB src cap. Cyrius
  self-host's `_self_host_pipe` uses 2 MB + raw status —
  not interchangeable. Compile-shaped helpers stay in
  `programs/check.cyr` for that reason.

WHAT IT DOES NOT PROVIDE:
- Test framework with assert macros / fixture discovery /
  parallel runners — that's `lib/test.cyr` material;
  decision (memory pin 2026-04-30): keep narrow + earn
  on consumer ask.
- cyrius-specific gate dispatchers (`_tcyr_relay_gate`,
  `_expected_output_gate`) — depend on audit-internal
  accounting, stay in `programs/check.cyr`.

CONSUMER NOTE — sandhi:
sandhi's `_sandhi_conn_connect_nb` shares the exact
non-blocking-connect + poll + SO_ERROR mechanics. If a
future sandhi minor (1.2.0+) wants to factor down to a
shared primitive, the natural target is a `lib/net.cyr`
extension `net_connect_nb(fd, addr, port, timeout_ms)`
that both modules compose-use. Default per sandhi's
1.2.0 roadmap entry: leave parallel impls intentional
unless profiling surfaces a hot-path reason.


[[entries]]
name = "f64v_simd_primitives"
description = "Flat-array SIMD packed-double builtins. f64v_add/sub/mul/div/sqrt/abs/fmadd (v1.9.0; cross-arch close v5.10.16) + f64v_dot/scale/axpy (v5.10.17 — keystone for hisab gap-close). x86 SSE2 + aarch64 NEON .2D + cx per-lane scalar loops (v6.4.32)."
category = "cyrius"
---
SHAPE:
    f64v_add(dst, a, b, n);    # dst[i] = a[i] + b[i]
    f64v_sub(dst, a, b, n);    # dst[i] = a[i] - b[i]
    f64v_mul(dst, a, b, n);    # dst[i] = a[i] * b[i]
    f64v_div(dst, a, b, n);    # dst[i] = a[i] / b[i]
    f64v_sqrt(dst, src, n);    # dst[i] = sqrt(src[i])
    f64v_abs(dst, src, n);     # dst[i] = |src[i]|
    f64v_fmadd(dst, a, b, c, n); # dst[i] = a[i]*b[i] + c[i]
    var s = f64v_dot(a, b, n); # f64 = sum(a[i]*b[i])
    f64v_scale(dst, a, s, n);  # dst[i] = a[i] * s (scalar)
    f64v_axpy(y, x, alpha, n); # y[i] += alpha * x[i]

All take RAW POINTERS (i64 addresses) and i64 element
counts (in DOUBLES, not bytes). f64 args / returns are
i64 bit-pattern representations — use f64_from(x) /
f64_to(x) to convert int <-> f64.

PAIR-STEP:
    Loop processes 2 doubles per iteration (SSE2 packed-
    double has 2 lanes; aarch64 NEON .2D similarly).
    For odd n the last iteration touches one element
    past the array (16-byte load/store starting at
    index n-1). Caller contract: over-allocate by one
    slot or pass even n. Pre-existing footgun preserved
    cross-arch for parity.

CROSS-ARCH ENCODINGS:
    x86 SSE2: addpd / subpd / mulpd / divpd / sqrtpd /
    andpd (sign-mask abs) / mulpd+addpd (fmadd) / haddpd
    (dot reduce) / unpcklpd (broadcast).

    aarch64 NEON .2D: fadd / fsub / fmul / fdiv / fsqrt
    / fabs / fmla (fmadd, dot accumulate, axpy) / faddp
    (dot reduce) / dup (broadcast). FMLA gives axpy a
    fused single-instruction y += x*alpha — measurable
    win over the x86 mulpd+addpd two-instruction shape.

    cx: per-lane scalar loops (v6.4.32 — EMIT_F64V_LOOP/
    UNARY/FMADD/DOT/SCALE/AXPY, with the fsqrt opcode 0x68).
    The cx f64 pipeline is no longer no-op/TBD.

CONSUMER COMPOSITION (hisab Vec3/Vec4/Mat4):
- |v|^2: f64v_dot(v, v, 3 or 4)
- normalize(v): f64v_dot then 1/sqrt then f64v_scale
- lerp(a, b, t): copy a → dst; f64v_axpy(dst, b-a, t, n)
  (or compose with f64v_sub + f64v_scale)
- mat4_mul row · col: f64v_dot per output cell
- ray_sphere intersect: dot products dominate the cost

PARSER DISPATCH:
- Tokens: 89-91 (add/sub/mul), 93 (div), 94 (sqrt), 95
  (abs), 96 (fmadd), 128 (dot), 129 (scale), 130 (axpy).
- 89-91 inlined in PARSE_TERM; 93-96 + 128-130 dispatch
  to PARSE_SIMD_EXT in src/frontend/parse_expr.cyr.
- Statement-level dispatch in parse.cyr extended at
  v5.10.17 to cover typ 128-130 (pre-fix bare
  `f64v_dot(...);` statements gave "unexpected unknown").

LATENT BUG HISTORY:
- Pre-v5.10.16 the existing primitives 89-96 were
  aarch64-stubbed (no-op) AND had an x86 codegen bug
  under auto-regalloc (40+ byte frame-disp mismatch
  vs EFLSTORE). Both surfaced via v5.10.16 premise
  check. See gotchas.cyml f64v_simd_frame_disp_under_
  regalloc.
- programs/simd_expand_test.cyr was the only consumer
  for ~3.5 years and was never wired into check.sh —
  that is why both bugs hid. v5.10.16 added
  tests/tcyr/simd.tcyr as the regression floor.

[[entries]]
name = "type_system_real_phase_arc"
description = "Real type system: annotations + call-site checks + registry-based overload dispatch (v5.10.1 - v5.10.26 5-phase arc)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX (CALL-SITE CHECKING):
    fn add(a: i64, b: i64): i64 { return a + b; }

    var x: i64 = 5;
    var y: i64 = 7;
    var r = add(x, y);     # types match → pass
    var s = add(x, "hi");  # type mismatch at parse-time → error

WHAT v5.10.x ADDED:
    - Type-annotation registry per fn: param_types[fi][pi]
      stored at parse-time. Call-site argument expressions
      get their result-type tagged; mismatch raises a parse
      error before codegen.
    - Overload dispatch: fns with the same base name but
      different param-type fingerprints register independently.
      Call sites pick the matching variant based on the actual
      argument types observed.
    - Type-rewriting for sum types / structs (consumed by
      derive). The compiler can now answer "what is the type
      of this expression" at every point in parse, not just
      "what is the slot width".

ARC PHASES:
    Phase 1 (v5.10.1)  — annotations parsed + stored in registry.
    Phase 2 (v5.10.13) — call-site arg-type checking.
    Phase 3 (v5.10.18) — overload dispatch (same-name disambig).
    Phase 4 (v5.10.22) — return-type recording + cross-call flow.
    Phase 5 (v5.10.26) — sum-type rewriting + derive-friendly.

WHY:
    Pre-v5.10.x, all fn args were i64-shaped at the codegen
    layer; type annotations existed only as documentation.
    Real typed-simd ABI (f64v2/f64v4 value-form params) couldn't
    work without compile-time knowledge of param types — needed
    to pick the right register class (GPR vs XMM/V) per param.
    The type system arc was the unblock for the typed-simd
    ABI arc (v5.10.28 - .39).

CONSUMERS:
    - Typed-simd value-form fns (lib/simd.cyr, value-form half).
    - Sum-type derive (agnosys serialize/deserialize).
    - Pointer-vs-value overload dispatch (v5.10.39).

[[entries]]
name = "typed_simd_value_type_abi"
description = "f64v2 and f64v4 as primitive value types — XMM/V0 register-pair param + return ABI across all backends"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    fn add(a: f64v2, b: f64v2): f64v2 {
        return f64v2_add(a, b);
    }

    var x: f64v2 = f64v2_make(0x4000000000000000, 0x4008000000000000);
    var y: f64v2 = f64v2_make(0x4010000000000000, 0x4014000000000000);
    var r: f64v2 = add(x, y);   # passes via XMM0/XMM1, returns via XMM0

ABI BY BACKEND:
    x86 SysV (Linux/macOS/BSD):
        f64v2 → 1 XMM register (XMM0..XMM7 for params, XMM0 for return).
        f64v4 → 2 XMM register pair (XMM0+XMM1, XMM2+XMM3, …; return
            in XMM0+XMM1).

    aarch64 AAPCS (Linux/macOS):
        f64v2 → 1 V register (V0..V7 for params, V0 for return).
        f64v4 → 2 V register pair (V0+V1, …; return in V0+V1).

    cx bytecode:
        f64v2 → register pair (r0+r1, r2+r3, …).
        f64v4 → 4 registers (r0..r3, r4..r7, …; return in r0..r3).

    Win64 PE:
        Value-form params NOT SUPPORTED on PE (deliberate gap —
        Win64 ABI requires retptr-style for >8-byte aggregates
        which would force per-call shadow space + caller-side
        return-buffer pre-alloc, breaks call-site overload).
        PE consumers use pointer-form wrappers (`f64v2_add_ptr`).

CYRIUS-INTERNAL SPLIT-COUNTER (SysV/AAPCS):
    A SIMD ordinal counts XMM0..XMM7 (or V0..V7) consumption
    independently of the int ordinal (RDI/RSI/RDX/RCX/R8/R9
    or X0..X7). A fn signature like `fn f(a: i64, b: f64v2,
    c: i64, d: f64v2)` places a→RDI, b→XMM0, c→RSI, d→XMM1
    (NOT b→RSI/RDX, d→R8/R9 as would happen if everything
    shared one counter).

WHEN TO USE:
    - Math / numeric kernels (hisab Vec3/Vec4/Mat4, geometry).
    - Reducing call overhead in hot loops vs pointer-passing.
    - Value-form is FASTER than pointer-form when chaining
      ops (no stack write+read round-trip between calls).

WHEN NOT TO USE:
    - PE-targeted code (use pointer-form).
    - Mixed-target stdlib code that must compile to all
      backends — gate value-form definitions with
      `#ifdef CYRIUS_HAS_VAL_SIMD_PARAMS`.

ARC PHASES (v5.10.28 - .39):
    Phase 1  (v5.10.28) — f64v2 ABI shape design (XMM0 single).
    Phase 2  (v5.10.29) — x86 SysV f64v2 return path.
    Phase 3  (v5.10.30) — aarch64 NEON f64v2 return (V0).
    Phase 4  (v5.10.31) — Win64 PE retptr fallback (return only).
    Phase 5  (v5.10.32) — cx bytecode pair (r0+r1).
    Phase 6  (v5.10.33) — macho Mach-O inherits SysV; full pair return.
    Phase 7  (v5.10.36) — aarch64 single V0 NEON (was X0+X1 GPR pair).
    Phase 8  (v5.10.37) — f64v4 quad-register return.
    Phase 9  (v5.10.38) — VALUE-FORM PARAMS (the unlock).
    Phase 10 (v5.10.39) — pointer-vs-value overload dispatch.

[[entries]]
name = "f32v4_packed_single_simd"
description = "f32v4 — 128-bit packed single-precision (4× f32 lanes) value type + addps/subps/mulps, on the structured-descriptor SLTYPE encoding. Complete on all four backends: x86 SSE (Phase 1, v6.4.4), aarch64 NEON (v6.4.28), Win64 PE value-form (v6.4.31), cx per-lane bytecode (v6.4.32). SIMD Phase 5 done."
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    var a: f32v4 = f32v4_make(0x40000000, 0x40000000,   # 2.0f × 4
                              0x40000000, 0x40000000);
    var b: f32v4 = f32v4_splat(0x3F800000);             # 1.0f × 4
    var r: f32v4 = f32v4_add(a, b);                     # 3.0f × 4 (addps)
    var l0     = f32v4_lane0(r);                        # extract lane (i32 bits)

    # value-form param + return (reuses the f64v2 XMM ABI):
    fn scale2(v: f32v4): f32v4 { return f32v4_add(v, v); }

f32 values are their i32 BIT PATTERNS (1.0f = 0x3F800000,
2.0f = 0x40000000). Convert with f32_from / f32_to.

THE ENCODING (Phase 0 structured descriptor):
    A SIMD vector's SLTYPE is a STRUCTURED descriptor in a
    reserved negative band <= -2048 (collision-free — struct
    sids cap at 1024, so struct sltypes are >= -1024). One
    `_vec_desc()` (src/common/util.cyr) decodes it:
        desc       = |sltype| - 2048
        is_float   = desc & 1
        lane_log2  = (desc >> 2) & 7   # 2 → 4-byte lanes
        lanes_log2 = (desc >> 5) & 7   # 2 → 4 lanes
    f32v4 = sentinel -2121 (desc 73 = float | 4-byte | 4-lane
    → 16 bytes). Legacy f64v2/f64v4 keep their FLAT -20/-21,
    so the f64 SIMD path is byte-identical; f32v8 / integer
    lanes extend by adding descriptor VALUES, not code arms.

FOOTPRINT + ABI:
    f32v4 = 16 bytes = 2 slots = 1 XMM = the SAME footprint as
    f64v2, so it reuses f64v2's value-form ABI machinery
    (ESTOREPARM_F64V2 / ELOAD_F64V2_TO_XMM). Value-form params
    are gated `#ifdef CYRIUS_HAS_VAL_SIMD_PARAMS` (non-PE), same
    as f64v2/f64v4 — PE consumers use the pointer-form wrappers.

PACKED OPS (x86 SSE, Phase 1):
    f32v_add / f32v_sub / f32v_mul → addps / subps / mulps —
    the f64 packed path MINUS the 66 prefix (SIB scale ×4,
    stride 4 bytes/lane). Builtin tokens 138-140 (NOT 135-137:
    135 is `await`). aarch64 NEON EMIT_F32V_LOOP shipped v6.4.28
    (fmul+fadd, NOT fmla, to match x86 rounding) and cx per-lane
    loops shipped v6.4.32 → f32v4 compute runs on x86, aarch64,
    PE, and cx.

MATMUL OPS (x86 SSE, Phase 2, v6.4.5) — flat-array builtins:
    f32v_fmadd(dst, a, b, c, n) → dst[i] = a[i]*b[i] + c[i]
        (mulps + addps; token 141). The dense-matmul inner loop.
    f32v_dot(a, b, n) → f32 bit pattern (sum a[i]*b[i]); token 142.
        mulps+addps accumulate, then TWO haddps fold the 4 lanes,
        movd eax extracts the 32-bit result. CALLER CONTRACT: n a
        multiple of 4, or over-allocate to a multiple of 4 with
        ZEROED padding — the tail iteration sums up to 3 lanes past
        n (same footgun as f64v_dot). aarch64 NEON (v6.4.28,
        EMIT_F32V_FMADD/DOT) + cx per-lane (v6.4.32) implement
        these — SIMD Phase 5 complete.

lib/simd.cyr WRAPPERS (value + pointer form):
    f32v4_make / f32v4_splat / f32v4_lane{0..3} /
    f32v4_add / f32v4_sub / f32v4_mul  — `&IDENT` auto-routes
    to the `_ptr` form (same overload dispatch as f64v2).
    f32v4_fmadd(a,b,c) / f32v4_dot(a,b) (v6.4.5) — the n=4
    single-vector forms of the matmul ops (always lane-safe;
    dot returns f32 bits, use f32_to for the value).

GUARDS (v6.4.4, adversarial-review-found):
    - `v.field` on any SIMD vector HARD-ERRORS ("SIMD vector
      has no named fields") — the -2121 sentinel must never be
      read as a struct id (it exceeds the 1024 struct cap →
      would be an out-of-bounds struct-table read). Extract
      lanes via f32v4_laneK(&v), not `.field`.
    - A value-form SIMD arg is type-checked against the param
      when the ABI engages (SIMD-returning callee): an f64v2
      arg to an f32v4 param is rejected, not silently run as
      addps over f64 bit patterns.

[[entries]]
name = "integer_vector_simd"
description = "Integer SIMD vectors (128-bit): i8v16/i16v8/i32v4/i64v2 (+ unsigned u*) + packed add/sub/mul (iv_add/sub/mul), value-form typed ops (i32v4_add(a,b)), and the u8·i8→i32 widening dot iv_dp8 (the b1.58 GEMM inner loop). SIMD Phase 3a/3b (v6.4.6/v6.4.7); implemented on x86 SSE2, aarch64 NEON (v6.4.30), and cx (v6.4.32) — Phase 5 complete."
category = "cyrius"
---
TYPES (128-bit, structured-descriptor SLTYPE like f32v4):
    i8v16 / u8v16   — 16 lanes ×  8-bit
    i16v8 / u16v8   —  8 lanes × 16-bit
    i32v4 / u32v4   —  4 lanes × 32-bit
    i64v2 / u64v2   —  2 lanes × 64-bit
  Descriptor: signed = bit 1 (i* set, u* clear), lane_log2 = bits
  2-4, lanes_log2 = bits 5-7 (util.cyr _vec_desc / GVEC_SIGNED).
  i32v4 = sentinel -2122 (vs f32v4 -2121: the float/signed bits).
  All share f32v4's 16 B / 1-XMM value-form ABI (descriptor-driven
  via _is_simd128 — return/receive/param, byte-identical for f64).

PACKED OPS — flat-array builtins (x86 SSE2):
    iv_add(dst, a, b, n, w)   dst[i] = a[i] + b[i]   (paddb/w/d/q)
    iv_sub(dst, a, b, n, w)   dst[i] = a[i] - b[i]   (psubb/w/d/q)
    iv_mul(dst, a, b, n, w)   dst[i] = a[i] * b[i]   (pmullw/pmulld)
  n = lane count; w = lane BYTE-WIDTH, a COMPILE-TIME LITERAL
  (1/2/4/8) that selects the instruction. Tokens 143-145. add/sub
  are two's-complement (signed==unsigned); mul is i16/i32 ONLY
  (w=2/4) — w=1/8 hard-errors (no 1-instruction pmulb/pmulq).
  Caller contract (same tail footgun as f32v_dot): n a multiple of
  16/w, or over-allocate the lane count. aarch64 NEON (v6.4.30,
  EMIT_IVEC_BINOP) + cx per-lane (v6.4.32) implement these too.

WIDENING DOT — iv_dp8(a, b, n) → i32 (SIMD Phase 3b, v6.4.7):
    iv_dp8(a, b, n)   sum(a[i] * b[i])   a = u8, b = i8, n%16==0
  The BitNet/b1.58 quantized-matmul inner loop (ternary weights
  {-1,0,1} are just i8). EMIT_IVEC_DP8 = pxor accumulator +
  pcmpeqw/psrlw ones-vector, 16-lane loop of movups / pmaddubsw
  (u8×i8 → i16 pairs) / pmaddwd ×ones (i16 → i32 widen) / paddd,
  reduced with two phaddd + movd eax + movsxd rax,eax (SIGN-extend
  — a negative dot must not come back zero-extended). Token 146.

lib/simd.cyr WRAPPERS:
  POINTER form (v6.4.6): i32v4_make(a,b,c,d) / i32v4_splat(x) /
    i32v4_lane{0..3}_ptr / i32v4_add_ptr / _sub_ptr / _mul_ptr ;
    i16v8/i8v16/i64v2 splat/make + _add_ptr/_sub_ptr(/_mul_ptr).
  VALUE form (v6.4.7): i32v4_add/_sub/_mul(a,b) + i32v4_lane{0..3}(v);
    i16v8_add/_sub/_mul ; i8v16_add/_sub ; i64v2_add/_sub. Integer
    128-bit VALUE params now route through XMM as a full 16 bytes:
    the value-form param-mask (class 1) was generalized from "exactly
    f64v2" to "any 16-byte vector" (_is_simd128), and the caller
    class-1 arg type-check relaxed to match (f32v4 code 3 stays
    strict). VALUE-form SIMD args must be BARE LOCAL IDENTs (not
    call-result temporaries) — the same constraint f32v4 carries.

[[entries]]
name = "f64v256_ymm_packed_double"
description = "f64v256_add/sub/mul/div — the 256-bit packed-DOUBLE kernel (4× f64 in one YMM), v6.5.24. The f64 sibling of f32v8, ten minors later: SIMD fix-list item 3, which the roadmap had gated behind an IR/regalloc substrate it never needed. Measured 15.9 -> ~7.9 ns."
category = "cyrius"
---
BUILTINS: f64v256_add / f64v256_sub / f64v256_mul / f64v256_div (dst, a, b, n)
  Tokens 154-157, CONSECUTIVE so `ptyp - 154` is the op index. Lexed in
  LEXKW_EXT (klen == 11 arm, shared 8-byte prefix "f64v256_" + a 3-byte op
  tail; all three tail bytes checked). EMIT_F64V4_LOOP, x86/float.cyr.

⚠ WHY NOT NAMED `f64v4_add`. That IS the f32v8 family's convention — the
  256-bit TYPE names the builtin — but for f64 the name was already taken:
  lib/simd.cyr defines the VALUE-FORM `fn f64v4_add(a: f64v4, b: f64v4)`.
  f64v4 shipped years before its widening; f32v8 shipped after, so only
  f32v8 ever got the plain name. Reserving `f64v4_add` as a keyword would
  make that lib definition a "reserved word as identifier" parse error and
  break every consumer of the value form. So the KERNEL is named for its
  register WIDTH — which also leaves `f64v512_*` free for AVX-512.

ENCODING: the `pd` sibling of EMIT_F32V8_LOOP. 2-byte VEX C5, second byte
  FD instead of FC (pp=01, the 66-prefix slot) so every op is packed
  DOUBLE; SIB 0xF2 = [rdx+rsi*8]; rsi += 4; vzeroupper on loop exit.
    C5 FD 10 04 F2   vmovupd ymm0, [rdx+rsi*8]
    C5 FD 58/5C/59/5E C1   vaddpd/vsubpd/vmulpd/vdivpd ymm0, ymm0, ymm1
    C5 FD 11 04 F2   vmovupd [rdx+rsi*8], ymm0
    C5 F8 77         vzeroupper
⚠ FD vs FC is ONE BIT. Emit FC and every f64 op silently becomes a
  packed-SINGLE operation on double bit patterns — wrong numbers, no
  diagnostic, no crash. tests/gates/codegen/f64v4_ymm_disasm.sh pins the
  bytes AND the mnemonics for exactly this reason.

⚠ rsi ADVANCES BY 4, so this kernel can NEVER serve an f64v2 caller — a
  4-lane stride over a 16-byte vector runs off the end. f64v2 keeps tokens
  89-91 / EMIT_F64V_LOOP. Mutation-proven that a value test cannot see a
  wrong stride: with the stride set to 2, crossos/f64v4_ymm.tcyr still
  passed 23/23 while writing 16 bytes PAST the vector, because the second
  iteration recomputes lanes 2-3 correctly.

GATING: unconditional VEX, so lib/simd.cyr picks it only when
  simd_has_avx2() == 1 and otherwise keeps the 128-bit f64v_* path. All
  EIGHT f64v4 wrappers carry the gate (4 ops × value/_ptr form).
  simd_has_avx2() returns 0 on every non-x86 target (its CPUID probe is
  #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86), so aarch64 / Mach-O-arm64 / cx take the
  128-bit path for free. The aarch64 + cx EMIT_F64V4_LOOP stubs DELEGATE
  to their 128-bit loop rather than `return 0;` — a return-0 stub makes
  the verb a silent no-op, the defect class the cx multi-return stubs
  turned out to be at v6.5.21.

cycc itself never calls these, so its own self-host emits no VEX byte and
  stays byte-identical everywhere — same as every prior SIMD phase.

[[entries]]
name = "f32v8_avx2_256bit_simd"
description = "f32v8 — 256-bit SIMD (8× f32 in one YMM). SIMD Phase 4 R1 (v6.4.8), x86 AVX2 with a runtime SSE fallback. The FIRST VEX/AVX instructions the toolchain ever emits (before this: zero VEX; decode.cyr says 'no AVX/VEX')."
category = "cyrius"
---
TYPE: f32v8 = 8 lanes × f32 = 32 bytes = 256-bit = one YMM.
  Structured-descriptor sentinel -2153 (util.cyr _vec_desc → nslots 4,
  laneb 4). Shares f64v4's 32-byte / 2×128-XMM value-form return ABI —
  the f64v4 return/receive paths (parse_fn.cyr + parse_decl.cyr) were
  generalized from exact -21 to _is_simd256 (byte-identical for f64v4).
  Construct/return works cross-arch (aarch64 too); arithmetic now runs on
  aarch64 (v6.4.29, via a 2×128-bit NEON fallback = EMIT_F32V_LOOP) and cx
  (v6.4.32) as well as x86 AVX2. NATIVE 256-bit stays x86-AVX2-only — the
  aarch64 256-bit emitters are return-0 stubs never reached at runtime;
  lib/simd.cyr routes f32v8 through native f32v4 NEON, so the verb works.

OPS (x86 AVX2, SSE2 is NOT baseline — vaddps ymm #UDs on pre-AVX2):
    f32v8_add / f32v8_sub / f32v8_mul (dst, a, b, n)  tokens 147-149
  EMIT_F32V8_LOOP = the f32v4 loop widened to 256-bit VEX: 128-bit SSE
  bytes + a 2-byte VEX prefix (C5 FC), 8 f32/iter (rsi += 8), vzeroupper
  (C5 F8 77) on exit. vaddps ymm = C5 FC 58 C1 (llvm-mc verified; the
  vvvv field is the 1's-complement-of-src1 byte-order trap). aarch64
  (v6.4.29, 2×128-bit NEON fallback) + cx (v6.4.32, aliases f32v_loop)
  implement these; the simd_f32v8 XFAIL was removed at v6.4.29. Disasm-gated
  (tests/simd_f32v8_disasm.sh) host-CPU-independently.

RUNTIME FALLBACK (the reason f32v8 is usable off AVX2 CPUs):
  simd_has_avx2() (lib/simd.cyr) — CPUID leaf 7 EBX bit 5, a byte-for-byte
  clone of sigil's _sha_ni_cpuid_probe; cached, idempotent, no CAS latch.
  The f32v8_*_ptr wrappers branch INSIDE the single call site: AVX2 →
  vaddps ymm; else f32v_*(…, 8) = 2×SSE. Each path emitted once
  program-wide (zero per-call bloat). cycc has no f32v8 → stays SSE2,
  self-hosts byte-identical everywhere.

lib/simd.cyr WRAPPERS (pointer-form first; value-form deferred):
    f32v8_make(8 args) / f32v8_splat(x) / f32v8_lane{0..7}_ptr /
    f32v8_add_ptr / _sub_ptr / _mul_ptr.
  R2 (v6.4.9): f32v8_fma (vfmadd231ps ymm = C4 E2 7D B8 D1 — FMA3, the
    FIRST 3-byte VEX; token 150) + f32v8_dot (token 151: vmulps/vaddps ymm
    accumulate, then vextractf128 xmm1,ymm2,1 + vaddps xmm **L=0** fold +
    vzeroupper + the f32v4 haddps×2/movd tail — two-haddps alone can't
    cross the 128-bit split). f32v8_fma_ptr / f32v8_dot_ptr wrappers.
    FMA needs a DIFFERENT CPUID bit than AVX2: simd_has_fma() = leaf 1 ECX
    bit 12 (clone of aes_ni's leaf-1 probe), NOT leaf 7 EBX bit 5. The
    fma fallback (mulps+addps, 2 roundings) can differ 1 ULP from fused —
    use exact-product inputs for bit-equality. bench_f32v8_gemm ~1.48×
    (256 vs 128-bit; sub-2× — fixed per-cell reduce overhead). Phase 4
    CLOSES: f32 SIMD complete on x86 (f32v4 128-bit + f32v8 256-bit).

[[entries]]
name = "overload_dispatch_pointer_vs_value"
description = "Pointer-form overload routing: `f64v2_add(&x, &y)` auto-routes to `f64v2_add_ptr`; `f64v2_add(x, y)` calls value-form base"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    # Pointer form — auto-routes to f64v2_add_ptr:
    var x: f64v2; var y: f64v2;
    var r: f64v2 = f64v2_add(&x, &y);

    # Value form — calls f64v2_add (value-form base) directly:
    var a: f64v2; var b: f64v2;
    var r: f64v2 = f64v2_add(a, b);

    # On PE, only pointer form available (overload-routed):
    var r: f64v2 = f64v2_add(&x, &y);  # → f64v2_add_ptr

HOW IT WORKS:
    At PARSE_FNCALL in src/frontend/parse_fn.cyr, when the
    first argument is an `&local` (PEEKT == 27), the parser
    constructs the name `<base>_ptr` in a 128-byte stack
    scratch and runs `_FINDFN_CSTR` against it. If found,
    `fi` is rerouted to the _ptr variant; the call site
    emits a call to the pointer-form fn.

    No new heap region is used; this is name-mangling +
    registry lookup. Defensive guard: `_po_base_len < 120`
    prevents stack-scratch overflow (lesson from v5.10.39
    in-slot bug where original 16-byte scratch overflowed
    for ≥12-char base names).

WHY:
    Cyrius stdlib SIMD modules ship BOTH forms — value-form
    (faster, register-passed) and pointer-form (universal,
    PE-compatible). Without dispatch, consumers writing
    `f64v2_add(&x, &y)` would get "fn signature mismatch"
    because the f64v2_add VALUE-form expects f64v2 args, not
    pointers. Dispatch hides the dual-form impl detail.

CONSUMER POV:
    Write `f64v2_add(...)` with whatever shape your args
    happen to be in — `&local`, value, mix. The parser
    picks the right variant. Code stays portable to PE
    (which only has pointer-form) without consumer code
    changes; the value-form fns are #ifdef-gated in the
    stdlib, and dispatch transparently falls back to
    pointer-form when value-form doesn't exist.

GOTCHA — duplicate parse paths:
    Argument stash for these intrinsics happens in MULTIPLE
    dispatch blocks (PARSE_SIMD_EXT for ptyp 93-130, plus
    a separate dispatch at ptyp 89-91 for f64v_add/sub/mul).
    Stash slots MUST clear locname[idx] (= -1) at each stash
    site or stale name entries from prior fn parses cause
    FINDLOCAL misroute. See gotchas.cyml
    locname_staleness_duplicate_dispatch_paths for the full
    bug class write-up.

[[entries]]
name = "struct_byval_int_pair_return_abi"
description = "9-16B int-class user struct return ABI (v5.10.45-.47 arc). x86 SysV rax+rdx pair; aarch64 AAPCS64 X0+X1 pair; PE uses same rax+rdx (cyrius-internal-ABI deviation from MS x64 strict retptr). Str's 16B handle-shape stays scalar-rax via _STR_SID carve-out."
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    struct Point { x: i64; y: i64; }
    fn make(): Point {
        var p: Point;
        p.x = 7;
        p.y = 35;
        return p;          # 9-16B → pair-return mode
    }
    fn use(): i64 {
        var got: Point = make();
        return got.x + got.y;   # 42
    }

WHAT v5.10.45-.47 ADDED:
    - 9-16B int-class struct returns transit via register-pair
      ABI (rax+rdx on x86 SysV; X0+X1 on aarch64 AAPCS64;
      cyrius-internal rax+rdx on Win64 PE — deviation from
      strict MS spec).
    - Caller-side `var got: StructName = fn();` allocates 2
      stack slots + receives the pair via the per-backend
      `EFLSTORE_STRUCT_INT_PAIR` helper.
    - Callee-side `return IDENT;` emits the pair via
      `EFLLOAD_STRUCT_INT_PAIR`.
    - Distinguished from `var s: Str = str_from(...)` (Str's
      16B handle-shape pointer-mode) via `_STR_SID(S)` lookup
      at slot entry; Str-returning fns continue to use scalar
      rax for the heap-pointer.
    - For ≤8B struct returns: scalar rax (unchanged).
    - For >16B struct returns: hidden retptr via RDI/X8/RCX
      (v5.5.36 / v5.9.26 / Win64 — unchanged).

PRE-v5.10.45 (the bug):
    Pre-fix, ANY 9-16B struct return returned only the LOW
    8 bytes via scalar rax. `got.y` (the high half) was
    read from whatever slot happened to be adjacent in the
    caller's frame — typically 0. Live for the entire v5.x
    cycle; surfaced via empirical premise-check at v5.10.44
    ship.

WIN64 ABI DEVIATION NOTE:
    Strict Microsoft x64 ABI requires hidden-RCX retptr for
    any ≥9B composite return (no rax+rdx pair). Cyrius uses
    rax+rdx pair on PE too — internal-ABI deviation that
    works because all callers + callees agree on the
    convention. Future foreign-C interop with value-typed
    struct returns would need a separate PE-retptr-mode emit
    path (out of scope for v5.10.x). Documented per the
    closed-system / no-consumer-impact rationale in
    CHANGELOG [5.10.47].

CONSUMER POV:
    Same syntax that was always documented for value-typed
    struct returns; the implementation now matches the
    type signature. No source migration needed for any
    Str-using consumer — Str's pointer-mode semantics
    preserved.

[[entries]]
name = "bare_return_in_fn"
description = "v5.10.48: bare `return;` in fn body synthesizes `return 0;` per cyrius's int-only return convention"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    fn early_zero(x): i64 {
        if (x > 0) { return; }    # synthesizes return 0
        return 99;
    }

WHAT v5.10.48 ADDED:
    PARSE_RETURN peeks for `;` before calling PCMPE. If
    present, emits `mov rax, 0` + jump-to-epilogue — byte-
    for-byte equivalent to `return 0;` at that position.

PRE-v5.10.48:
    `if (x > 0) { return; }` errored with
    `error:<source>:N: unexpected ';'` because PCMPE was
    called unconditionally and rejected an empty expression.
    Required workaround: `return 0;` explicitly.

CONSUMER POV:
    Idiomatic early-exit shape now works. No-op for fns that
    already used `return 0;` explicitly; new ergonomic for
    early-exit branches in conditional bodies. Cyrius
    treats every fn as returning int (no `void`), so
    bare `return;` is unambiguously sugar for `return 0;`.

[[entries]]
name = "enum_ident_array_size"
description = "v5.10.48: var name[IDENT] accepts an enum-const ident as a compile-time-known array size, in both fn-local PARSE_ARRAY and top-level PARSE_GVAR_ARR paths"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    enum BufSz { SMALL = 8; MEDIUM = 64; }
    var small_buf[SMALL];          # top-level
    fn use(): i64 {
        var local[SMALL];          # in-fn
        store64(&local, 42);
        return load64(&local);
    }

WHAT v5.10.48 ADDED:
    Both PARSE_ARRAY (in-fn) and PARSE_GVAR_ARR (top-level)
    accept an IDENT in `[]` if it resolves to a compile-time-
    known enum constant (high-bit-set marker at
    `enum_const_val[idx]` per the v5.5.2 fold registration).

PRE-v5.10.48:
    `var buf[SIZE]` (where SIZE is an ident) errored with
    `error: expected number, got identifier 'SIZE'`.
    Workaround: inline the literal `var buf[16];`. The
    ident error pointed at the offending token without
    explaining the rule.

PLAIN var-NAME = LITERAL NOT SUPPORTED:
    `var SIZE = 16; var buf[SIZE];` still errors — cyrius
    has no named-constant infrastructure outside enums.
    The idiomatic pattern is `enum Sz { N = 16; }
    var buf[N];`. A specific error message points at the
    enum-const idiom rather than the generic
    "expected number, got identifier".

CONSUMER POV:
    Named constants for array sizes via enums. Bad-shape
    ident triggers a specific diagnostic explaining the
    enum-only rule, not the generic message.

[[entries]]
name = "socket_syscall_wrappers_v51118"
description = "7 socket-family syscall wrappers in lib/syscalls_x86_64_linux.cyr + lib/syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr peers (sys_socket, sys_bind, sys_listen, sys_connect, sys_recvfrom, sys_recvmsg, sys_accept4)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX (Linux only — Windows / macOS use platform-native APIs):
    var fd = sys_socket(domain, type, protocol);
    var rc = sys_bind(fd, sockaddr_ptr, addrlen);
    var rc = sys_listen(fd, backlog);
    var rc = sys_connect(fd, sockaddr_ptr, addrlen);
    var n = sys_recvfrom(fd, buf, len, flags, srcaddr_ptr, srcaddrlen_ptr);
    var n = sys_recvmsg(fd, msghdr_ptr, flags);
    var cfd = sys_accept4(fd, srcaddr_ptr, srcaddrlen_ptr, flags);

USE CASES:
    sys_socket + sys_bind + sys_recvfrom: sd_notify dgram for
        systemd-style service-readiness notification (kybernet 1.1.5+).
    sys_socket + sys_connect + sys_recvmsg: SCM_CREDENTIALS peer-cred
        capture for trusted-peer auth (argonaut 1.6.2+ already used
        sys_recvmsg via per-arch #ifdef enum — now centralized).
    sys_socket + sys_bind + sys_listen + sys_accept4: supervisor
        control-socket accept loop (kybernet 1.2.x edge-boot).

CROSS-ARCH SAFETY (load-bearing motivation):
    Pre-v5.11.18 consumers re-rolled per-arch syscall dispatch:
        #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86_64
            enum SockSysNr { SOCKET = 41; BIND = 49; RECVFROM = 45; }
        #else
            enum SockSysNr { SOCKET = 198; BIND = 200; RECVFROM = 207; }
        #endif
    Forgetting the dispatch (or copy-pasting from an x86 example)
    silently misrouted on aarch64: x86 41 → socket; aarch64 41 →
    pipe2. CI on x86 passes; aarch64 deployment misbehaves.
    The stdlib wrappers centralize this — no consumer can route
    wrong.

ARCH-SPECIFIC SYSCALL NUMBERS (encoded in lib/syscalls_*.cyr's
SysNr enums):

    Wrapper            x86_64  aarch64
    sys_socket            41     198
    sys_connect           42     203
    sys_recvfrom          45     207
    sys_recvmsg           47     212
    sys_bind              49     200
    sys_listen            50     201
    sys_accept4          288     242

TEST: tests/tcyr/socket_syscalls.tcyr runtime-exercises
sys_socket on AF_UNIX/SOCK_DGRAM (load-bearing aarch64
verify — a misrouted dispatch returns pipe2 fd, not socket).
Other 6 wrappers verified via &fn compile-time callability.

[[entries]]
name = "bote_async_primitives_v51115"
description = "lib/thread.cyr chan_try_recv + lib/async.cyr cancel_token_* (cancel_token_new / signal / check) — bote-streaming-dispatch building blocks"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    # Non-blocking channel receive (lib/thread.cyr)
    var val = chan_try_recv(ch);     # 0 if empty (avoid sending 0
                                     #   if 0-as-sentinel matters)

    # Cancellation tokens (lib/async.cyr)
    var tok = cancel_token_new();    # alloc 8-byte atomic-backed slot
    cancel_token_signal(tok);        # idempotent set; atomic_store(tok, 1)
    var cancelled = cancel_token_check(tok);  # 0 live, 1 cancelled

INTENDED PATTERN (bote streaming dispatch):
    # Transport thread:
    var tok = cancel_token_new();
    dispatch_streaming(handler, tok, progress_chan);
    # ... on $/cancelRequest:
    cancel_token_signal(tok);

    # Worker thread (handler):
    while (more_work()) {
        if (cancel_token_check(tok) == 1) {
            cleanup();
            return;
        }
        chan_send(progress_chan, partial_result);
        do_work_chunk();
    }

UNDERLYING:
    chan_* shipped at v5.5.31 (MPSC, futex + mutex backed,
        chan_new/send/recv/close). chan_try_recv added v5.11.15
        completes the non-blocking surface. MPMC-safe, and blocking
        wherever CHAN_BLOCKING == 1, since 6.6.16.
    cancel_token_* backed by lib/atomic.cyr's atomic_load /
        atomic_store (aligned i64 ops on x86_64 + aarch64; explicit
        API documents the atomicity intent). No busy-wait — worker
        polls between work chunks at its own cadence.

PREMISE-CHECK NOTE: bote's original filing speculated 3 missing
primitives (MPSC channel, cancellation, per-thread arena). All 3
already existed; the slot shipped 4 thin shims (`chan_try_recv` +
3 cancel-token verbs) instead of inventing parallel infrastructure.
See CHANGELOG [5.11.15] for the discovery doc block at top of
lib/async.cyr.

[[entries]]
name = "arena_lifecycle_v51114"
description = "Arena lifecycle in lib/alloc.cyr — arena_new/alloc/reset/free (v5.11.14) + the v6.5.9 growable arena and ARENA_FULL_* exhaustion policy"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    var a = arena_new(SIZE);      # fixed-capacity arena
    var p = arena_alloc(a, n);    # bump-pointer alloc
    arena_reset(a);               # rewind to start (per-frame reuse)
    arena_free(a);                # FINAL release (v5.11.14)

THE LIFECYCLE:
    arena_new → many arena_alloc → arena_reset (reuse) → many
    arena_alloc → ... → arena_free (terminal)

LOAD-BEARING USE CASE (bote per-frame reuse):
    # Per WS / SSE frame: alloc scratch buffers, dispatch handler,
    # reset arena for next frame, terminate arena at session end.
    var a = arena_new(64 * 1024);
    while (session_alive == 1) {
        var msg_buf = arena_alloc(a, MAX_FRAME);
        var resp_buf = arena_alloc(a, MAX_RESP);
        handle_frame(msg_buf, resp_buf);
        arena_reset(a);
    }
    arena_free(a);

== EXHAUSTION IS A POLICY, NOT A RETURN VALUE (v6.5.9) ==

What an exhausted arena DOES was the gap, not what it returns.
`arena_alloc` returning 0 was always correct; the problem is that
a `Str` of 0 is indistinguishable from a valid `Str`, there is no
option type and no error channel through the `_a` families, so
the 0 flows on and the first thing that touches it dereferences
it. "Arena too small" was observable as a SIGSEGV several layers
away, in a JSON walker, with no indication which allocator ran out.

⭐ THE REGRESSION DIRECTION IS THE POINT. Before threading a route
onto an arena, an oversized response was a LEAK on the no-free
global bump. After threading — a change whose whole purpose is to
stop leaking — the same response is a CRASH. Adopting the feature
correctly made the failure worse and quieter.

Four behaviours, one field on the arena header:

    ARENA_FULL_NULL  = 0   return 0 — TODAY'S BEHAVIOUR, still the
                           DEFAULT, so no existing arena moves
    ARENA_FULL_GROW  = 1   chain another chunk
    ARENA_FULL_SPILL = 2   serve overflow from the global allocator
                           (weaker — spilled bytes are never
                           reclaimed by reset — but the crash
                           becomes the pre-arena cost)
    ARENA_FULL_ABORT = 3   die loudly AT the allocation instead of
                           faulting three layers away

    var a = arena_new_growable(64 * 1024);   # = new + policy GROW
    arena_set_on_full(a, ARENA_FULL_ABORT);  # or set it explicitly
    var pol = arena_on_full(a);              # read it back (-1 if a == 0)
    var cap = arena_capacity_total(a);       # whole chain, which
                                             # arena_used cannot show
                                             # once it has grown

Allocator-vtable form: `arena_allocator_growable(initial)` and
`arena_allocator_set_on_full(al, policy)` (the vtable's state
pointer IS the arena handle, so the setter reaches through).

⚠ GROW RETAINS ITS CHUNKS ACROSS `arena_reset`. The bump allocator
underneath has no free(), so releasing them is not expressible —
and it is not what an arena wants anyway. Reset rewinds to the
FIRST chunk and re-uses the chain, so a request loop converges on
its high-water mark and then allocates nothing.

⚠ `arena_free` previously cleared only base/ptr/end, leaving the
policy set — so a "freed" growable arena took the grow path and
SUCCEEDED. It now resets the policy to `ARENA_FULL_NULL`, which is
what makes "further allocs fail" true again.

Header layout is `{ base, ptr, end, policy, first_chunk, cur_chunk,
chunk_size }` = 56 bytes; base/end mirror the CURRENT chunk so the
hot path stays two loads.

PREMISE-CHECK NOTE: bote's original filing was titled "fl_free" —
premise check showed `arena_reset` (the load-bearing per-frame
reuse primitive) already shipped at v5.5.x; the actual missing
surface was the terminal `arena_free`. v5.11.14 added the
terminator. The v6.5.9 filing (from agnosai) likewise asked for
THREE things — a growable arena, a spilling arena, and an
exhaustion policy — which collapse into the one policy field
above, which is why all three shipped together.

[[entries]]
name = "sock_set_recv_timeout_v51113"
description = "lib/net.cyr sock_set_recv_timeout(fd, secs, usecs) — Slowloris defense for HTTP / WS servers"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    var rc = sock_set_recv_timeout(fd, 5, 0);    # 5-second timeout
    var rc = sock_set_recv_timeout(fd, 0, 250000);  # 250ms

SEMANTICS:
    Sets SO_RCVTIMEO via setsockopt. After setting, sock_recv(fd, ...)
    that blocks longer than (secs, usecs) returns -EAGAIN (or -EWOULDBLOCK).
    Per-fd setting; persists for the fd's lifetime.

LOAD-BEARING USE CASE (Slowloris defense):
    # Per accept(), set a recv timeout so a misbehaving client
    # can't tie up a worker thread by trickling bytes:
    var cfd = sys_accept4(lfd, ..., SOCK_CLOEXEC);
    sock_set_recv_timeout(cfd, 5, 0);    # 5s wall-clock cap on slow reads
    serve_http(cfd);

VERIFICATION (from bote 1.9.5 audit response):
    var fd = sys_socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
    # ... connect to a slow-server fixture ...
    sock_set_recv_timeout(fd, 1, 0);    # 1-second timeout
    var t0 = gettime_ns();
    var n = sock_recv(fd, buf, len);    # blocks ~1.056s, returns -EAGAIN
    var elapsed_ms = (gettime_ns() - t0) / 1000000;
    assert(elapsed_ms >= 1000 && elapsed_ms < 1200);

GAP (part B of original bote filing): getaddrinfo equivalent —
DNS resolver surface — was deferred (multi-slot scope) and not
pinned in v5.11.x. Track via bote consumer asks.

[[entries]]
name = "ws_handshake_rfc6455_v51116"
description = "lib/ws_server.cyr ws_server_handshake enforces RFC 6455 §4.1: Sec-WebSocket-Key must be 24-char base64 (16-byte payload encoded with `=` padding)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SEMANTICS:
    ws_server_handshake(cfd, req_buf, req_len) parses the HTTP
    upgrade request. Pre-v5.11.16, it accepted any non-empty
    Sec-WebSocket-Key value — including malformed lengths.

POST-v5.11.16 (RFC §4.1 conformance):
    Sec-WebSocket-Key is the base64 encoding of a 16-byte value.
    Base64 of 16 bytes = 22 chars + 2 chars of `=` padding = exactly
    24 chars. Pre-fix accepted "short" / 27-char / arbitrary lengths;
    post-fix rejects all but 24-char and returns 0 (per docstring:
    caller responds 400 Bad Request).

FIX SHAPE (5 lines in lib/ws_server.cyr::ws_server_handshake):
    var klen = strlen(key);
    if (klen != 24) { return 0; }      # NEW: reject malformed length
    var magic = _wss_magic();
    # ... SHA-1 + base64 Accept derivation continues unchanged

CONSUMER IMPACT:
    Spec-compliant clients send 24-char keys; pre-fix they were
    accepted regardless of compliance. Post-fix they're STILL
    accepted (no behavior change for compliant clients), but
    malformed clients now fail-closed at the handshake instead of
    silently progressing to bidirectional frame I/O.

WHY THIS MATTERS:
    The handshake produces Sec-WebSocket-Accept = base64(sha1(key +
    magic)). A SHA-1 over arbitrary-length input still produces a
    syntactically-valid Accept header — the client would receive the
    upgrade and the WS connection would proceed normally over a
    spec-violating handshake. Closes a real conformance hole bote
    filed in audit.

[[entries]]
name = "cap_raises_v51118_v51119"
description = "Compiler capacity caps and their history. CURRENT (6.6.20): fn_table init 2048 growable to a 131072 hard ceiling, identifier pool 8 MiB at 0xEC00000, var_table to 1,048,576, fixup table to 64M entries, preprocessed source 24 MB. The v6.4.76 block and the v5.11.18/.19 raises this entry was written for (131072 → 262144 / 4096 → 8192) are HISTORY — see the SUPERSEDED blocks."
category = "cyrius"
---
CURRENT CAPS (6.6.20 — verify against src/common/util.cyr
`_capacity_warnings`, which tests/gates/memory/capacity_meter_denominators.sh
derives; READ THIS BLOCK, the rest is history):

    fn_table:           init 2048 (v6.5.51), doubles, hard ceiling 131072
                        (v6.5.40; `error: too many functions (max 131072)`)
    identifier pool:    8,388,608 B (8 MiB) at 0xEC00000 (v6.5.40)
    var_table:          init 8192, doubles to a hard 1,048,576 (v6.3.0)
    fixup_table:        init 1,048,576, doubles to a 67,108,864-entry
                        runaway ceiling (the warning still divides by
                        1,048,576)
    string_data:        2,097,152
    code buffer:        8 MiB base, +8 MiB steps, 64 MiB ceiling (v6.4.49)
    preprocessed source: 24 MB (`_SRC_CAP` = 25,165,824, v6.5.40)

WARNING THRESHOLDS (85% — integer `count * 100 / cap >= 85`):
    fn_table warn at:           111,412 fns   (85% of the 131072 CEILING,
                                               not of the live _fnt_cap)
    identifier pool warn at:    7,130,317 B   (85% of 8,388,608)
    LEX hard error at:          np + need >= 8,388,336 (lex.cyr; 272 B of
                                slack for the inner write loop)

SUPERSEDED v6.4.76 (this was the CURRENT block until 6.6.20, 4-16x
stale by then):

    fn_table:           8192 INITIAL, doubles to a 32768 hard ceiling
    identifier pool:    524288 bytes (512 KB) at heap 0x60000-0xE0000
    var_table:          8192
    fixup_table:        1048576
    fn_table warn at 27853 fns; identifier pool warn at 445645 bytes;
    LEX hard error at np + need >= 524016.

SUPERSEDED v6.4.75 / v6.4.76 — what actually changed, and why the old
numbers here were dangerous:
    * v6.4.75 (P0): six fn-INDEXED side tables were still pinned to
      FIXED 8192-slot heap bands even though the fn table itself became
      growable at v6.2.0. Past 8192 fns, index `fi` of each table wrote
      into slot `fi-8192` of its NEIGHBOUR — silent metadata corruption
      with NO diagnostic. Fixed by lazy-alloc'ing all six at the 32768
      max. The capacity WARNING was complicit: it divided by the LIVE
      `_fnt_cap`, so it screamed "99 %" at 8191 fns and went SILENT the
      instant the table doubled (9000/16384 = 54 %) — exactly when the
      corruption started. It now divides by the fixed 32768 ceiling.
    * v6.4.76: the identifier pool doubled 256 KB → 512 KB IN PLACE
      (0x60000-0xE0000, into the free 0xA0000-0x100000 band). The pool
      END had to stay <= 0x100000 — above that physical ceiling the
      compiler HUNG rather than erroring. LEX thresholds moved
      261872 → 524016. (HISTORY since v6.5.40 moved the pool out of
      that band entirely, to 8 MiB at 0xEC00000.)
    * NOTE ON "8192" (v6.4.76): most 8192 references in the tree were
      then CORRECT — it was the fn_table INITIAL cap (and the var_table
      cap outright). A blanket 8192 → 32768 sweep would have INTRODUCED
      errors. Today 8192 is the var_table INITIAL cap only; the
      fn_table starts at 2048.

SUPERSEDED v6.2.0 (Phase 0):
    The fn-tables (and both fn hashes) became GROWABLE — when the count
    would exceed `_fnt_cap` (init 8192), REGFN doubles the family and
    rehashes the name-hash, up to the 32768 ceiling (the 2 B hash slot
    stores fi+1, so fi+1 <= 65535). So the "load factor degrades to 1.0
    at 8192" cliff below no longer applies: the hash grows with the cap
    (mask = cap-1), keeping the factor bounded.

--- HISTORY (v5.11.18 / v5.11.19, the raises this entry was filed for) ---

CAPS AS OF v5.11.19:

    fn_table:           4096  → 8192    (v5.11.19)
    identifier buffer:  131072 → 262144 bytes  (v5.11.18)

WARNING THRESHOLDS THEN (85% of the then-cap):

    fn_table warn at:           >= 6963 fns        (was >= 3686)
    identifier buffer warn at:  >= 222822 bytes    (was >= 111430)

CONSUMER MOTIVATION:
    kybernet 1.1.0 (AGNOS PID-1 init system) assembled the full
    AGNOS surface (stdlib + agnosys-full + agnostik + libro + patra
    + argonaut + sigil + sakshi) and hit:

        fn_table at 92% (3779 / 4096)
        identifier buffer at 85% (112094 / 131072 bytes)

    Warned thresholds; non-recoverable at 100%. kybernet 1.1.1
    worked around via profile-bundle trim (agnosys-full →
    agnosys-core); v1.2.0 edge-boot work brings agnosys-storage +
    agnosys-trust back into scope — would tip past hard cap.

UPSTREAM FIX SHAPE:
    v5.11.18 Part A.i — identifier buffer doubled in-place; uses
        existing 0xA0000-0x18C100 heap-map gap; no relocation.
    v5.11.18 Part B — 7 socket-syscall wrappers (separate consumer
        ask from kybernet 1.1.5 P(-1) audit).
    v5.11.19 Part A.ii — fn_table heap-map refactor: 7 scattered
        fn_* tables relocated, contiguous extended + primary blocks
        stride doubled, IR + fixup regions shifted +0x80000. cc5
        self-host byte-identical at 804,456 B (was 804,464).
        Hash tables (fn_name_hash + fn_start_hash) kept at 8192
        slots × 2B (mask 8191 preserved) — gap couldn't fit doubled
        hash tables AND doubled per-fn tables; trade-off documented.

CONSUMER REGRESSION TESTS:
    `tests/tcyr/capacity_*` (existing gates) verify warn-threshold
    semantics with the new caps. programs/check.cyr::_capacity_gate
    bumped the synthetic stress fixture from 3500 fns → 7000 fns
    at v5.11.19 to re-trigger the >= 85% threshold (3500/8192 =
    42.7% no longer fires).

NO ALGORITHMIC / API / ON-DISK CHANGE:
    Pure cap raise. Existing consumer source compiles unchanged.
    Hash table load factor at 8192 fns degrades to 1.0 (was 0.5
    at 4096); for the kybernet 3779-fn worst case load factor is
    0.46 — well below probe cliff. (Both superseded — see the two
    SUPERSEDED blocks at the top of this entry.)

[[entries]]
name = "named_op_refactor_drift_gate_v51135_v51139"
description = "v5.11.35-.39 parser-to-emit named-op refactor arc: per-backend EMIT_<OP> helpers replace direct EB(S, 0xXX) byte emits in parse_*.cyr. Classes B (PIC-vs-direct address loads), C (f64 unary ops), D (v5.7.12 audit doc). v5.11.39 drift gate locks the pattern: programs/check.cyr scans parse_*.cyr for EB(S,...) and fails if any reappear"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.11.35-.38 migrated parser-to-emit direct-byte calls
to named-op helpers per backend:

  CLASS B (v5.11.36) — PIC-vs-direct address loads:
      EMIT_LEA_ABS / EMIT_MOVI_ABS per backend
      (x86: 48 8D 05 disp32 vs 48 B8 imm64;
       aarch64: ADRP+ADD vs LDR=literal)

  CLASS C (v5.11.37) — f64 unary ops:
      EF64_NEG, EF64_FROM, EF64_TO, EF64_ABS, EF64_SIN,
      EF64_COS, EF64_EXP, EF64_LN (8 new named ops
      per backend covering ptyps 71/83/84/85/86/87/88/99)

  CLASS D (v5.11.35) — aligned with v5.7.12 cx audit
      doc; covers ptyp 89-91 missed by v5.10.35's PARSE_SIMD_EXT
      locname-staleness fix

  v5.11.38 — Class B missed-site closeout
  v5.11.39 — ESWITCH_DISPATCH_* family; drift gate

DRIFT GATE — `_parse_emit_drift_gate()` in
`programs/check.cyr` scans all `parse_*.cyr` files
for any line containing `EB(S,` (the direct byte-emit
shape) and fails check.sh if found. The 6 parse files
covered (`parse_decl.cyr`, `parse_expr.cyr`,
`parse_fn.cyr`, `parse_ctrl.cyr`, `parse_types.cyr`,
`parse_top.cyr`) are now structurally locked against
re-introducing parser-side architecture-specific
direct emits.

WHY THIS MATTERS — cross-arch leak prevention:

Pre-refactor pattern: a parser fix lands a `EB(S, 0x48); EB(S, 0xB8); ...`
sequence for x86. The same parser code runs on the
aarch64 build, but those bytes are x86 — silent
miscompile when the aarch64 build emits them. Named
ops force each backend to provide its own
EMIT_<OP>(S) impl; missing impl = link error, not
silent garbage.

The drift gate locks the refactor. Without it, the
next parser fix that "just adds a few bytes" would
quietly re-introduce the class.

[[entries]]
name = "f64_abs_peephole_v51140"
description = "v5.11.40 — f64_abs(x) peephole optimization. Single fold replaces 6-instruction sequence (mov-xmm + and-mask + ret) with a 2-instruction sequence (ANDPS xmm0, [rip+mask] / RET). Long-pinned audit optimization landed at .40"
category = "cyrius"
---
The `f64_abs(x)` peephole consolidates a 6-instruction
sequence into 2 instructions. Long-pinned in the audit
doc (Class C refactor scope) and landed at v5.11.40
after the .37 Class C f64-unary named-op refactor
made the consolidation site singular instead of
scattered.

EF64_ABS(S) is the named op per backend (x86 emits
ANDPS xmm0, [rip+0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF mask] which clears
the sign bit; aarch64 emits FABS d0, d0).

[[entries]]
name = "cve_08_hardening_cld_v51141"
description = "v5.11.41 — CVE-08 security hardening: emit cld (0xFC, 1 byte) before any rep movsb / rep stosb sequence. Closes the direction-flag-set-by-prior-callee class"
category = "cyrius"
---
Per the v5.0.x security audit, calling convention does
NOT guarantee DF (direction flag, EFLAGS bit 10) is
cleared at function entry. A misbehaving callee that
sets DF (via `std`) would cause `rep movsb` in the
caller to walk backwards through memory — silent data
corruption.

Fix at v5.11.41: cyrius's PE / Linux / Mach-O backends
now emit `cld` (0xFC, 1 byte) immediately before any
`rep movsb` / `rep stosb` sequence. 1-byte hardening
per call site; cc5 size delta +<10 B.

Reference: docs/audit/2026-04-13-security-audit.md
CVE-08.

[[entries]]
name = "lsp_semantic_tokens_locals_params_v51142"
description = "v5.11.42 — cyrius-lsp textDocument/semanticTokens/full legend extended to color locals + parameters (was: only keywords + types + identifiers)"
category = "cyrius"
---
cyrius-lsp's `semanticTokens/full` provider now emits
distinct token-type values for:

  - Local variables (`var x = ...` inside fn bodies)
  - Function parameters (`fn f(p1, p2)`)

These were previously emitted as plain identifiers,
indistinguishable from globals / fn names / type
names in syntax highlighting. v5.11.42 added the
legend entries + the per-token-type emission logic
in `programs/cyrius-lsp.cyr`. Editors with LSP
semanticTokens support (Helix, Zed, VS Code via
extension, JetBrains Fleet) now render locals
differently from globals.

[[entries]]
name = "elf64_kernel_multiboot2_emit_v51143"
description = "v5.11.43 — EMITELF64_KERNEL: ELF64 + multiboot2 + EFI64-entry tag kernel emit. Gated by env var CYRIUS_ELF64_KERNEL=1 flipping _TARGET_ELF64_KERNEL flag. Routes kmode==1 dispatch to the new emitter instead of EMITELF_KERNEL (legacy ELF32+mb1)"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.11.43 added ELF64-kernel emit for AGNOS UEFI x86_64
boot via GRUB-EFI long-mode handoff. Same `kernel;`
source-language directive; new env var
`CYRIUS_ELF64_KERNEL=1` flips `_TARGET_ELF64_KERNEL`
at `src/main.cyr:1003` and routes the kmode==1
dispatch in `src/backend/x86/fixup.cyr:1884`:

    if (L64(S + 0x18FCA0) == 1) {
        if (_TARGET_ELF64_KERNEL == 1) { EMITELF64_KERNEL(S); }
        else { EMITELF_KERNEL(S); }
        return 0;
    }

EMITELF64_KERNEL writes:

  ELF64 header        64 B  (e_ident class=2 ELFCLASS64;
                              e_type=ET_EXEC; e_machine=EM_X86_64)
  Program header       56 B  (PT_LOAD, p_vaddr=0x100000,
                              p_flags=RWX, p_align=0x1000)
  Multiboot2 header    48 B  (magic 0xE85250D6, arch i386,
                              tags: ENTRY_ADDRESS_EFI64 type=9,
                                    MODULE_ALIGN type=6, END type=0)
  Code                 cp B   (.text)
  BSS-as-zeros        totvar B (in p_filesz; SHT_NOBITS in shdr)
  RODATA              spos B   (.rodata strings)
  Section header tab  + .shstrtab (GRUB compat — v5.11.29)

→ entry = 0x100000 + 120 + 48 = 0x1000A8

KEY GOTCHA pinned at v5.11.46: FIXUP's kmode==1
hard-coded `entry = 0x100060` (legacy ELF32+mb1
layout) which produces a 0x48-byte fixup shift on
ELF64-kernel binaries. See
field_notes/compiler/gotchas/elf64_kernel_fixup_entry_mismatch_v51146.

[[entries]]
name = "efi_application_pe_emit_v51147"
description = "v5.11.47-.49 — UEFI Application PE32+ emit mode. CYRIUS_TARGET_EFI=1 env var flips both _TARGET_PE=1 and _TARGET_EFI_APPLICATION=1. Subsystem byte 3→10 (WINDOWS_CUI→EFI_APPLICATION); EEXIT emits single ret; no kernel32 imports; Data Dirs [1]/[12] zeroed; RELOCS_STRIPPED cleared (.49)"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.11.47-.49 added UEFI Application emit unblocking
the AGNOS sovereign UEFI bootloader (gnoboot).

ACTIVATION:

    CYRIUS_TARGET_EFI=1 cyrius build src/main.cyr build/BOOTX64.EFI

Sets both `_TARGET_PE = 1` (EFI implies PE32+
container + MS x64 ABI + 4KB SectionAlignment +
0x200 FileAlignment + .reloc + DllCharacteristics
NX_COMPAT) and `_TARGET_EFI_APPLICATION = 1` (the
deltas below).

DELTAS FROM WINDOWS-CUI PE:

  pe/emit.cyr:746   Subsystem byte 3 → 10 (EFI_APPLICATION)
  x86/emit.cyr:545  EEXIT emits single 0xC3 (ret) — firmware
                    reads rax as EFI_STATUS (vs the 13-byte
                    kernel32!ExitProcess IAT call in CUI mode)
  pe/emit.cyr:439   skip _pe_imp_add("ExitProcess") in _pe_layout
  pe/emit.cyr:_pe_register_kernel32
                    fail-fast on any kernel32 reroute under EFI
                    mode — UEFI has no kernel32; silent miscompile
                    avoided
  pe/emit.cyr:Data Dirs [1] + [12] zeroed under EFI
  pe/emit.cyr:776   DllCharacteristics force NX_COMPAT under EFI
                    even with zero .reloc (v5.11.48)
  pe/emit.cyr:714   COFF Characteristics clears RELOCS_STRIPPED
                    under EFI (v5.11.49 — UEFI firmware needs
                    this latitude to place the image at any
                    free address)

CONSUMER ENTRY-POINT SHAPE:

UEFI firmware calls AddressOfEntryPoint with
RCX=ImageHandle, RDX=SystemTable (MS x64 ABI). cyrius
PE entry prologue is `e9 00 00 00 00` (5-byte jmp +0,
register-preserving), so firmware-supplied RCX/RDX
survive into the first user instruction. CAPTURE
PATTERN: first user statement must be inline asm
(fn calls clobber RCX/RDX with arg-setup mov's).
See programs/efi_probe.cyr for the canonical shape.

REGRESSION GATES:

  _efi_emit_gate()         (v5.11.48) — structural
                                        Subsystem + NX_COMPAT + Data Dirs zeroed
  _efi_ovmf_smoke_gate()   (v5.11.49) — runtime
                                        boot qemu+OVMF; grep "hello, uefi"

UNBLOCKS: gnoboot (sovereign UEFI bootloader, ~2000
LoC of cyrius) for AGNOS MVP boot path. Consumer pin
`cyrius = "5.11.49"` in cyrius.cyml.

[[entries]]
name = "efi_probe_canonical_uefi_program_v51148"
description = "programs/efi_probe.cyr — minimal 64-LoC cyrius UEFI Application that writes 'hello, uefi' via SystemTable->ConOut->OutputString. Single asm block; no globals, no fixups, all runtime register-indirect addressing. Canonical inline-asm-first capture pattern for UEFI entry args"
category = "cyrius"
---
programs/efi_probe.cyr is the lower-bound shape for a
cyrius UEFI Application:

```cyrius
kernel;
asm {
    # mov rcx, [rdx+0x40]    ; ConOut = SystemTable->ConOut
    0x48; 0x8B; 0x4A; 0x40;
    # mov rax, [rcx+0x08]    ; OutputString fn ptr
    0x48; 0x8B; 0x41; 0x08;
    # lea rdx, [rip+0x0E]    ; rdx = &msg (14 bytes past end-of-LEA)
    0x48; 0x8D; 0x15; 0x0E; 0x00; 0x00; 0x00;
    # sub rsp, 0x28          ; shadow space + 8B align (MS x64)
    0x48; 0x83; 0xEC; 0x28;
    # call rax               ; OutputString(rcx=ConOut, rdx=msg)
    0xFF; 0xD0;
    # add rsp, 0x28
    0x48; 0x83; 0xC4; 0x28;
    # xor eax, eax           ; EFI_SUCCESS = 0
    0x31; 0xC0;
    # ret                    ; firmware reads rax as EFI_STATUS
    0xC3;
    # nop (align msg to 2-byte CHAR16)
    0x90;
    # UTF-16LE "hello, uefi\r\n\0" (28 bytes, 14 CHAR16)
    0x68; 0x00;  # h
    0x65; 0x00;  # e
    # ... (msg bytes)
}
```

NO GLOBALS by design — zero fixups means zero DIR64
entries means no .reloc section (test the cyrius PE
backend's "EFI mode with empty .reloc" path).

RIP-RELATIVE LEA with hand-computed displacement —
no need for inline-asm fixup-record machinery (which
cyrius doesn't have). The msg sits 14 bytes past the
end of the LEA instruction; disp32 = 0x0E.

UEFI ABI USED:

  MS x64 calling convention — RCX/RDX = first 2 args
  0x20 byte shadow space + 16-byte stack alignment
  EFI_STATUS return in rax

SYSTEM TABLE LAYOUT (UEFI spec § 4.3):

  offset 0x40 — ConOut* (EFI_SIMPLE_TEXT_OUTPUT_PROTOCOL)

SIMPLE_TEXT_OUTPUT_PROTOCOL (UEFI spec § 12.4):

  offset 0x00 — Reset
  offset 0x08 — OutputString  ← this is what we call
  offset 0x10 — TestString
  ...

CHAR16 STRINGS:

  UTF-16LE, NUL-terminated, 2-byte aligned

USE AS REFERENCE: when writing a heavier cyrius UEFI
app (gnoboot, future EFI tools), capture the firmware
args via inline asm first thing, then transition to
regular cyrius code. The captured handles can be
stored in globals once captured (subsequent global
writes won't clobber the firmware-supplied registers
because they're already saved).

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_dce_verbose_env_v51145"
description = "v5.11.45 — CYRIUS_DCE_VERBOSE=1 env var gates the per-fn name list in cycc's 'note: N unreachable fns ...' stderr output. Default-off: 38 lines collapse to 1 line per compile. Verbose mode opt-in for actual cleanup work"
category = "cyrius"
---
Pre-v5.11.45 cycc always emitted the full list of
unreachable fn names to stderr during compile:

    note: 37 unreachable fns (22871 bytes — set CYRIUS_DCE=1 to eliminate)
      dead: TS_lex_token_advance
      dead: TS_parse_type
      dead: _macho_emit_load_command
      ... 37 entries ...

Most of these are mode/arch-dispatched false positives
(TS_*, _macho_*, ir_*, aarch64-only fns reachable
only via --mode flags or other main_*.cyr) per the
v5.8.48 dead-code-audit-scope rule. 38 lines of
noise per compile.

v5.11.45 gated the per-fn list behind
`CYRIUS_DCE_VERBOSE=1`:

  Default mode:       1 line summary "note: N unreachable fns (B bytes)"
  CYRIUS_DCE_VERBOSE=1: full per-fn list (38+ lines)

Pre-existing byte-count off-by-2 bug fixed in the
same slot (was dropping the `)\n` from each line's
byte estimate).

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_vet_restored_v51145"
description = "v5.11.45 — cyrius vet restored as a real audit tool. build/cybs had been silently replaced with the 12 KB bootstrap binary (no vet/deny dispatch); install.sh _rebuild_stale skipped because mtime was newer than source. Fixed via explicit syscall(60) exit in cybs.cyr + scan-buffer cap raise 16 KB → 256 KB. cybs 12,344 B → 44,672 B"
category = "cyrius"
---
`cyrius vet` walks an entry .cyr source's include
graph and lists transitive dependencies. `cyrius deny`
lists policy violations.

At v5.11.45 entry, `cyrius vet src/main.cyr` was
silently returning "no dependencies" — incorrect:
main.cyr has 14 includes. Root cause: `build/cybs`
had been replaced with the 12 KB bootstrap binary
(which has no vet/deny dispatch). `install.sh
--refresh-only` carried the bad binary into
`~/.cyrius/bin/cybs`; the `_rebuild_stale` check
skipped rebuild because the bad binary's mtime was
newer than the source — same shape of silent slip
as build/cc5's mabda contamination.

FIXES IN v5.11.45 P(-1) BUNDLE:

  programs/cybs.cyr — added explicit `syscall(60, r);`
    exit (vet was running twice; the second run was
    being preempted by the runtime fall-through path)
  programs/cybs.cyr — scan-buffer cap 16 KB → 256 KB
    (was truncating reads on src/main.cyr's 86 KB
    source and falsely reporting "no dependencies")

Post-fix: `cyrius vet src/main.cyr` correctly lists
14 includes, exit 0; `cyrius deny` lists 0 violations.
cybs 12,344 B (bootstrap) → 44,672 B (real audit tool).

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_lsp_argv0_self_resolution_v51144"
description = "v5.11.44 — cyrius-lsp uses /proc/self/exe to resolve its install dir and find cycc via <install-dir>/cycc BEFORE the 3 existing fallback paths. Editors with minimal-env LSP launchers (Claude Code et al.) no longer silently disable diagnostics"
category = "cyrius"
---
cyrius-lsp's `find_cc5()` previously tried 3 fallback
paths to locate the cycc binary:

  1. $CYRIUS_HOME/bin/cycc
  2. ~/.cyrius/bin/cycc
  3. /usr/local/bin/cycc

In editors that launch LSP servers with a minimal env
(no $HOME, no $CYRIUS_HOME) — Claude Code is the
canonical example — all 3 fallbacks miss and the LSP
silently returns "diagnostics disabled, can't find
cycc". User sees no syntax errors / type errors /
unresolved-name warnings on edit; manual `cyrius
build` is the only way to surface them.

v5.11.44 added a 4th, FIRST-tried path:
`<install-dir>/cycc` where install-dir is resolved via
`/proc/self/exe → realpath → dirname`. The cyrius-lsp
binary is shipped at `~/.cyrius/bin/cyrius-lsp`
alongside `~/.cyrius/bin/cycc`, so the self-resolution
finds cycc immediately regardless of caller env.

IMPLEMENTATION SHAPE (mirrors v5.11.39
programs/cyrius-init.cyr::_resolve_templates_dir):

```cyrius
fn _resolve_install_dir(): Str {
    var buf[1024];
    var n = sys_readlink("/proc/self/exe", &buf, 1024);
    if (n <= 0) { return str_from(""); }
    # walk back to dirname
    while (n > 0 && load8(&buf + n) != '/') { n = n - 1; }
    return str_from_buf(&buf, n);
}
```

Startup log line `[cyrius-lsp] found cycc:
<resolved-path>` makes the resolution path debuggable.

Reference: docs/development/proposals/archived/cyrius-lsp-argv0-self-resolution.md
(filed 2026-05-02, closed 2026-05-13).

[[entries]]
name = "ts_tsx_to_js_emitter"
description = "cycc --emit-js <file.tsx> — emit runnable browser JavaScript from TypeScript/TSX (v6.1.11)"
category = "cyrius"
---
Cyrius has a TypeScript/TSX frontend (`src/frontend/ts/*`) and a JS
backend (`src/backend/js/emit.cyr`). `cycc --emit-js file.tsx`
type-strips and lowers TS/TSX to real, runnable JavaScript:

- Strips interfaces / type aliases / annotations / `as` / `!` /
  generics (`<T>`).
- Lowers JSX to `h(tag, props, ...kids)` calls — the pragma is
  configurable via `CYRIUS_JSX_PRAGMA` (default `h`), with a
  standalone `h` runtime prelude emitted.
- ESM passthrough with type-only export pruning; verbatim
  string / template literals.
- AST-driven (built on the v6.1.10 children-list allocator fix);
  emits indented output (v6.1.12). `for` / `for-of` / `for-in`
  headers fixed at v6.1.12.

The emitted JS passes `node --check` + a `--parse-ts` round-trip.
This is x86-Linux-only (the TS frontend is not in the cross
binaries). See also `build_target_js`.

[[entries]]
name = "build_target_js"
description = "cyrius build --target=js — CLI wrapper over cycc --emit-js (v6.1.12)"
category = "cyrius"
---
`cyrius build --target=js <file.tsx>` is the wrapper-level entry
point for the TS/TSX → JS emitter (`ts_tsx_to_js_emitter`). It
resolves the source, invokes `cycc --emit-js`, and writes the
JavaScript output — the consumer-facing way to compile a `.tsx`
to browser JS without calling `cycc` directly. v6.1.12.

[[entries]]
name = "preprocessor_if_numeric"
description = "#if NAME >= VALUE — numeric preprocessor comparison (==, !=, <, >, <=, >=) (v5.6.x)"
category = "cyrius"
---
Beyond `#ifdef` / `#ifndef` (defined-ness), the preprocessor
evaluates NUMERIC comparison directives:

```
#define LEVEL 3
#if LEVEL >= 2
  ... compiled in ...
#endif
#if LEVEL >= 4
  ... compiled out ...
#endif
```

Operators: `==` `!=` `<` `>` `<=` `>=`. The LHS is a `#define
NAME VALUE` integer (a bare `#define NAME` carries presence only,
no value — and `cyrius build -D NAME` likewise sets presence, NOT
a value, so a threshold knob must be set in source). Implemented in
`src/frontend/lex_pp.cyr` (`PP_EVAL_IF`) since the v5.6.x
preprocessor work; works at top level AND inside fn bodies.

Production use: sakshi's `#define SAKSHI_LEVEL <0..5>` compile-time
log-level threshold (`#if SAKSHI_LEVEL >= n` per level) — every
level above the threshold costs zero bytes and zero cycles.

[[entries]]
name = "pie_codegen"
description = "--pie / CYRIUS_PIE=1 — position-independent (ET_DYN) executables (x86_64 v6.1.6, aarch64 v6.1.8)"
category = "cyrius"
---
`--pie` (or env `CYRIUS_PIE=1`) emits a position-independent
executable: `ET_DYN` ELF with `p_vaddr=0` and RIP-relative (x86_64)
/ `adrp`+`add` (aarch64) addressing, so the loader can ASLR the
whole image. Userland PIE shipped x86_64 at v6.1.6 and aarch64 at
v6.1.8 (the full `.tcyr` corpus runs as ASLR'd PIE binaries). It
reuses the proven `shared` / object-emit (`_IS_OBJ`) machinery;
non-PIE output stays byte-identical. A kernel-PIE ELF wrapper
(`EMITELF64_KERNEL`, AGNOS KASLR) exists under `--pie` for the
`kernel;` form but awaits an AGNOS boot harness.

[[entries]]
name = "gnu_hash_dynamic_link"
description = ".gnu.hash dynamic-linking — O(1) Bloom-filter symbol lookup, drops SysV .hash (v6.1.9)"
category = "cyrius"
---
Cyrius shared objects (`EMITELF_SHARED`) emit a single-bucket
`.gnu.hash` section + `DT_GNU_HASH` instead of the legacy SysV
`.hash` / `DT_HASH`. The native loader (`lib/dynlib.cyr`) was
already gnu-hash-only, so this flips `.so` symbol resolution onto
the O(1) Bloom-filter path instead of a linear `.dynsym` fallback.
x86-only (aarch64 has no `.so` path). v6.1.9.

[[entries]]
name = "windows_directory_listing"
description = "dir_list / is_dir / dir_walk on Windows via FindFirstFileW (v6.1.18)"
category = "cyrius"
---
On Windows (PE), `lib/fs.cyr`'s directory primitives now work —
they were no-ops (returning `-38`) before v6.1.18 because
`getdents64` has no Windows route. `lib/fs_win.cyr` (included under
`#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN`) routes `dir_list` to
FindFirstFileW / FindNextFileW / FindClose and `is_dir` to
GetFileAttributesW, via four new `0xF016`-`0xF019` kernel32
reroutes. `dir_walk` / `dir_list_full` / `find_files` work
transitively. This completes the Windows file surface (process /
thread / TLS-via-args / env / file-I/O / directory enumeration all
work as of v6.1.16–v6.1.18). v6.1.18.

[[entries]]
name = "bayan_ganita_stdlib_carve"
description = "Data-format + advanced-math carved OUT of the core stdlib into opt-in siblings — lib/bayan.cyr (json/toml/cyml/csv/base64/bigint/u128, v6.1.25) + lib/ganita.cyr (matrix/linalg/advanced-math, v6.1.26)"
category = "cyrius"
---
The data-format and advanced-math families no longer ship inside the
always-present stdlib. They were carved into two opt-in sibling
modules:

  - `lib/bayan.cyr` (v6.1.25) — json / toml / cyml / csv / base64 /
    bigint / u128.
  - `lib/ganita.cyr` (v6.1.26) — matrix / linalg / advanced-math.

OPT-IN — they are NOT auto-prepended. A consumer that wants them
includes explicitly:

    include "lib/bayan.cyr"
    include "lib/ganita.cyr"

NAMING:
    Canonical symbols are `bayan_*` / `ganita_*`. The legacy names
    (`base64_encode`, `json_parse`, `csv_*`, `bigint_*`, `matrix_*`,
    etc.) are kept as THIN SHIMS that forward to the canonical fns —
    existing source keeps compiling, new code should prefer the
    `bayan_*` / `ganita_*` spelling.

DELETED FILES:
    The old standalone `lib/base64.cyr`, `lib/csv.cyr`,
    `lib/json.cyr`, `lib/toml.cyr`, and `lib/bigint.cyr` were
    REMOVED — their content lives in `lib/bayan.cyr` now. Replace any
    `include "lib/json.cyr"` (etc.) with `include "lib/bayan.cyr"`.

RATIONALE:
    Bare-metal / kernel consumers (AGNOS objects) were dragging
    data-format + math code into the link whenever they pulled in a
    transitive stdlib include. Carving these out keeps kernel objects
    free of JSON / bignum / linear-algebra code they never call —
    you pay for the data-format/math surface only when you opt in.

[[entries]]
name = "native_tls_default"
description = "Native TLS 1.2/1.3 (client+server, over sigil) is the no-flag DEFAULT since v6.1.21 — libssl is now opt-OUT"
category = "cyrius"
---
Native TLS — the pure-cyrius TLS 1.2 / 1.3 stack (client AND server)
built over sigil — is the DEFAULT transport as of v6.1.21. No flag is
needed to get it; `tls_*` calls resolve to the native implementation
out of the box.

OPT-OUT (libssl):
    The legacy libssl-backed path is now opt-OUT, selected with:

        cyrius build -D CYRIUS_TLS_LIBSSL ...

    Without that define, no OpenSSL / libssl dependency is linked or
    dlopen'd — the TLS surface is fully sovereign.

This is the inverse of the pre-v6.1.21 default (libssl was the
no-flag path; native TLS was opt-in). The flip makes the
no-dependency, no-crates posture the one you get for free.

[[entries]]
name = "f64_named_type_and_operators"
description = "`var x: f64` / `var x: f32` tags a variable IEEE-754 double / single, so bare `+ - * /` route to EMIT_F{64,32}_BINOP and `< > <= >= == !=` to EF{64,32}_CMP (a REAL float compare, not integer-bit ordering) on x86/PE/Mach-O (SSE2/SSE) + aarch64 (NEON) + cx. Scalar `f64` is also a legal fn RETURN type (xmm0, sentinel -9). Shipped v6.2.18 (f32 conv) + v6.2.19 (named f64) + v6.4.55 (f64 return) + v6.4.56 (f32 ARITHMETIC + comparison + WARN-only typecheck) + v6.4.57 (float params + compound assign) + 6.6.11 (compound assign on a float GLOBAL and in a `for` step, the f32 right-operand warning, typed copies `var t = p.y`)."
category = "cyrius"
---
NAMED FLOAT TYPE (v6.2.19 for f64, v6.4.56 for f32):
  var pi: f64 = ...;        # local
  var e: f64;               # global
  var s: f32 = ...;         # single precision (v6.4.56)
A `: f64` / `: f32` annotation tags the var in the type table, so bare
operators on it lower to float ops instead of integer ops:

  ARITHMETIC  + - * /          -> EMIT_F64_BINOP   (x86 addsd/subsd/mulsd/
                                  divsd; aarch64 fadd/fsub/fmul/fdiv .d)
                               -> EMIT_F32_BINOP   (x86 addss/subss/mulss/
                                  divss; aarch64 fadd/fsub/fmul/fdiv s)
  COMPARISON  < > <= >= == !=   -> EF64_CMP / EF32_CMP  (real float compares —
                                  verified on negatives, NOT integer-bit order;
                                  ucomisd / ucomiss + the NaN parity fold)

Normal arithmetic precedence; the typed-operator dispatch reuses the
existing GESTYPE / SESTYPE infrastructure. Literals and the conversion
builtins propagate their result type, so `f64_from(n)` feeds straight
into operator expressions. A 3-byte-prefix collision once let `f64`
hijack the f64v2 / f64v4 SIMD types — the scalar f64/f32 checks run
AFTER the SIMD ones to disambiguate.

f32 SCALAR ARITHMETIC IS LIVE (v6.4.56 — this entry said "type-only /
no f32 arithmetic" until the v6.4.82 closeout; that was WRONG from
v6.4.56 onward):
    var a: f32 = f32_from(f64_from(3));
    var b: f32 = f32_from(f64_from(4));
    var s: f32 = a + b;            # addss / fadd s0,s0,s1
    if (a < b) { ... }             # ucomiss / fcmp — a REAL f32 compare
    var out = f64_to(f32_to(s));   # extract back to an i64 you can print
  A f32 is BOXED as its IEEE-754 bit pattern in the low 32 bits of the
  GP accumulator. There is no f32 literal syntax — CONSTRUCT with
  `f32_from(f64_from(int))`, EXTRACT with `f64_to(f32_to(x))`.
  Complete on x86 / Win64 PE / Mach-O / aarch64 NEON (S-regs) / cx
  (widen-op-narrow). `f32_from` / `f32_to` (v6.2.18, cvtsd2ss /
  cvtss2sd / fcvt) remain the conversion pair.

SCALAR f64 AS A RETURN TYPE (v6.4.55) — returned in xmm0:
    fn half(x: f64): f64 { var two: f64 = f64_from(2); return x / two; }
  Return-type sentinel -9 (the SIMD band is <= -2048). The callee emits
  `movq xmm0, rax` before the epilogue, the caller `movq rax, xmm0` after
  the call and tags the result F64_TYID, so a bare operator on the call
  result dispatches to the f64 emitters. Real `movq` on x86/PE/Mach-O,
  no-op on aarch64/cx (both already return the bits in the GP reg).
  **`: f32` is NOT a legal return type** — the allow-list is
  i8/i16/i32/i64/Result/Option/Tagged/cstring/f64/f64v2/f64v4 + structs
  + the SIMD sentinels. Return an f32 as `i64` (it is already just bits).

FLOAT PARAMS + COMPOUND ASSIGN (v6.4.57): a `: f64` / `: f32` PARAM's
local slot is tagged too, so `fn inc(x: f64): f64 { ... x + one ... }`
computes instead of integer-adding the bit pattern; and `x += y`,
`-=`, `*=`, `/=` on a float local route through EMIT_F{64,32}_BINOP.

⚠ COMPOUND ASSIGNMENT ON A FLOAT GLOBAL AND IN A `for` STEP (6.6.11).
Until 6.6.11 only a float LOCAL got float arithmetic from `+= -= *= /=`:
    var G: f64 = 1.5;
    G += 1.0;          # 6.6.10: 9216616637413720064 (the two bit patterns
                       # added as integers); `G -= 0.5` and `G *= 2.0` gave 0
    for (var x: f64 = 0.0; x < 1.0; x += 0.25) { ... }   # 6.6.10: 2 passes, not 4
— silently, at top level and inside a fn, on x86 and aarch64 alike; an
f32 global was garbage the same way. The classic-`for` step was its own
integer-only copy of the compound arm and knew five of the ten compound
operators (`i <<= 1` as a step was "expected '='"). Since 6.6.11 the
statement and the step share one implementation (`_asg_compound_tok` /
`_asg_compound_load` / `_asg_compound_op`, parse.cyr): `+= -= *= /=`
on an f64 / f32 local OR global, in a statement, a top-level statement
or a `for` step, are float operations, and the RIGHT operand is checked
as `x = x op y` would be (`t += 1` on an f64 warns — 1's bits are a
subnormal; write `t += 1.0`). `%= &= |= ^= <<= >>=` are integer
operations on any variable, and a `for` step accepts all ten.

TYPECHECK — A WARNING, NOT AN ERROR (v6.4.56/.57): a plain `+ - * /` on
an f64/f32 value with a NON-float right operand warns on stderr:

    var a: f64 = f64_from(3);
    var b = a + 1;
    warning:<source>:2:14: f64 arithmetic with a non-f64 right operand

  This is the exact trap where `x + 1` silently means "add the
  denormal whose bits are 1", not "add 1.0". It stays a WARNING because
  an error would break the legal i64-boxed-float idiom (ADR-002). Gated
  on the default-on `CYRIUS_TYPE_CHECK`. The compare-mix warning
  (v6.4.57) suppresses literal 0, so `if (x > 0)` does not fire but
  `if (x > 5)` does.

  f32 GOT ITS OWN WARNING AT 6.6.11 (kind 5). An f32 `+ - * /` takes
  the LOW 32 BITS of its right operand, so an f64 literal or an integer
  there is reinterpreted, not converted — with `x: f32`, `x * 2.0` is 0,
  `x + 1.0` is `x`, and `f32_from(u) + 1.0` drops the 1.0 — and the f32
  arms never checked the right operand while the f64 arm beside them did:

    var x: f32 = f32_from(3.0);
    var y: f32 = x * 2.0;
    warning:<source>:3:25: f32 arithmetic with a non-f32 right operand

  Write `x * f32_from(2.0)`. An f64 literal is NOT narrowed for you.
  Warn-only, like kind 1; `CYRIUS_TYPE_CHECK=0` silences it. The one
  ecosystem site it found at landing (ranga, `one - ganita_f32_min(..)`)
  is a callee declared `: i64` that returns f32 bits — bind the call to
  a `var m: f32` first.

TYPED COPIES (6.6.11). A `var` with no annotation that is a pure COPY of
a declared float takes its type: `var t = p.y;` (an `f64` field, or a
chain `o.i.x`), `var u = y;` (an f64 / f32 local or parameter) and
`var g = G;` (a typed global) make `t`, `u`, `g` floats, so `t + t` is a
float add. Before 6.6.11 the copy stayed i64 and `t + t` added the bit
patterns (only `-t` noticed, as a warning). The rule is deliberately
NARROW — the initialiser must be exactly a name or a field chain, the
v6.5.21 rule `var x = f();` already applied to a declared `: f64`
return. A literal (`var z = 0.0;`), a float builtin (`var s =
f64_sqrt(u);`) and any arithmetic (`var t = p.y * 2.0;`) stay UNTYPED,
so existing `==` / `<` code does not silently become float compares.
Globals in the leading declaration block are judged by the same shape
(`= G;` or `= G.f;`). Across 1,652 ecosystem compiles no output binary
changed.

PARSER NOTE — an intrinsic call on EITHER side of a TERM-tier operator
(`a * f64_from(2)`, `f64_from(2) * a`, `x * f32_from(2.0)`) parses and
computes correctly; it was a parse ERROR until v6.4.83 (re-verified on
cycc 6.6.11). This entry said "cannot flank" until the 6.6.11 refresh —
see field_notes/language/parser_syntax.cyml
`float_bit_intrinsics_cannot_flank_term_operators` (a misnomer kept for
link stability).

Scalar f64 was usable before the named type (literals, f64_add/sub/mul/div,
f64_lt/gt/eq, f64_sqrt/abs/floor, int<->f64 f64_from / f64_to) — v6.2.19 is
the SUGAR letting you write `a + b` instead of `f64_add(a, b)` once `a`/`b`
are `: f64`. (CHANGELOG 6.2.18 / 6.2.19 / 6.4.55 / 6.4.56 / 6.4.57.)

[[entries]]
name = "closures_v637_v638"
description = "Closures: `|params| body` (and `|| body` thunk) as a first-class value; a closure referencing an enclosing local CAPTURES it BY VALUE at construction (heap env object). Invoke via callptr(f, args). Non-capturing closures stay bare fn pointers. Shipped v6.3.7 (non-capturing base) + v6.3.8 (lexical capture). DEFAULT-ON (not gated). PE: non-capturing only."
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    var add = |a, b| a + b;          # two-param closure
    var r   = callptr(add, 20, 22);  # -> 42

    var thunk = || 7;                # zero-param closure (|| is NOT
                                     # logical-or here — at a factor
                                     # position it's an empty-param
                                     # closure; `a || b` still parses
                                     # as short-circuit OR)

    var block = |x| { var t = x * 2; t + 1; };  # { block } body

CAPTURE (v6.3.8) — BY VALUE:
    var base = 100;
    var addbase = |x| x + base;      # captures `base` = 100
    base = 999;                      # does NOT change the closure's copy
    callptr(addbase, 1);             # -> 101, not 1000

    A closure that references a name failing FINDLOCAL in its own scope
    (and that isn't a global/fn) records it as a CAPTURE. A capturing
    closure emits a heap env object `[fn_ptr, cap0, cap1, …]`
    (`alloc((1+ncap)*8)`) — the closure VALUE is that object. So a
    capturing closure REQUIRES `include "lib/alloc.cyr"` + `alloc_init()`
    (ERR_MSG fails loud if alloc is unresolved). A NON-capturing closure
    is detected as such and stays a BARE fn pointer (no heap).

INVOCATION — callptr auto-dispatches:
    `callptr(f, args…)` is the canonical call. When the callee local
    carries the CLOSURE_TYID (0x40000003) var-type tag, callptr loads the
    fn pointer from `[obj]` and passes the object itself as the hidden
    first (env) argument — so the call site is IDENTICAL whether or not
    the closure captured. (A capturing closure fn gains an env parameter
    at arg-reg 0 / local slot 0; user params shift up by one — handled
    transparently.) fncallN also works for the bare-fn-pointer case.

SEMANTICS / IMPLEMENTATION:
    A closure is an anonymous fn emitted INLINE in the enclosing fn.
    v6.3.7 fixed the four bugs that made the scaffolded feature
    non-functional (param passing via stale regalloc, unpatched frame
    size, shared local-table clobbering, regalloc post-pass rewriting the
    inline code → SIGILL) — a fn that emits any closure skips the
    regalloc byte-scan post-pass (`_cur_fn_has_closure`).

PLATFORM: capturing closures run on Windows PE too since v6.4.26 (the
v6.3.8 fail-loud guard `capturing closures not yet supported on Windows
PE` is removed; PE routes them through `ECALLPTR_PE`, env as arg 0 →
rcx). Re-verified 6.6.20 under wine: a closure capturing a local returns
42. Capturing and non-capturing closures run on every target.

CROSS-REF: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
`closure_inline_emit_shares_enclosing_fn_state` (v6.3.7 codegen bugs) +
`closure_lexical_capture_by_value_env_object` (v6.3.8 env object).

[[entries]]
name = "generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310"
description = "Generics via MONOMORPHIZATION: `fn f<T>(a: T): T`, `var x: Box<i32>`, generic structs + fns, scalar + struct type-args, explicit `f<i64>(x)` + inferred receivers. Shipped v6.3.9 (i64 fns) + v6.3.10 (non-i64 + structs + explicit syntax), engine finalized through v6.3.39. DEFAULT-ON as of v6.4.0 (the CYRIUS_MONOMORPH flip); `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0` opts out."
category = "cyrius"
---
STATUS — DEFAULT-ON as of v6.4.0:
    Generics compile WITHOUT any env flag. `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0` is the
    OPT-OUT (previously `=1` was the opt-in). The flip is byte-identical:
    cycc has no generic fns, so flag-on cycc == flag-off cycc (size
    UNCHANGED, 1,024,552 B). The precise per-fn signal that made the flip
    safe is GFTP(S, fi) (generic-fn-type-params; non-zero => generic); the
    flip decoupled monomorph from general inlining (_INLINE_OK) and
    narrowed the v6.3.29 frame-trim to generic fns only. Verified correct
    behind the flag at v6.3.39; v6.4.0 de-risked the default flip.

SYNTAX:
    fn id<T>(x: T): T { return x; }          # generic fn
    fn add<T>(a: T, b: T): T { return a + b; }
    var r = id<i64>(5);                      # explicit type argument
    var s = add<i32>(3, 4);                  # non-i64 instance
    var t = add(3, 4);                       # inferred (i64) receiver

    struct Box<T> { val: T; }                # generic struct
    var b: Box<i32> = Box<i32> { 7 };        # literal names its instance (6.6.12)
    struct Holder<T> { v: T; tag: i64; }
    var h: Holder<Point> = ...;              # struct type-argument
    id<i32>(4);                              # explicit call as a bare statement (6.6.12)

GENERIC STRUCT LITERALS (6.6.12): `Box<Point> { p, 5 }`, `Box<Point> { 1, 2, 5 }`,
`Pair<i32> { 40, 2 }` build the INSTANCE — in a fn, in the leading declaration block
and after the first top-level statement. Before 6.6.12 every one of them was
`undefined variable 'Box'` (the three literal heads committed only on `Name {`). It
is the instance an annotation names, so `var b: Box<Point> = Box<i64> { 1, 2 };` is
refused ("struct literal type does not match the declared type of this variable"),
and `Box { .. }` with no arguments is the base (all-i64) struct. See
struct_literals_generic_heads_and_nested_values_v6612.

EXPLICIT GENERIC CALL AS A STATEMENT (6.6.12): `id<i32>(4);` on its own was
`expected '=', got '<'` (in a fn and at top level) while the inferred `id(4);` and the
expression `var r = id<i32>(4);` worked — PARSE_STMT sent only `IDENT (` to the call
path. It now behaves exactly like the `IDENT (` statement: the result is dropped for
every return class, a `.field` / `.method()` on the result works, `#must_use` warns,
and a postfix `?` on a `Result`-returning generic propagates both halves
(`g<i32>(n, d)?;`). `a < b;` never reaches it (the lookahead needs a generic callee
and `name<..>(`). ⚠ STILL OPEN at 6.6.12 (backlog): the EXPRESSION form
`var v = g<i32>(n, d)?;` on a `Result`-returning generic exits 139 — the expression
side recognises a pair-returning callee only as `name (`, not `name<T>(` (and
`var t, p = g<i32>(n, d);` is refused: "multi-value destructure needs a call on the
right-hand side"). Use the statement form, or the INFERRED call, which reaches the
i64 instance and handles both (`var v = g(n, d)?;`, `var t, p = g(n, d);` — measured
right on 6.6.12).

HOW IT WORKS — instantiate-once:
    In an i64-everywhere language the BASE fn/struct IS its own i64
    instantiation (body emitted once with `T -> i64`). A non-i64 use mints
    `foo$ty` / `Box$ty` into the name pool (`$` separator), with FINDFN /
    FINDSTRUCT as the dedup index; on a miss the specialized instance is
    emitted by RE-INVOKING PARSE_FN_DEF / PARSE_STRUCT_DEF on the base's
    verbatim tokens with a concrete `T -> conc` binding override (full
    fidelity — params, ABI masks, struct args, return, regalloc — for
    free). Explicit `foo<…>(` is disambiguated from `a < b` via a
    non-consuming lookahead gated on the flag + a generic-fn callee.

INFERENCE (6.6.10): a call's T is inferred from the argument in the FIRST
parameter T types, wherever it sits (`fn g<T>(n, p: T)` -> `g(2, p)` binds
from `p`). A struct argument (local, by-value parameter, global, struct-
returning call) binds T to the struct on EVERY call path — expression,
`var r = g(p)`, `r = g(p);`, `b.v = g(p);`, `return g(p);`, an argument, a
struct receive — exactly as `g<Pt>(p)` does. Only a WHOLE argument binds T:
`g(p.y)`, `g(p.x + p.y)` and a `pp: *Pt` pointer parameter infer i64.
Before 6.6.10 only the inline replay inferred a struct (positionally, and it
then re-typed the param i64), so every other path ran the i64 base.

NO SCALAR INSTANCE (6.6.10): a generic that uses a T-typed value (param or
`var q: T` local) as a struct (`p.y`) has only a dead-stub base. A scalar call
(`g(5)`, `g<i64>(5)`, `g<i32>(5)`, tail included), `&g`, and a generic that
forwards its T into one (`outer(5)` with `outer<T>(p: T) { return g(p); }`, in
either definition order) are compile errors naming the fn. They all compiled
and returned 0 before. See field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml
`generic_using_t_as_a_struct_has_no_i64_instance`.

SIGNATURE TYPES (6.6.10): `fn mk<T>(x: T): Box<T>` returns `Box<Pt>` from its
`Pt` instance; `fn f(r: Box<Pt>): Box<Pt>` takes and returns the instance,
`return mk(p);` / `return mk<Pt>(p);` included in the register-pair and retptr
classes (was sized as the base `Box`: `r.v.x` failed to parse). All-i64 args =
the base. An instance's `: T` return with T a struct returns that struct by
value, like a `: Pt` fn (`var q: Pt = id(p)`; it returned the address).

COVERED (x86 + aarch64 + PE + cx): generic fns over i64 + narrow scalars +
struct type-args; generic structs; explicit + inferred receivers; struct
type-arg on a generic fn (`wrap<Point>(pt)`, v6.3.39); multi-field generic
struct return/receive. INTENTIONALLY rejected: a struct type-arg beside a
second type-arg (`Two<Point,i64>`, or two inferred structs, 6.6.10) — on every
call path, struct receives and assignments included.

CROSS-REF: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
`generic_fn_i64_base_and_ret_stash` (v6.3.9) +
`generic_instantiate_once_reinvoke` (v6.3.10 engine).

[[entries]]
name = "async_await_futures_v6311"
description = "async/await: an `async fn` call builds a Future (does NOT run the body); `await` forces it. A plain Future runs its body ONCE (force-once memo, 6.6.10); an async fn that awaits in its body is a stackless COROUTINE (x86 family only). OPT-IN behind CYRIUS_ASYNC=1. Needs lib/alloc.cyr + lib/fnptr.cyr + lib/async.cyr. Shipped v6.3.11; coroutines v6.5.69; memo, arity, fall-off and await-value fixes 6.6.10."
category = "cyrius"
---
STATUS — GATED, default-OFF: behind `CYRIUS_ASYNC=1` in the COMPILER'S
environment (`CYRIUS_ASYNC=1 cyrius build …` passes it on; there is no
command-line flag). Default off → cycc self-host byte-identical; the tokens
are lexed unconditionally but the FEATURE is gated in pass 2.

SYNTAX:
    async fn work(n): i64 { return n * 2; }   # `async` before `fn`
    var fut = work(21);                       # builds a Future — does
                                              # NOT run the body
    var v   = await fut;                      # forces it -> 42
    var v2  = await work(21);                 # await a call directly

PLAIN FUTURES (an async fn with no `await` in its body), every backend:
    The call compiles to a CONSTRUCTOR that allocates
    `[ &f$impl, argc|256, args…, done, value ]` and returns its pointer;
    the body is a hidden `f$impl`. `await fut` = `future_force(fut)`: the
    FIRST force runs the impl (fncallN) and keeps the value, every later
    force answers from it (6.6.10 — before, each await re-ran the body).
    A hand-built `[ fp, argc, args… ]` (no marker) re-runs per force.
    0–8 parameters; 9+ is refused at the declaration, naming the fn
    (6.6.10 — it used to compile and exit 70 at the first force).
    Spawnable with `async_spawn_future`, driven by `async_run`.

COROUTINES (an async fn that awaits in its own body, v6.5.69) — x86_64
Linux, x86_64 macOS, PE only; aarch64 and cx refuse them by name:
    Locals live in a heap frame; the argc word is `1|512` (SELF is the one
    argument, whatever the declared arity — a 0-param coroutine SIGSEGV'd and
    a 9-param one exited 70 before 6.6.10). Each force resumes at the last
    `await` and runs to the next (answering 0) or to the end; a completed
    coroutine answers with its value and runs nothing (6.6.8). Falling off
    the end completes it with 0 and runs its defers (6.6.10 — it hung).
    `await e` INSIDE a coroutine (6.6.10 — it used to yield the suspend
    INDEX): e is evaluated and the coroutine suspends; at the next force
      - e a call to an `async fn`, or a bare variable → forced; `await`
        yields its value, re-suspending while a coroutine Future is still
        pending (pass 1 marks async fns, so a callee defined later counts);
      - anything else (a plain call, e.g. `await async_wait_fd(rt, fd)`,
        the park idiom) → e's own value.
    The test is on the operand's SHAPE; parentheses do not change it
    (`await (inner(a))` forces). A Future reached any other way — a field,
    `vec_get`, an index — yields its POINTER: bind it first
    (`var F = vec_get(futs, i); await F`).
    `await` may sit anywhere in an expression (`t = t + await f`,
    `add(1000, await g())`, `s += await g()`, `store64(p + 8, await g())`):
    the words the expression had pushed before the await are kept in the
    coroutine frame across the suspend (6.6.10 — they were lost, so
    `b + await five()` gave 5 and the store64 form SIGSEGV'd).
    Under the reactor (`async_spawn_future(rt, co(..))` + `async_run(rt)`, or
    `task_join(rt, h)`) a coroutine is driven to its value. One that PARKS
    (`await async_wait_fd(rt, fd)` / `async_wait_writable`) sleeps until its fd
    is ready; one whose `await` did not park (an `await` of a Future, or of a
    call that does not park) stays runnable and resumes on the next step, and
    while one does the Linux / macOS / Windows reactors still poll — without
    blocking — for the fds, timers and deadline sentinels other tasks are
    parked on, so neither side starves (6.6.11). ⚠ Until 6.6.11 every backend's
    `_async_step` marked a task that returned still-READY as DONE, so such a
    coroutine was finished with the 0 its suspend returned (measured: 0 where
    `future_force` gave 41), and this entry told you to force it yourself
    with future_force. That workaround is no longer needed.

REQUIRES: `include "lib/alloc.cyr"` + `lib/fnptr.cyr` + `lib/async.cyr`
(future_force / future_pending / async_spawn_future / async_run). The
constructor and lib/async.cyr ship in LOCKSTEP (the argc markers).
(6.6.11: the constructor lost a redundant leading skip-`jmp` that sat in
no fn's range — DCE's seed pass took it as a root for the NEXT fn, so the
fn defined right after an `async fn` was never reported dead. 5 B less per
async fn on x86.)

DEFERRED: async generic fns, value-form vector params, by-value struct
returns over 8 bytes (all refused by name).

CROSS-REF: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
`async_await_futures_dce_nameread` (the DCE name-read landmine when a
modifier keyword precedes `fn`). Gate: cyrius
tests/gates/frontend/coroutine_fnptr_and_completion.sh.

[[entries]]
name = "reserved_word_class_toknames"
description = "Reserved words are a CLASS, not a short list: 79 builtin/intrinsic names (TOKNAME_BUILTIN in src/common/util.cyr) plus 28 tokens IS_KEYWORD_TOK enumerates itself (26 statement keywords + `f64_sqrt` / `callptr`) = 107 reserved token types at 6.6.20. Since v6.4.77 the parser NAMES the one you hit instead of saying `got unknown`; since 6.6.2 no token is shared, so it names exactly the word you wrote."
category = "cyrius"
---
THE RULE: you cannot use a builtin/intrinsic name as an identifier —
not as a `var`, not as a struct field, not as an fn name. That set is
**79 builtin/intrinsic names** (TOKNAME_BUILTIN: 67 at v6.4.77, 76 at
v6.6.1, 79 since 6.6.13's f64_le/f64_ge/f64_trunc) plus **28 tokens
`IS_KEYWORD_TOK` enumerates separately**: the 26 statement keywords
(`if`, `while`, `fn`, `enum`, `match`, `pub`/`public`, `private`,
`shared`, `in`, `default`, `object`, `stack`, …) and the two intrinsics
renumbered at 6.6.2, `f64_sqrt` (136) and `callptr` (137), which TOKNAME
names, not TOKNAME_BUILTIN = **107 reserved token types** (`pub` and
`public` share one). Derive, never quote:
`sed -n '/^fn TOKNAME_BUILTIN/,/^}/p' src/common/util.cyr | grep -cE 'if \(typ == [0-9]+\)'`
(79) and the same over `IS_KEYWORD_TOK` (28).

THE 79 IN TOKNAME_BUILTIN (families, not a hand-list — read the table):
    syscall
    load8 / load16 / load32 / load64
    store8 / store16 / store32 / store64
    the whole  f64_*  family   (f64_add/sub/mul/div/from/to/eq/lt/gt/
                                le/ge/neg/abs/floor/ceil/round/trunc/
                                sin/cos/exp/ln/atan …; f64_sqrt is
                                reserved too, via IS_KEYWORD_TOK)
    the whole  f64v_* / f32_* / f32v_* / f32v8_* / iv_*  families
    union · defer · secret · async · await · u128
    bitget / bitset / bitclr · ret2 / rethi

THE DIAGNOSTIC (v6.4.77) — it now names the collision:

    $ echo 'var iv_add = 1;' | cycc > /dev/null
    error:<source>:1:5: expected identifier, got reserved keyword 'iv_add'
        (cannot be used as an identifier; rename the variable/field/fn)
        var iv_add = 1;
            ^

Before v6.4.77, `IS_KEYWORD_TOK` and `TOKNAME` both stopped at token
111, so all 67 of these fell through to `expected identifier, got
unknown` — a syntactically clean identifier called "unknown". It cost
the **hisab** consumer its whole 312-assertion suite, found only by
bisection. `IS_KEYWORD_TOK` now derives its BUILTIN half from
`TOKNAME_BUILTIN`, so for the 79 builtin/intrinsic names the "is it
reserved?" set and the "what is it called?" set cannot drift. The other
28 tokens (the statement keywords plus `f64_sqrt` and `callptr`) are
still enumerated in `IS_KEYWORD_TOK` itself, separately from `TOKNAME`,
and those two lists CAN drift — util.cyr's own comment on
`IS_KEYWORD_TOK` says so ("Adding to one and not the other is exactly
the drift that note claims is impossible").

NO TOKEN IS SHARED SINCE 6.6.2: `f64_sqrt` is token 136 and `callptr`
137. They were 79 and 111, shared with `object` / `stack`, so a
collision used to print both spellings (and before v6.4.77 named the
WRONG one). Every collision now names the exact word: `var f64_sqrt =
1;` -> `reserved keyword 'f64_sqrt'` (re-verified 6.6.20, as are
`object`, `callptr`, `stack`). The one double SPELLING left is `pub` /
`public`, one token, printed `'pub'/'public'`.

DON'T EXTEND A LIST HERE. Earlier docs named three or four of these
("`secret`, `pub`, `shared` are reserved") — a partial observation
written down as a language rule. If you need to know whether a name is
reserved, either read `TOKNAME_BUILTIN` or just compile
`var <name> = 1;` and read the error. RENAME (`iv_add` → `ivadd`,
`f64_sqrt` → `fsqrt`) is the only fix; there is no escape syntax.

[[entries]]
name = "diagnostics_column_caret_multierror"
description = "Compiler diagnostics carry line:COLUMN + a source excerpt with a caret (v6.4.60), report MANY errors per compile with panic-mode recovery instead of stopping at the first (v6.4.62), emit NO output file when any error fired, and never hang on hostile input."
category = "cyrius"
---
WHAT AN ERROR LOOKS LIKE NOW (v6.4.60 + v6.4.62):

    error:<source>:1:5: expected identifier, got reserved keyword 'iv_add'
        (cannot be used as an identifier; rename the variable/field/fn)
        var iv_add = 1;
            ^

  `file:LINE:COLUMN`, then the source line, then a caret under the
  offending token. Warnings carry the same header:

    warning:<source>:2:14: f64 arithmetic with a non-f64 right operand

COLUMN SEMANTICS (v6.4.60): the column is the CURRENT parse token. For a
backward-looking check ("expected `;`") that is the token AFTER the one
you probably want — read the caret, not the number, in those cases. Tabs
count as ONE column. Implemented with ZERO new heap: the token byte
offset is packed into the HIGH 32 bits of the existing `tok_lines` entry.

MANY ERRORS PER COMPILE (v6.4.62): a syntax error no longer stops the
compile. The parser enters PANIC MODE, skips to the next statement
boundary, and keeps going, so one run reports the whole batch. Scope is
the `ERR_EXPECT` syntax class (346 sites); ~25 inline fatal errors still
fail fast on the first hit.

THREE GUARANTEES THAT COME WITH IT:
  1. **No output on error.** If ANY error fired, the compiler writes no
     binary (all forks, including cx). You can never accidentally run a
     stale/partial artifact — a 0-byte file is the failure signature.
  2. **It never hangs.** A `_had_error`-gated watchdog inside `PEEKT`
     aborts cleanly if the token index stalls past a bound far above any
     legal recursive-descent depth. On VALID input none of this
     machinery ever runs.
  3. **Truncated input terminates.** `PEEKT` clamps to EOF past the
     token count (v6.4.78) — a truncated file used to cascade 166,670
     error lines; it now prints 5.

PRACTICAL: pipe stderr through `head` when bringing up a new file — you
will get the real first error plus its neighbours, not one error per
edit-compile round trip.

[[entries]]
name = "visibility_public_private_v650"
description = "private / public — file-scoped visibility for fns and global vars (v6.5.0; scoped at symbol insertion since v6.5.38; enforced on every resolution path incl. &fn, methods, struct receives since v6.6.4; forward references enforced and the whole file covered regardless of where the marker sits since v6.6.5)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:

    private                        # THIS FILE is private-by-default
    fn helper(): i64 { return 7; } # file-private
    var _state = 0;                # file-private (vars too)
    public fn api(): i64 {         # re-exposed
        return helper();           # in-file use is unrestricted
    }
    public var CONFIG = 7;

`pub` is a synonym for `public` — the same lexer token (73).

THE OPT-IN RULE — this is the part to internalise. A file with NO `private`
declaration behaves EXACTLY as it did before v6.5.0. Visibility is not
retrofitted onto the tree; you turn it on one file at a time. That is why the
entire ecosystem kept compiling on adoption day with a blast radius of zero.

SCOPE OF `private`: the FILE the keyword appears in. NOT the files it includes,
and NOT the file that includes it. Cyrius includes are textual, so this is the
distinction to be careful about — the boundary is the ORIGIN file each fn/var
was parsed from, tracked per-item, not the expanded translation unit.

VIOLATIONS ARE HARD ERRORS (not warnings):

    error:main.cyr:12:9: 'helper' is private to lib/thing.cyr
            var x = helper();
                ^

Reported through the multi-error path (v6.4.62), so ONE compile lists every
violation. Enforcement covers ordinary calls, TAIL calls, operator-overload
dispatch, and global-var reads — the var path enforces inside `FINDVAR`, which
is the single resolver every global read reaches.

PRIVATE SYMBOLS ARE SCOPED AT INSERTION (v6.5.38) — THE HALF THAT WAS MISSING.
Two files may each define a private `_helper`; each file's calls bind to its
OWN, and neither can capture the other's. A same-named PUBLIC fn stays
reachable from everywhere else.

    # a.cyr                            # b.cyr
    private                            fn _helper(x) { return 7; }
    fn _helper(x) { return 1; }        fn b_entry() { return _helper(0); }  # 7
    public fn a_entry() {
        return _helper(0);             # 1 — always a.cyr's own
    }

⛔ v6.5.0 THROUGH v6.5.37 DID NOT DO THIS, and the failure was silent. The fn
table held ONE entry per NAME, so b.cyr's definition overwrote a.cyr's —
INCLUDING for a.cyr's own internal calls — under a `duplicate fn ... last
definition wins` warning that reads as benign shadowing. `private` on BOTH
sides did not help: visibility was enforced only where a name was USED, layered
on an unchanged global symbol table. Filed from owl 1.4.7, where vyakarana and
sankoch each define a private `_stream_grow` that disagree about arity, buffer
offsets AND RETURN POLARITY — a wrong binding there does not fault, it reports
every successful buffer grow as a FAILURE, inside a tokenizer, on file content.
v6.5.37 closed only the arity-differing case (as a hard error); v6.5.38 closed
the same-arity half, which is the dangerous one precisely because no check can
see its shape.

MECHANISM: definitions SPLIT into separate symbols when a private one is
involved and the origin files differ. Resolution then prefers (1) a definition
private to the caller's own file, (2) the first non-private one, (3) the first
match — arm 3 exists so `_vis_check` still produces the precise "is private to
its file" error instead of a misleading "undefined function". The split is
flagged per-fn, so a name with one definition costs nothing extra to resolve.

UNCHANGED ON PURPOSE: duplicates between two NON-private files still only warn,
and two definitions in ONE file are still a duplicate (a redefinition of one
symbol, not a collision between two files).

VISIBILITY ALSO CONTROLS LINKAGE: a private fn is omitted from the exported
symbol table, so it disappears from `.dynstr` / `nm` and from
`cyrius api-surface`. Measured on adoption of `lib/regex.cyr`: exported
symbols 24 -> 14. The API surface a consumer sees is now the API surface you
declared, rather than every non-underscore fn in the file.

RELATION TO THE `_` PREFIX: the leading-underscore convention still suppresses
export and is unchanged. `private` is the enforced mechanism; `_` remains a
naming convention. They compose — a `_`-prefixed fn in a `private` file is
private for both reasons.

RESERVED WORDS: `public`, `pub` and `private` cannot be used as identifiers.
They are part of the `TOKNAME_BUILTIN` reserved CLASS (see the reserved-word
entry) — do not treat this as a three-name list.

EVERY RESOLUTION PATH, NOT JUST THE DIRECT CALL (v6.6.4): the boundary is
enforced per RESOLUTION SITE (fn side) — `_vis_check` is one helper, but each
path that resolves a user identifier through FINDFN and emits its own
call/fixup must call it. v6.5.0 wired three (ordinary call, tail call,
operator overloading). hisab found `&_helper` callable from any file through
callptr/fncallN; the sweep found EIGHT unchecked paths: `&fn`, `s.method()`,
the retptr (asv) and pair (asp) struct receives (`var s: T = f()` and the
inferred `var s = f()`), and four Win64-only SIMD receive/return paths. All
eleven now check; `grep -n '_vis_check(' src/frontend/` is the list. Two
same-class stamp gaps closed with it: a monomorphized instance inherits its
BASE's visibility (a `public fn pgen<T>` in a private file used to refuse
`pgen<i32>(42)` from another file while accepting `pgen(42)`), and top-level
ARRAYS are stamped (PARSE_GVAR_ARR returned before the "one point every global
var reaches", so `var _buf[N]` in a private file was never private). A
field access on a private global reports ONCE per line (it resolved its base
twice). Enum constants and type names carry no visibility — always public.
THE MARKER IS SCOPED TO ITS ITEM (v6.6.4): `_TL_VIS` arms `_pub_pending` only
when the next token can carry visibility — `var`/`secret`, `fn`/`async`, or a
fn-attribute directive (`_PUB_CAN_ARM`, a POSITIVE list). Before, it armed
unconditionally and only fn/var definitions cleared it, so `public enum E {..}`
(and bare `public struct` / `union` / `impl` / `use`, and `public var a[N]`)
in a private file silently re-exposed the NEXT fn or var — hisab's `_ad_pow`,
one leak among 457 probes (456 refused). Pass 2 had the same shape one level deeper: its
inline `var` skip re-armed at `public var` and the next PASS-2-ONLY definition
(an impl's first method, the first relaxed-ordering fn after top-level code)
inherited it — PARSE_IMPL and that path now clear a stale marker on entry.
`public impl` therefore marks NO method; per-method `public fn` is the form.
`public var a, b = f();` covers every name (it stamped b private). Derived
CODECS inherit the type's `public` as the v6.6.3 accessors did — closing the
leak would otherwise have made a public type's `T_to_json` unreachable — and
the once-per-unit `_cy_enum_name_eq` helper is emitted public (a second
private file's enum codec was refused when the first file was private).
Globals on the PARSE_PROG path (after the first top-level statement) are now
stamped; they were never private. Gate:
tests/gates/frontend/public_marker_scoped_to_its_item.sh (56 rows; the 6.6.3
compiler reds 30). PARSE_PROG itself clears a stale marker on entry too — the
first declaration after top-level code used to consume one left by a
`public var` above it.

FORWARD REFERENCES ARE CHECKED, AND THE MARKER'S POSITION NO LONGER MATTERS
(v6.6.5) — the last residual, and it was four holes rather than one. The
enforcement above is only as good as what PASS 1 STAMPED, and pass 1 did not
stamp: impl methods (all seven forks brace-skipped impl bodies), `mod`-scoped
fns (a `GMOD == 0` guard), every fn after the FIRST TOP-LEVEL STATEMENT (pass 1
stops there; PARSE_PROG keeps defining fns), and anything above the `private`
line (the flip was recorded as pass 1 walked past the marker, so an earlier
definition was prescanned while the file still read public — and a global above
the line was never private in either order). An unstamped callee has no flag
and no owner, so `_vis_check` returned at its FIRST test and a call that merely
came earlier in the concatenated stream reached it from any file.

⭐ THE SAME ROOT RAN THE OTHER WAY, IN ORDINARY INCLUDE ORDER, which is why
this is not a corner case: pass 1 kept one entry per NAME, so a second file's
same-named helper overwrote the first file's owner, arity and private bit
before either body was parsed — FALSE `is private to its file` and FALSE
`expects N arguments` on legal programs, and the two-file `_helper` example
above REFUSED when b.cyr was included first (the private bit was only ever set,
never cleared, so the PUBLIC fn inherited it). And with no pass-1 signature a
forward call passing a >8-byte struct took the mask-0 ABI: silent SIGSEGV on
x86, aarch64 and PE.

Pass 1 now stamps every definition kind and is the AUTHORITY: the file-level
mark happens once before either pass, the v6.5.38 identity rule runs in BOTH
passes from one place (so a same-named definition splits in pass 1, before any
forward call can bind), PARSE_FN_DEF finds the entry pass 1 gave THIS definition
by its own `fn` token and takes its bit, and the bit is CLEARED for a public
definition. Under it, `_vis_check` defers a reference whose callee has no owner
and no body and re-judges it at the end of PARSE_PROG — fail-closed-later, so a
future unstamped definition kind is reported rather than let through.
Gates: tests/gates/frontend/visibility_private.sh (per-path axes, now in BOTH
include orders), tests/gates/frontend/private_forward_reference.sh (7-fork
static parity + a forward-vs-backward refusal differential) and
tests/tcyr/crossos/forward_ref_abi_binding.tcyr (the ABI half, real hardware).

NO PER-ITEM `private`: `private fn h()` is a hard error naming the file-level
form (v6.5.56). ⚠ Until v6.6.5 it printed TWICE and privatised the file anyway,
so a legitimate sibling fn was then reported private at its caller.

[[entries]]
name = "threads_detached_and_is_done_v658"
description = "lib/thread.cyr — thread_create_detached / thread_is_done (v6.5.8), and why sys_exit_group (v6.5.6) is the epilogue once any thread exists"
category = "cyrius"
---
== THE EPILOGUE COMES FIRST (v6.5.6) ==

`sys_exit` is exit(2) — it ends the CALLING THREAD, not the process. A
program that has spawned any worker and exits through the idiomatic
`syscall(SYS_EXIT, r)` does not terminate: the main thread dies, the
workers keep running, and the process hangs with no diagnostic. Measured,
one token apart:

    syscall(SYS_EXIT, r);      # exit 124 (hung)
    sys_exit_group(r);         # exit 42

`sys_exit_group` is defined on all three syscall peer families (Linux
common routes SYS_EXIT_GROUP; Windows routes SYS_EXIT because
ExitProcess already ends every thread; agnos routes SYS_EXIT because `#0`
tears down the whole proc). `cyrius init` shipped the old epilogue in
seven templates — it only bites once a program grows its FIRST thread,
long after the epilogue was written, which is why nothing flagged it.

== FIRE-AND-FORGET WORKERS (v6.5.8) ==

    var ok = thread_create_detached(&worker, arg);   # 1 = spawned, 0 = failed

No handle is allocated at all: CLONE_PARENT_SETTID /
CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID exist so a joiner can wait on the tid word, and
nobody waits here, so both flags come off. The child unmaps its own
stack in the trampoline tail (musl's `__unmapself` shape), and the TLS
block is carved from the TOP of that same mapping so ONE munmap frees
stack and TLS together.

Measured: 100 unjoined threads leaked **210,124,800 B** of VA — exactly
100 × 2,101,248 — now **0**. Freeing only the 2 MiB and leaving the 4 KB
TLS behind would have been the same half-fix shape that cost v6.5.7 seven
found-by-ports defects.

== NON-BLOCKING COMPLETION PROBE (v6.5.8) ==

    var t = thread_create(&worker, arg);
    while (thread_is_done(t) == 0) { ... keep serving ... }
    thread_join(t);

`thread_is_done(t)` returns 1 = done, 0 = still running, -1 on a null
handle. It reads the same CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID slot `thread_join`'s loop
already tests, so it adds no state. It exists because `thread_join`
blocks unconditionally, which made a reaper — "collect whichever workers
have finished, keep serving the rest" — impossible to write without a
detached thread plus a side channel.

⛔ VALID ONLY BEFORE `thread_join`. Join CONSUMES the handle — on Windows
it CloseHandle()s it — so a post-join query inspects a closed handle and
reports "not done". Caught on real cass hardware by a test that asserted
the wrong contract. A detached thread has no handle by design, so it
cannot be probed this way.

== THE JOIN DEADLOCK THAT PROMPTED ALL OF THIS ==

`thread_join` used to read the tid word TWICE per iteration — once for
the loop condition, once as the `FUTEX_WAIT` expected value — under a
comment asserting the two were "in lockstep". They are the opposite: a
worker exiting between the loads let the kernel's one-shot
`CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` wake fire with nobody parked, after which the
second load returned 0 and `FUTEX_WAIT(t, 0)` parked on an address that
would never be woken again. Permanent, silent, log-less; ~1 join in 150k;
SIGKILL the only recovery. Fixed v6.5.8 — one load now feeds both uses,
so the joiner can only park on a tid it positively observed live.

Gates: `tests/gates/concurrency/thread_join_single_load.sh`, plus the
`tests/tcyr/crossos/` corpus on real hardware (that corpus was selected by a
`vr01_` filename prefix until v6.5.11; it is the DIRECTORY now).

[[entries]]
name = "darwin_concurrency_syscall_wrappers_v6543"
description = "sys_bsdthread_* and sys_ulock_* — Darwin's threading and blocking primitives, routed on both Mach-O backends (v6.5.43)"
category = "cyrius"
---
macOS has neither `clone` nor `futex`. Real threading on Darwin is
`bsdthread_create` / `bsdthread_terminate` / `bsdthread_register`, and blocking
is `__ulock_wait` / `__ulock_wake`. v6.5.43 routes all five on BOTH Mach-O
backends and adds the peer wrappers:

    fn sys_bsdthread_register(threadstart, wqthread, flags,
                              stack_addr_hint, targetconc, dq_offset): i64
    fn sys_bsdthread_create(func, func_arg, stack_addr, pthread, flags): i64
    fn sys_bsdthread_terminate(stackaddr, freesize, port, sem): i64
    fn sys_ulock_wait(operation, addr, value, timeout_us): i64
    fn sys_ulock_wake(operation, addr, wake_value): i64

Source numbers are the v6.5.7 private-alias band (>= 1000, renumbered by
ESYSXLAT / EMACHO_SYSXLAT, spelled `1000 + the native number` so the alias
documents itself): 1360 -> 360, 1361 -> 361, 1366 -> 366, 1515 -> 515,
1516 -> 516. `UL_COMPARE_AND_WAIT` is operation 1; `ULF_WAKE_ALL` is 0x100;
`sys_ulock_wait`'s timeout is MICROSECONDS, 0 meaning forever.

⛔ THESE DO NOT MAKE A THREAD ON THEIR OWN. `bsdthread_register` must run once
before any `bsdthread_create`, and the created thread enters the REGISTERED
TRAMPOLINE with a Darwin-specific register layout — not the user fn. Phase 1
(v6.5.43) is routing and reachability only; the trampoline, stack mmap, real
create/join and the `__ulock` mutex are phase 2.

⚠ WHERE THEY LIVE, which is not where you would guess. `lib/syscalls.cyr`
selects `lib/syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr` for aarch64-Linux AND for arm64-macOS;
`lib/syscalls_macos.cyr` is x86-macOS ONLY. So the arm64-macOS wrappers live in
the file named for Linux, guarded by `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS`, and real
aarch64-Linux — which has futex and clone — is untouched. That surprise is why
the first `kqueue` pass built on Intel-Mac and failed on ecb with
`undefined function sys_kqueue`.

⚠ `stack` IS A RESERVED KEYWORD, so `sys_bsdthread_create`'s third parameter is
`stack_addr`. See TOKNAME_BUILTIN — reserved words are a CLASS, not a list.

⚠ ARITIES COME FROM DARWIN'S `syscalls.master` and phase 2 is what confirms them
by execution. `bsdthread_register` takes 6 args in older SDKs and 7 in newer
ones (a trailing `tsd_offset`); the 6-arg form is used because cyrius `syscall()`
carries at most 6.

[[entries]]
name = "threads_concurrent_capability_v6544"
description = "THREADS_CONCURRENT — ask whether thread_create really produces a second thread, instead of assuming per target (v6.5.44)"
category = "cyrius"
---
Every cyrius target exposes the same threading surface (`thread_create` / `thread_join` /
`thread_create_detached` / `thread_is_done` / `gettid` / the `chan_*` family), but not every
target runs bodies on a SECOND thread. Two peers (x86-macOS, agnos) run them INLINE at `thread_create` —
correct results, serially. `THREADS_CONCURRENT` says which you have:

    1  Linux (real clone(2) threads)
    1  arm64-macOS  (real Darwin threads via __got[5] pthread_create, v6.5.44)
    1  Windows      (real CreateThread threads since v6.0.61; the flag read 0 until 6.6.16)
    0  x86-macOS    (static LC_UNIXTHREAD binary: no libSystem, no __got)
    0  agnos

For the channel, ask `CHAN_BLOCKING` (6.6.16, `chan_blocking_capability_v6616`): real threads
do not imply a blocking channel, and arm64 macOS and Windows had the first without the second
through 6.6.15.

Use it to guard anything whose CORRECTNESS depends on two things running at once — a
rendezvous, a producer/consumer handoff, a benchmark that claims parallel speedup:

    if (THREADS_CONCURRENT == 1) {
        # ... assertions that an inline backend cannot satisfy ...
    }

⚠ DO NOT GUARD ON THE TARGET NAME INSTEAD. A capability guard starts asserting the day a peer
becomes real; a `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS` guard waits for a human to remember to remove it.
That is not hypothetical — macOS bodies ran inline from v6.5.11 to v6.5.43 and the whole
cross-host concurrency corpus was green throughout, because every assertion in it ("the worker
ran", "join returned 0", "the count is exact", "the mutex excluded") is honestly satisfiable by
an inline call. See the field note `a_corpus_that_cannot_tell_real_threads_from_inline_calls`.

⚠ A BACKEND CAN ALSO UNDER-REPORT (6.6.16 — the mirror image). Windows had real threads from
v6.0.61 but declared 0 from v6.5.44 to 6.6.15, and the "honesty" gate
(`macos_arm64_real_threads.sh` axis 6) hard-coded it in its serial list, so the gate ENFORCED
the wrong value. An honesty gate asserts each backend's value from its mechanism (clone /
pthread_create / CreateThread -> 1, inline `fncall1` -> 0), never from a frozen list.

⚠ A `0` PEER IS NOT BROKEN. Bodies run and results are correct; only the parallelism is
absent. Code that uses threads for THROUGHPUT still works there, just serially — so guard on
this only where concurrency is a correctness precondition, not everywhere threads appear.

[[entries]]
name = "chan_blocking_capability_v6616"
description = "CHAN_BLOCKING — ask whether chan_recv / chan_send block, instead of assuming it from the target or from THREADS_CONCURRENT (v6.6.16)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["threads", "channels", "chan", "capability", "macos", "windows", "v6.6.16"]
---
`THREADS_CONCURRENT` says whether `thread_create` starts a second thread. It does NOT say whether
the channel waits. Through 6.6.15 the two disagreed on arm64 macOS and Windows: real threads, but
a `chan_recv` that answered 0 on an empty channel and a `chan_send` that failed on a full one.
`CHAN_BLOCKING` answers the channel question, and every thread peer exports it:

    1  Linux        futex-parked ring
    1  arm64-macOS  __ulock-parked ring (6.6.16)
    1  Windows      ring whose waiters block on an I/O completion port (6.6.16)
    0  x86-macOS    serial ring: chan_recv answers 0 when empty, chan_send -1 when full
    0  agnos        serial ring, the same
    0  cx           one guest thread: a wait that only another thread could end spins

On 1 the ring is locked and shared safely by any number of producer and consumer threads
(MPMC — the Linux ring deadlocked with several producers AND consumers until 6.6.16, see
field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml `one_futex_word_two_kinds_of_waiter_wake_one_strands_the_other_kind`).
chan_recv blocks until a value arrives and returns 0 once the channel is closed AND empty.
chan_send blocks while the ring is full and returns -1 once the channel is closed.
chan_try_send / chan_try_recv / chan_close keep one contract on every target.

A pool of workers that loops on `chan_recv` and treats 0 as "closed" needs CHAN_BLOCKING == 1.
Off it, serve inline:

    if (CHAN_BLOCKING == 1) {
        # spawn workers; each loops `v = chan_recv(ch); if (v == 0) break; ...`
    }
    if (CHAN_BLOCKING == 0) {
        # serve on this thread — a worker's first chan_recv would answer 0 at once
    }

⚠ DO NOT KEY THIS ON THE TARGET NAME, and do not key it on THREADS_CONCURRENT. sandhi 1.10.7
keyed its pooled server on CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX because no capability existed. A target guard
stays wrong after the peer gains the behaviour. THREADS_CONCURRENT was 1 on arm64 macOS while its
channel did not block, so it was the wrong question.

[[entries]]
name = "stack_enum_unboxed_payload_variants_v6555"
description = "enum Name: stack — payload variants that return (tag, payload) in registers instead of a 16-byte heap box (v6.5.55)"
category = "cyrius"
---
A payload-carrying enum variant normally **allocates**. `Ok(42)` is a 16-byte box from the
global bump allocator — tag at `+0`, payload at `+8` — and the only reclaim is `alloc_reset()`,
which invalidates every pointer the allocator has ever returned, so a long-running server can
never call it. 100 `sock_send` calls grow the heap 1600 bytes, permanently.

`: stack` on the declaration opts that variant family out:

```
enum Res: stack { Ok(v); Err(e); }

fn parse(x): i64 {
    if (x > 0) { return Ok(x * 2); }
    return Err(0 - x);
}

fn use(): i64 {
    var tag, val = parse(21);    # zero allocation
    if (tag == 0) { return val; }
    return 0 - val;
}
```

The constructor hands back `(tag, payload)` in the multi-return register pair — rax/rdx on
SysV and Win64, x0/x2 on aarch64 — and you read it with the destructuring bind the language
already had.

RULES, all enforced or diagnosed:
- **Zero or one field per payload variant.** The pair holds a tag and one value; `Pair(a, b)`
  in a `: stack` enum is a compile error (`stack enum variant takes 0 or 1 fields`), not a
  silently dropped field. A **nullary** variant — `None()` — carries no payload, so it needs no
  second register: it returns its tag alone and binds to a single variable. (Until v6.5.67 the
  rule read "exactly 1", which refused `enum Option: stack { None(); Some(v); }` outright.)
- **Bare variants are unchanged** — still integer constants, still sharing the discriminant
  numbering with the payload variants.
- **The destructuring bind works anywhere** (top level since v6.6.0).
- **A pair must be bound as a pair.** `var r = f();` (v6.5.67), `r = f();` and `store64(&slot, f());`
  (v6.6.0) are refused: *"a `: stack` enum returns two values — bind both: `var tag, val = f();`"*. The
  check follows the value through `return`, so a forwarding wrapper is caught too. It is a fn-body check: a
  top-level `var v = f();` is not refused yet (roadmap backlog).
- **`?` propagates the pair** (v6.6.0), in expression position and as a statement; the Err path re-emits
  both halves. (v6.5.67 to 6.6.0 it was a diagnostic; before that it read the tag as a pointer.)
- **`f` is any call spelling** (v6.6.16): `f(..)`, an explicit generic `g<T>(..)` and a method `p.m(..)`
  behave the same for all three rules above. Before 6.6.16 the generic and method spellings were not
  recognised: `?` crashed (rc 139), the single binds dropped the payload silently, and their destructure was
  "multi-value destructure needs a call" (field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml
  `pair_callee_any_spelling_6_6_16`).
- **A `return f(..)` inside a closure body is the closure's** (v6.6.16): it no longer marks the enclosing
  fn as a pair fn (`closure_returns_belong_to_the_closure_since_6_6_16`).

⚠ **OPT-IN, AND THE DEFAULT DELIBERATELY DID NOT CHANGE.** The boxed layout is not an internal
detail; it is the documented representation. `?` reads `+8`, `lib/result.cyr`'s `is_ok` /
`result_unwrap` read `+0` and `+8`, and roughly 300 sites across the ecosystem do the same by
hand. A register pair read as a pointer is a plausible-looking address, so flipping the default
would have broken all of them SILENTLY.

📎 `stack` is not a new keyword — it is token 111, reserved since v5.5.36 for
`stack var buf[N]` ("lives on the call stack rather than being hoisted"), which is the same
meaning one level up. Introducing a `value` keyword instead would have broken any program using
`value` as an identifier.

[[entries]]
name = "inline_directive_v6563"
description = "`#inline` marks the next fn for the inline-replay path — opt-in, per function, and it WARNS when it cannot be honoured"
category = "cyrius"
---
`#inline` (v6.5.63) requests that the next `fn` definition be inlined at its call sites:

    #inline
    fn Pt_x(p) { return load64(p + 0); }

    #inline
    fn addk(a, b) { return a + b + 7; }

⚠ BEFORE v6.5.63 THIS DIRECTIVE DID NOT EXIST. `#` opens a comment in cyrius, so `#inline`
lexed as a comment and did nothing — silently. Consumers were already writing it: svara
carried four markers in `src/formant.cyr`, and its own `src/lod.cyr` recorded measuring one
at "+0.7% -- noise" — a measurement of an optimisation that was never there. If you are on
6.5.62 or earlier, your `#inline` markers are inert.

WHEN IT PAYS. Small, flat, hot functions — above all generated accessors. Measured on an
8-getter loop: 20.84 ms -> 6.04 ms, a 3.45x speedup, for +192 bytes of `.text`.

WHAT QUALIFIES. The request is honoured only for a function that is:
  - at most 2 parameters,
  - at most 32 body tokens,
  - free of control flow (`if` / `while` / `for`) and of `var` declarations,
  - free of struct/aggregate parameters (SIMD vector params are fine).

WHEN IT CANNOT BE HONOURED, YOU GET A WARNING naming the constraint:

    warning: #inline ignored: fn takes more than 2 parameters
    warning: #inline ignored: body exceeds 32 tokens
    warning: #inline ignored: struct/aggregate parameter
    warning: #inline ignored: body has control flow or a var declaration

That diagnostic is half the feature. A directive that silently does nothing is the exact
defect `#inline` was added to end, so a request that cannot be met says so rather than
quietly reverting to a call. A warning never changes behaviour: the function is still called
and still returns the right answer.

⚠ THE ARITY LIMIT IS STRUCTURAL, not a tunable. The inline record packs two 32-bit parameter
name offsets into one 64-bit word, so a third parameter has nowhere to go. Restructure to two
parameters (or a pointer) if you need the inline.

⚠ GENERAL INLINING IS NOT AVAILABLE. `_INLINE_OK` is 0 on every backend. Two source comments
justified that with "+25.6 % on cycc at v1.11.3" for about fifteen majors; re-measured at
v6.5.63 that figure is unquotable, because a compiler built with `_INLINE_OK = 1` does not
compile at all — it dies with "function exceeds 1023 jump targets (x86 LASE cap)". `#inline`
is the supported route, and being per-function it cannot drive any one function toward that
cap the way whole-program inlining does.

RELATED: `#naked`, `#regalloc`, `#alloc` follow the same "attribute on the next fn" shape.


[[entries]]
name = "compile_time_assert_v6610"
description = "`#assert A OP B, \"msg\";` — a compile-time check over literals, sizeof(T) and enum constants (bare or qualified since v6.6.10)"
category = "cyrius"
---
`#assert` checks a compile-time fact and stops the build when it does not hold:

    enum Wire { HDR = 16; }
    struct Hdr { magic; len; }
    #assert sizeof(Hdr) == Wire.HDR, "Hdr must match the wire header";

Add a third field to `Hdr` and the build stops (exit 1, no output):

    error:<source>:3:34: #assert failed: Hdr must match the wire header

GRAMMAR — `#assert ATOM [OP ATOM] [, "message"];`
  - ATOM is ONE of: an integer literal, `sizeof(T)` (a scalar name or a struct), or an
    enum constant spelled `NAME` or `Enum.NAME`.
  - OP is `== != < > <= >=`; a lone atom asserts non-zero.
  - There is NO arithmetic inside an `#assert`; precompute the constant as its own enum
    variant. Only `,`, the message string, `;` or the end of the line may follow the
    operands, and anything else
    is refused: `#assert: expected `,` or `;` after the operands (no arithmetic inside
    #assert)`. ⚠ Before that check (v6.6.10) the rest of the line was skipped UNCHECKED, so
    `#assert E.EB * 2 == 9;` evaluated `E.EB` alone and a FALSE assertion compiled clean.
  - It works at top level (a declaration-phase directive — structs, enums and fns may follow
    it, wrapped or on one line) and inside a fn body. It emits no code.

⚠ BEFORE v6.6.10 ENUM CONSTANTS WERE REFUSED. `#assert EB == 4` and `#assert E.EB == 4`
failed with "expected number or sizeof()" on every backend, because #assert's evaluator
predates the enum-constant fold. The same release taught BOTH array-size parsers the qualified
form: `var buf[E.EB]`, `var a: i64[E.EB]` and the fn-local `var b[E.EB]` were refused while
`var buf[EB]` and `syscall(60, E.EB)` compiled. All three now go through one recogniser
(`_enum_atom_idx`, parse.cyr), so they cannot drift again.

⚠ `sizeof` MUST BE THE WHOLE WORD. The recogniser compared a 6-byte prefix, so
`#assert sizeofzz(P) == 16` compiled as if it said `sizeof` (v6.6.10 compares 7 bytes,
including the terminator). So must its TYPE (6.6.11): `#assert sizeof(i16v8) == 2` used to
PASS because the scalar ladder matched the `i16` prefix; `i16v8` and `i8zz` are now
`sizeof: unknown type` (see `sizeof_operator`).

⚠ `Enum.NAME` MUST NAME NAME'S OWN ENUM (6.6.11). `#assert Nope.EB == 2` is refused with
`'Nope' is not an enum`, and `E2.EB` with `'EB' is not a variant of 'E2'` — in #assert, in
array sizes and in expressions alike (one resolver; see `enum_namespacing`). Before 6.6.11 the
qualifier was ignored and any base compiled.

⚠ AN `#assert` WITH NO `;` ENDS AT THE END OF ITS LINE (6.6.11). It took the line of the
token at the cursor — already the NEXT line's — so

    fn f() {
        #assert 1 == 1
        return 42;
    }

dropped `return 42;` and f returned garbage, rc 0, on every backend. The message may still
wrap onto the next line (`#assert X,` then `"msg";` below it): a string at the cursor
continues the assert.

⚠ A FAILING `#assert` NO LONGER STOPS THE COMPILE ON THE SPOT (6.6.11). It exited during the
declaration phase, so an earlier syntax error in the same file (`var x = ;` above a failing
top-level assert) was never reported. Now each failing assert records its error and the parse
continues: every failing assert and every other error is printed, and no binary is written.
An assert inside an `#inline` body replayed per call reports once.

⚠ ONE MISTAKE, ONE ERROR. A bad operand is reported by name — "expected a number, sizeof(T)
or an enum constant" — and no longer followed by a message-less "#assert failed" for the same
mistake.

⚠ A VARIABLE IS NOT A CONSTANT. `var n = 4; #assert n == 4;` is refused, and a base that is a
variable (`#assert p.x == 0`) is a field access, not an enum variant — the same rule the
expression parser applies to `Enum.VARIANT`.

RELATED: `enum` constants as array sizes (`var buf[Sz.BUF]`), `sizeof`, and
`tests/gates/frontend/assert_enum_constants.sh`.


[[entries]]
name = "string_literal_escapes_and_line_accounting_v6611"
description = "String-literal escapes are a CLOSED list: since cycc 6.6.11 any other byte after `\\` is `unknown string escape`; `\\` + newline KEEPS the newline (it is not a C line-splice); and a newline inside a string counts as a source line, so diagnostics after a multi-line string name the right line and file. Since 6.6.15 identical literals share storage and a literal differing in any byte (an embedded NUL included) never does"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE WHOLE ESCAPE LIST for string literals (char literals have their own, shorter ladder):

    \n  \r  \t  \0          LF, CR, TAB, NUL
    \\  \"  \'              backslash, double quote, single quote
    \a  \b  \f  \v          BEL, BS, FF, VT (v5.7.13)
    \x##                    one byte, exactly 2 hex digits (`\x1b`)
    \u####                  one BMP codepoint as 1-3 UTF-8 bytes, exactly 4 hex digits
    \u{1..6 hex}            1-4 UTF-8 bytes, up to U+10FFFF (D800-DFFF refused)
    \ + LF                  KEEPS the LF (0x0A)
    \ + CR LF               keeps BOTH bytes (0x0D 0x0A)

⚠ SINCE 6.6.11 ANY OTHER BYTE AFTER `\` IS A LEX ERROR, reported at the backslash:

    var s = "ab\q";
    error:<source>:1:12: unknown string escape

cycc 6.6.10 and earlier dropped the backslash and stored the byte, so `"ab\q"` compiled to
`abq` with no diagnostic (char literals already refused an unknown escape). A comment-,
char- and string-aware scan of every cyrius source under ~/Repos and ~/.cyrius found no
other escape in use when this landed, so nothing downstream broke. Write a literal
backslash as `\\`.

⚠ `\` AT THE END OF A LINE IS NOT A C LINE-SPLICE. It is an escape whose value is the
newline, so the string still contains it:

    var s = "a\
b";
    # s is 3 bytes: 'a', 0x0A, 'b'

That is the v6.5.18 contract `cyrius fmt` relies on when it wraps a long literal
(tests/gates/toolchain/cyrfmt_string_continuation.sh). If you want the two halves JOINED,
end the first half with `"` and start a second literal; do not reach for `\`.

⚠ A NEWLINE INSIDE A STRING IS A SOURCE LINE (6.6.11). LEX's string loop stored a raw LF
(and a `\` + LF) without bumping its line counter, so after any multi-line string every
later token was reported ONE LINE HIGH PER NEWLINE:

    var s = "a
    b
    c";
    var t = 1;
    var x = ;      # line 5 — 6.6.10 reported `error:<source>:3:9: unexpected ';'`

A wrong line number was the mild symptom. Line 1 of the NEXT `include` landed on that
include's own `#@file` marker line and printed a bare `error:4:12:` with no file name; and
`private`, which decides a token's FILE from its line, attributed code to the wrong file,
so a call to another file's private fn COMPILED and the privacy error (if any) landed on an
innocent name (CVE-55). The "diagnostics in backend/x86/fixup.cyr are one line high" report
was this defect, triggered by the single raw-newline literal in parse_expr.cyr. Since 6.6.11
both paths count the line; the string token itself keeps its OPENING line, matching the
column its diagnostic head prints.

WHAT YOU CAN DO WITH A NEWLINE IN SOURCE: raw newlines inside a literal are legal and stay
in the string (as does `\` + newline). Neither changes line numbering any more, so there is
no reason to avoid multi-line literals for diagnostic accuracy.

GATES: tests/gates/frontend/lexer_errors_name_file_line.sh (axis 6: the line after a
multi-line string, the include's file name, and the `private` refusal of a cross-file call
that compiled on 6.6.10; axis 4: the escape rules). CROSS-REF:
field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
`file_attribution_is_a_line_lookup_so_the_lexer_line_counter_is_a_security_boundary`.

SHARED STORAGE (6.6.15). Identical literals share one copy (pointer-equal); a literal that
differs in ANY byte — an embedded NUL included — never shares. Until 6.6.15 a literal holding a
NUL could be aliased onto the tail of an earlier one and lose bytes to the next literal
(`"a\0a"` after `"a"` read `'z'` at offset 2 once `"zzzz"` followed), silently — so a project
rule of the form "no NUL bytes in string literals" (sankoch's ADR 004) is no longer needed.
Mechanism: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
`an_intern_search_that_writes_the_candidate_before_searching_must_stop_at_the_committed_end`.


[[entries]]
name = "struct_value_copies_and_result_type_checks_v6611"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.11 a struct copied OUT of a struct-typed field (`q = b.v`, `var p: P3 = b.v`) is a whole byte-exact copy; a struct-valued call / method / operator result is type-checked against a struct destination at EVERY size (field, assignment, declaration; in a fn, at top level and in the leading declaration block); a typed `self: T` of 8 bytes or less is passed BY VALUE; and `var y: Q = x` from an address-passed struct parameter copies instead of aliasing the caller"
category = "cyrius"
---
Five shapes that each stored ONE WORD (or aliased) silently before 6.6.11, on x86, aarch64,
PE and cx alike. The 6.6.10 half (a struct copied INTO a field) is in
field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml
`struct_field_types_checked_and_f64_fields_typed_since_6_6_10`.

1. A STRUCT VARIABLE TAKEN FROM A STRUCT-TYPED FIELD COPIES THE WHOLE STRUCT.

       struct Pt { x; y; }
       struct Box { v: Pt; n; }
       q = b.v;              # into a local, a global or a by-value param
       var p: Pt = b.v;      # p is its OWN copy — writing p.x leaves b.v alone

   Before 6.6.11 the assignment copied one word (`q.y` stayed stale) and the declaration
   stored that word into a slot typed `Pt` that was not inline, so the next `p.x`
   dereferenced it — rc 139 on x86 and aarch64, a page fault on PE, garbage on cx. A field of
   a DIFFERENT struct type is refused by name.

2. A TYPED `self: T` OF 8 BYTES OR LESS IS PASSED BY VALUE — `x.m()` passes `self` exactly as
   `T_m(x)` would.

       struct Odd { a: i8; b: i16; }          # 3 bytes
       fn Odd_sum(self: Odd) { return self.a + self.b; }
       x.sum();                               # 3, same as Odd_sum(x)

   Writing `self.a` inside such a method changes the method's copy, not `x`. Before 6.6.11 the
   dot form pushed `&x` for EVERY self, so a small typed self read its fields out of an
   address (-23905 / 11130 / 4047 on x86 / aarch64 / cx, want 3). Unchanged: an UNTYPED self
   (the `impl` form) is the receiver's address. A typed self OVER 8 bytes is address-passed —
   and since 6.6.16 copied on entry like every struct parameter, so it is a value too
   (struct_parameter_is_a_copy_v6616).

3. A STRUCT RESULT IS TYPE-CHECKED AGAINST A STRUCT DESTINATION AT EVERY SIZE. A call,
   method or operator returning a different struct is refused, whatever its size and whichever
   way it is stored:

       p = mkq();                 # cannot copy 'mkq' into a variable of a different
                                  #   struct/vector type: 'p'
       var z: Odd = y.same();     # fn return struct-id differs from declared var type
       h.o = y + y;               # cannot copy 'Odd_add' into a struct field of a
                                  #   different struct type: 'o'

   Before 6.6.11: a result of 8 bytes or less from a METHOD or OPERATOR was never checked (it
   arrives in rax untyped), and an ASSIGNMENT `p = mkq()` from a call returning a different
   struct compared the type only on some exits and otherwise fell to the scalar store — 24-,
   16- and 3-byte mismatches all compiled and stored one word. A generic whose inferred
   instance differs is refused too: `s = mk(r.v)` into a `Box<Pt>` infers `T = i64` (a FIELD
   argument is not a whole argument), so the call reaches `Box<i64>` — copy the field into a
   `Pt` first (`var p: Pt = r.v; s = mk(p);`).

4. `var y: Q = x;` FROM AN ADDRESS-PASSED BY-VALUE STRUCT PARAMETER COPIES. A by-value
   struct parameter over 8 bytes holds the caller's address; `y = x;` has copied through it
   since 6.6.6, but the DECLARATION bound `y` as a second pointer to the CALLER's struct, so
   `y.a = 9` changed the caller and `return y;` returned garbage. It is a copy now.

5. GLOBALS: THE LEADING DECLARATION BLOCK IS CHECKED LIKE EVERYTHING ELSE. Globals declared
   before the first top-level statement are replayed by a separate path (EMIT_GVAR_INITS), and
   that path had no struct checks at all:
     - `var G: W1<Pt> = ...;` there was typed as the generic BASE `W1` (`v: T` an i64), so
       `G.v.x` failed to parse and `w1s(G)` read the wrong storage. It is the `W1<Pt>`
       instance now, as after a statement.
     - a struct-valued call there — `var G: Pt = mkp(2);`, generic or not — built and
       SIGSEGV'd on `G.x`. It is refused as at any other top-level position: "'mkp' returns a
       struct by value, and a struct result needs storage in a fn's frame — call it inside a
       fn". A mismatched method / operator result into a struct global is refused too.

GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/struct_field_value_copy.tcyr (18 new rows, cross-OS),
tests/gates/frontend/struct_result_type_refused.sh (30 refusals at 3 / 8 / 16 / 24 B plus 11
same-type acceptances, each against a field-by-field control),
tests/tcyr/crossos/generic_struct_inference.tcyr `run_globals`, and
tests/gates/frontend/generic_type_arg_unknown_refused.sh axis Q.


[[entries]]
name = "typed_array_subscripts_v6612"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.12 an integer element-typed array `var a: T[N]` (T = i8..i64, u8..u64) takes a subscript: `a[i]`, `a[i] = v` and all ten `a[i] OP= v`, in a fn, at top level, in a `for` step and inside a closure. `a[i]` is exactly the element at `&a + i * sizeof(T)`, read sign- or zero-extended by T and stored truncated to T. There is NO bounds check and a fn-local array is NOT zero-initialised. A bare `var a[N]`, `stack var a[N]`, a scalar, a pointer, a u128 element and an f64 / f32 / bool / struct element are refused by name"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["arrays", "subscript", "typed-array", "element-width", "sign-extension", "v6.6.12"]
---
Before 6.6.12 the language had NO subscript, read or write: `return a[1];` was
`expected ';', got '['` and `a[i] = 5;` was `expected '=', got '['` — local or global, in a
fn or at top level — so `store64(&a + i * 8, v)` was the only spelling. (The roadmap item
"`var c: i64[4];` inside a fn is refused" was this same diagnostic misread as a declaration
error; the declaration always compiled.)

    var g: u32[3];                 # a global array (zeroed, like every global)
    fn f(): i64 {
        var t: i8[8];              # a fn-local array: NOT zeroed, see below
        var i = 0;
        while (i < 8) { t[i] = 0 - 120 - i; i = i + 1; }
        t[2] += 1;                 # all ten: += -= *= /= %= &= |= ^= <<= >>=
        var s = 0;
        for (var k = 0; k < 8; k = k + 1) { s = s + t[k]; }   # -987: i8 sign-extends
        var u: u8[4];
        u[0] = 300;                # stores 44: a store truncates to the element
        if (t[0]) { s = s + 1; }   # tests the ELEMENT
        return s;
    }
    g[2] = 7;                      # top level works too
    g[2] <<= 2;                    # 28

WHAT `a[i]` IS. The bytes `load16(&t + i * 2)` / `store16(&t + i * 2, v)` touch for an
`i16[N]`: the element at `&a + i * sizeof(T)`, read at T's width (i8/i16/i32 sign-extended,
u8/u16/u32 zero-extended) and written at T's width. The `load*` / `store*` idiom stays valid.
The index is any expression — a call, another subscript (`a[b[1]] + a[two()]`). A subscript
read carries T's width as the expression width, so storing an `i64` element into a narrower
slot draws the usual "narrowing assignment" warning.

⚠ NO BOUNDS CHECK. `a[N]` and `a[0 - 1]` compile and touch the neighbouring bytes, silently.
A `slice<T>` local keeps its own bounds-checked `s[i]` (slice_type_v58x), unchanged.

⚠ A FN-LOCAL ARRAY IS NOT ZERO-INITIALISED. Measured on 6.6.12: a fn fills its
`var b: i64[8]` with 77 and returns; a second fn reads its own `var a: i64[8]` before any
store, and `a[3]` is 77 — the first frame's leftovers. Globals are zeroed; a stack array is
whatever the stack held. Initialise before you read. (An array local over the per-fn frame
budget, about 120 KB, keeps STATIC storage and the compiler warns so.)

⚠ INSIDE A CAPTURING CLOSURE the subscript reads and writes the closure's captured COPY,
exactly as `&a` there does — captures are by value. Measured: `a[1] = 10`, then a closure
doing `a[1] = a[1] + 5; return a[1];` returns 15 while the enclosing fn's `a[1]` is still 10.

REFUSED BY NAME — one message, `cannot subscript 'a': ...`, that lists the reasons:

    var a[8];              # bare: no element width (bytes in a fn, slots at top level)
    stack var a[8];        # same
    var a: f64[4];         # f64 / f32 / bool / struct (Pt[4]) element: integer only
    var a = 5;             # a scalar
    var a: *i64 = p;       # a pointer: pointer subscripts are not in the language
    var a: u128[4];        # own message: a u128 element does not fit one register -
                           #   use load64/store64 on its two halves

For a bare array keep `load*` / `store*` at an explicit byte offset, or declare the width:
`var a: u8[N]` for bytes, `var a: i64[N]` for slots.

⚠ STILL OPEN at 6.6.12 (backlog): INSIDE A FN, `var a: f64[N]` AND `var a: Pt[N]` RESERVE
ONLY N BYTES. The fn-local path sizes a float or struct element array like a bare `var a[N]`
(the element type is not passed to it). Measured: two adjacent `var a: f64[4]; var b: f64[4];`
(or `Pt[4]`) sit 8 bytes apart, where two `i64[4]` sit 32 apart, so `store64(&a + 16, ..)`
writes into whatever follows. Top level sizes them N*8. Until it is fixed, spell a fn-local
float buffer `var a: i64[N]` (the same 8-byte slots) and move f64 bit patterns through
`load64` / `store64`, or alloc() it.

HOW IT IS BUILT. The declaration records a one-byte element descriptor (width 1/2/4/8/16,
0x40 = signed): a stack array keeps it in its depth word (`SLAEW` / `GLAEW`), a global — and a
fn-local array that fell back to static storage — in the per-var sign table as `0x100 | desc`,
a range disjoint from that table's scalar 0/1 (no heap-map change). `_arr_sub_addr`
(src/frontend/parse_expr.cyr) resolves the base exactly as `&a` does (a local, a closure
capture, a global) and computes the address; `_arr_sub_read`, `_arr_sub_stmt` and
`_arr_sub_assign` (shared with the classic-for step) load and store at the width, the compound
operators through the shared `_asg_compound_op`. Two compiler traps were closed on the way:
field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml `flags_reflect_rax_is_emitter_state_so_a_load_after_an_eaddr_must_clear_it`
and the sixth instance of `any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes_must_know_every_site_in_the_stream`.

BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a global array redeclared with a different element type (`var a: u8[8];`
then `var a: i8[8];`) is now the "redeclared with a different type or size" error —
signedness is part of a global's shape, as it already was for scalars. 6.6.11 accepted it.

NOT PART OF THIS: `*T` pointer subscripts, slice writes, an opt-in bounds-checked mode.

GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/typed_array_subscript.tcyr (69 rows: every width, local and
global, canaries around every array, the ten compound operators, index expressions, `if` /
`while` on an element, the for step, a closure capture, top level; green on x86,
`CYRIUS_IR=3`, aarch64, PE, cx and the four real hosts) and
tests/gates/frontend/array_subscript_forms.sh (15 refusals, 4 typed controls). The guide's
"Subscripts" section is the reference.


[[entries]]
name = "struct_literals_generic_heads_and_nested_values_v6612"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.12 a positional struct literal fills the struct's LEAVES in declaration order at every nesting depth, each at its own offset and width (a nested field used to be flattened at 8 bytes per inner field and never descended, writing past the object); a nested struct field also takes a whole struct VALUE of its type; and a generic struct literal names its instance, `Box<Pt> { p, 5 }` — it was `undefined variable 'Box'`"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["structs", "struct-literal", "generics", "nested-structs", "silent-corruption", "v6.6.12"]
---
THREE THINGS ABOUT `Name { ... }`, all on every backend (the literal is frontend-only).

1. NESTED LEAVES ARE WRITTEN AT THEIR OWN OFFSET AND WIDTH.

       struct Odd { a: i8; b: i16; }            # 8 bytes with padding
       struct HO  { o: Odd; t: i8; u: i32; }
       var H = HO { 0x11, 0x2222, 0x33, 0x44444444 };

   Before 6.6.12 the literal flattened a nested struct field as ONE 8-BYTE STORE PER INNER
   FIELD, whatever the inner widths, and never recursed: that literal wrote at +0, +8, +16,
   +17 — 13 bytes past the object, over the next global or (fn-local) the caller's frame —
   and every field but the first read back wrong. Measured with a canary global declared right
   after `H`: 6.6.11 got `H.o.a` right and nothing else, canary included; 6.6.12 gets all five.
   A struct nested TWO levels deep was never descended at all, so it took the wrong NUMBER of
   values. An all-i64 nesting is byte-identical before and after, which is why cycc's own
   fixpoint never noticed.

2. A NESTED STRUCT FIELD TAKES A WHOLE STRUCT VALUE.

       struct Point { x; y; }
       struct Box   { v: Point; n; }
       struct Outer { t; b: Box; }
       var a = Point { 2, 3 };
       var o  = Outer { 1, a, 4 };          # a fills Box.v (Point is Box's FIRST field's type)
       var o2 = Outer { 5, mkp(7), 9 };     # a call returning Point, likewise
       var b  = Box { a, 7 };               # the direct case

   The value may be a local, a parameter, a global, a struct-typed field, a call, a method or
   an operator result; it is copied byte for byte. A value whose type is on the field's
   FIRST-field chain fills that inner struct; a struct of any other type is refused by name
   ("cannot copy 'q' into a struct field of a different struct type: 'p'"). At TOP LEVEL a
   call returning a struct has no frame to land in and is refused by name ("'mkp' returns a
   struct by value, and a struct result needs storage in a fn's frame — call it inside a
   fn"), as the ONLY error. Before 6.6.12 `B2 { p, 5 }` with `p: Pt` was `unexpected '}'`;
   only the flattened `B2 { 1, 2, 5 }` worked.

3. A GENERIC STRUCT LITERAL NAMES ITS INSTANCE.

       struct Box<T> { v: T; n; }
       var G = Box<Pt> { 1, 2, 5 };                   # leading declaration block
       fn f() { var b = Box<Pt> { p, 7 }; var c: Box<i64> = Box<i64> { 9, 6 }; }

   All three literal heads (PARSE_VAR, the pass-1 global lookahead and the leading-block
   replay) committed only on `Name {`, so every form above was `undefined variable 'Box'` on
   6.6.11. One recogniser, `_lit_head`, now resolves the instance with the annotation's own
   resolver, so `var b: Box<Pt> = Box<i64> { .. }` is refused ("struct literal type does not
   match the declared type of this variable"). The leading declaration block now compares a
   literal with its annotation at all — `var G: Pt = Q { 1, 2 };` compiled on 6.6.11.

GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/struct_field_value_copy.tcyr (every field AND a canary after the
object, leading-block global and fn-local, two-level rows, 16 whole-value rows),
tests/tcyr/crossos/generic_struct_inference.tcyr (the LIT group) and
tests/gates/frontend/struct_literal_type_refused.sh (16 refusals, 4 acceptances). See also
generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310.


[[entries]]
name = "field_chains_any_depth_and_call_result_fields_v6612"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.12 a field chain reaches any depth for reading and writing (`n.v.v.x = 3;` stopped after two levels), and a `.field` applies to a call that returns a struct — in any expression position and as a bare statement, with chains, widths, f64 typing, a method on the result and a struct-typed field taken whole. At top level it is refused by name (no frame), as is an assignment to a result's field and a field of a call that returns no struct"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["structs", "field-access", "call-result", "temporaries", "v6.6.12"]
---
Both were LOUD before 6.6.12 — syntax errors, nothing compiled wrong.

FIELD CHAINS OF ANY DEPTH. `_resolve_leaf_field`, which serves the field load and the field
store, descended exactly ONE nested level (an `if`), so `n.v.v.x = 3;` was
`expected '=', got '.'` and `var r = n.v.v.x;` was `expected ';', got '.'` — through a local,
a global (inline or pointer-mode) and a by-value or `*T` parameter alike. It is a loop now
(a `..` range and a `.method(` after a nested field keep their own paths).

A `.field` ON A CALL RESULT:

    struct Pt  { x; y; }
    struct Box { v: Pt; n; }
    fn mk(a): Box { var b: Box; b.v.x = a; b.v.y = a + 1; b.n = 5; return b; }
    fn Box_total(self: Box): i64 { return self.n + self.v.x; }
    fn take(p: Pt): i64 { return p.x + p.y; }
    fn demo(): i64 {
        var y = mk(3).v.y;          # 4
        var s = mk(3).n + 1;        # 6 — an INTEGER (6.6.11 typed s as Box, wanted Box_add)
        var q: Pt = mk(7).v;        # a struct-typed field, copied whole
        var t = mk(2).total();      # a method on the result itself
        mk(3).total();              # ... and as a bare statement
        return y + s + take(mk(1).v) + q.x + t;
    }

Inside a fn the result lands in a frame temporary and the field is read through the same code
a named receiver uses: widths, sign-extension, f64 typing, deeper chains. Every return class
works (a <= 8 B struct in rax, 9-16 B in rax:rdx, larger through a retptr; a `Str` handle into
a pointer-mode slot).

REFUSED BY NAME:
- at TOP LEVEL, for every return class — `var G = mk(3).n;` and the bare `mk(3).n;` get
  "'mk' returns a struct by value, and a struct result needs storage in a fn's frame — call
  it inside a fn";
- an assignment to a result's field, `mk(3).n = 5;` — "cannot assign to a field of a call
  result: the result is a temporary";
- a field of a call that returns no struct — "cannot take a field of the result of 'sc': it
  does not return a struct".

STILL NOT SUPPORTED (unchanged, as for a named struct): a method on a NESTED field —
`mk(3).v.sum()`, like `b.v.sum()`, is `expected ';', got '.'`. Bind the field first.

COMPILER NOTE: the new factor arm parses its own call and must clear the literal-fold flag
AFTER the call, not before — see field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
`cfo_const_fold_rewind_class_four_occurrences_grep_the_shape_not_the_operator_and_every_tier_that_reenters_the_fold`
(occurrence 5).

GATES: tests/tcyr/lang/struct_field_chain_depth.tcyr (29 rows: 3- and 4-level read and
write through every base, packed i8/i16/i32 leaves checked on the storage, an f64 leaf),
tests/tcyr/crossos/generic_struct_inference.tcyr (the CF group, 30 rows; cx has no
register-pair struct return, so those rows are off there) and
tests/gates/frontend/call_result_field.sh (18 rows).


[[entries]]
name = "struct_field_arguments_and_toplevel_copy_init_v6612"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.12 a struct-typed FIELD passed to a by-value struct parameter (`take(r.v)`) is a COPY (inside a fn from 6.6.12; everywhere since 6.6.16, when the callee makes it), and a top-level struct copy-init (`var B: Pt = A;`, `var G: Pt = BX.v;`) gives the global its own bytes. Both used the source's FIRST WORD as the struct's address and SIGSEGV'd. The source of a top-level copy must be declared ABOVE the copy; a source of another struct type is refused by name"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["structs", "by-value", "copy-init", "globals", "declaration-order", "sigsegv", "v6.6.12"]
---
Two more places where a struct moved as one word, both SIGSEGV (rc 139 on x86 and aarch64,
a page fault on PE, 0 on cx) before 6.6.12. The 6.6.11 half (a struct copied OUT of a
field into a variable) is struct_value_copies_and_result_type_checks_v6611.

1. A STRUCT-TYPED FIELD AS A BY-VALUE ARGUMENT.

       struct P3 { a; b; c; }
       struct R  { k; v: P3; }
       fn take(p: P3): i64 { p.a = 100; return p.a + p.b + p.c; }
       fn f(): i64 { var r = R { 1, 10, 20, 30 }; return take(r.v); }   # 150; r.v.a stays 10

   A by-value struct parameter over 8 bytes is address-passed. For `take(r.v)` the call
   pushed the field's first word as that address, so the callee dereferenced 10 — from a
   local, a global, a by-value parameter and a pointer-mode base, and through `mk<P3>(r.v)`.
   Now the callee gets a COPY, so its writes do not reach `r.v`. From 6.6.12 to 6.6.15 the copy
   was the CALLER's (a frame temporary), so it existed only inside a fn: at top level the callee
   got the field's own address and wrote it (`take(BX.v)` left `BX.v.a == 100`), and a NAMED
   struct argument was never copied at all. Since 6.6.16 the callee copies every struct
   parameter on entry, so both hold everywhere (struct_parameter_is_a_copy_v6616). A field of a
   different struct type is refused ("cannot pass 'v' to a
   parameter of a different struct type in a call to 'take'"). Since 6.7.3 every struct
   argument is checked, not only a field: see struct_argument_type_checked_v673.

2. TOP-LEVEL COPY-INIT.

       var A  = P3 { 3, 4, 5 };
       var B: P3 = A;              # B has its OWN 24 bytes; A.a = 99 later leaves B.a == 3
       var BX = R { 1, 7, 8, 9 };
       var G: P3 = BX.v;           # from a global's field, any chain depth

   Pass 1 knew only the LITERAL form as an inline struct global, so `var B: P3 = A;` got ONE
   8-byte pointer-mode slot holding A's first word and `B.a` dereferenced 3 — in the leading
   declaration block and after the first statement alike. One predicate (`_gci_src`) is now
   asked by pass 1, by the leading-block replay and by PARSE_VAR's top-level arm: a whole
   source of the declared struct registers the global inline and byte-copies it. A source of
   another struct type is refused ("cannot copy 'A' into a variable of a different
   struct/vector type: 'B'"). A POINTER-MODE source (`var p: P3 = mk();` in a fn, or a
   pointer-mode global) keeps the pointer copy, as in a fn.

⚠ DECLARE THE SOURCE ABOVE THE COPY.

       var B: P3 = A;              # error: cannot copy-init 'B' from a global declared
       var A = P3 { 3, 4, 5 };     #   below it (declare the source first): 'A'

   Pass 1 cannot see a global declared later (so it registered the pointer-mode slot) while
   the replay sees a whole `P3`, and the store that disagreement fell back to was the crash
   again. And a global initialised at run time is initialised in DECLARATION ORDER, so even an
   inline copy would read the source before its init. The refusal covers a struct over 8
   bytes (measured at 12, 16 and 24 B); an 8-byte struct copy-init from a global declared
   below it compiles and reads 0 (measured: `G.a` is 0 for a `{ a: i32; b: i32; }`), as any
   forward reference to a run-time-initialised global does (`var b = a; var a = f();` —
   6.6.11 the same). The value is 0, not a crash, and the rule is the same: declare the
   source first.

GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/struct_field_value_copy.tcyr (15 field-argument rows, 11
copy-init rows, 94/94 on x86, aarch64, PE and the four real hosts) and
tests/gates/frontend/struct_copy_source_type_refused.sh (12 refusals, each the only error;
every one compiled clean on 6.6.11).


[[entries]]
name = "unterminated_string_literal_is_an_error_v6612"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.12 a string literal still open at the end of the source is `unterminated string literal`, reported at the OPENING quote's file:line:col — including at the end of an INCLUDED file, where the literal used to run on into the preprocessor's `#@file` resume marker and the error landed in the main file, on the wrong token"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["lexer", "strings", "diagnostics", "include", "v6.6.12"]
---
LEX's string loop treated end-of-input as a closing quote, so the error surfaced later and
somewhere else. It never compiled (the exit status was never 0), but the message pointed at
the wrong place:

    var a = 1;
    var s = "abc                 # 6.6.11: error:5:9: expected ';', got end of file
                                 # 6.6.12: error:<source>:2:9: unterminated string literal

    "abc                         # a bare one — 6.6.11: unexpected string

A `\` as the very last byte read one byte past the source as its escape; it now escapes
nothing and reaches the same report.

⚠ THE INCLUDED-FILE CASE was the misleading one, and it is the usual shape in a
`cyrius build` project. The preprocessor splices an included file into one stream and follows
it with a resume marker line, `#@file "<parent>" N`. A literal left open at the end of the
included file did not reach end-of-input at all: it ran into that marker, closed on the
marker's own quote, and every later quote paired wrongly. With `part.cyr` ending in
`var s = "oops;` and the includer declaring `var source = "fine";`:

    6.6.11: error:7:16: undefined variable 'source'      # the main file, a VALID literal
    6.6.12: error:part.cyr:2:13: unterminated string literal

Mechanism: a LF inside a literal followed by a WHOLE line in the marker's exact shape
(`#@file "<name>" <digits>`) is taken as the end of the included file. The whole line is
matched, so a valid string that merely contains `#@file ` still compiles.

GATE: tests/gates/frontend/lexer_errors_name_file_line.sh axis 4 (eight unterminated rows —
single-line, multi-line naming the opening line, trailing backslash, bare statement, four in
an included file — plus two anti-vacuous rows; 6.6.11 fails all eight). The escape rules and
line accounting are string_literal_escapes_and_line_accounting_v6611.


[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_xattr_statx_rlimit_fchown_wrappers_and_native_region_exemption_v6612"
description = "cycc 6.6.12 names the xattr family, fchown, statx and getrlimit in the stdlib (`sys_{,l,f}{set,get,list,remove}xattr`, `sys_fchown`, `sys_statx`, `sys_getrlimit`); on ELF-aarch64 most of them could not be reached by number at all, so the aarch64 peer spells them through the 1000+N private alias band. The raw-syscall literal warning is no longer raised inside a region compiled only for aarch64 (where the number IS native), and a wrong 3-arg `kill` warns again on x86-Linux"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["syscalls", "aarch64", "esysxlat", "alias-band", "xattr", "statx", "diagnostics", "darwin", "v6.6.12"]
---
THE WRAPPERS. Fifteen, in lib/syscalls_linux_common.cyr:

    sys_setxattr  sys_lsetxattr  sys_fsetxattr     sys_getxattr  sys_lgetxattr  sys_fgetxattr
    sys_listxattr sys_llistxattr sys_flistxattr    sys_removexattr sys_lremovexattr sys_fremovexattr
    sys_fchown(fd, uid, gid)   sys_statx(dirfd, path, flags, mask, buf)   sys_getrlimit(resource, rlim)

with `SYS_*` names in the x86_64, aarch64, x86-macOS and Windows peers. Linux: real
everywhere. macOS: `sys_fchown` and `sys_getrlimit` are real (Darwin 123 / 194 —
`RLIMIT_NOFILE` is 8 there, not 7); the xattr calls and `sys_statx` return -78, because
Darwin's xattr calls take two extra arguments and Darwin has no statx. PE and agnos: -38 for
all fifteen. Measured on 6.6.12: statx of `/`, getrlimit(7), getxattr on a missing path
(-2) and fchown(fd, -1, -1) all right on x86 and on aarch64 under qemu.

WHY aarch64 NEEDED THE ALIAS BAND. `ESYSXLAT`'s aarch64-Linux arm renumbers x86-compat
numbers so raw x86 syscalls keep working on ARM — and those row SOURCES are native aarch64
numbers too: fstat 5, lstat 6, poll 7, mmap 9, mprotect 10, munmap 11, brk 12, ioctl 16,
getsockopt 55. So native setxattr 5 ran fstat, lgetxattr 9 ran mmap, fremovexattr 16 ran
ioctl, fchown 55 ran getsockopt (qemu-aarch64 -strace). And the x86 numbers were no use
either: x86 lremovexattr 198 / fremovexattr 199 are aarch64's own socket / socketpair. So the
aarch64 peer declares them in the v6.5.7 **private alias band** — a source number >= 1000 no
OS will ever mint, spelled `1000 + native`:

    SYS_SETXATTR = 1005 ... SYS_FREMOVEXATTR = 1016   SYS_FCHOWN = 1055   SYS_STATX = 1291
    SYS_GETRLIMIT = 163      # native: neither a row source nor a row product

renumbered by fourteen pure rows at the TAIL of the ELF chain (below every compat row whose
source their product would re-match). getxattr 8, flistxattr 13, removexattr 14,
lremovexattr 15 and statx 291 are not row sources but take the band anyway: declaring
`SYS_GETXATTR = 8` natively would have silenced the raw-literal warning for x86 lseek (8),
because the generator drops a warning row for any number the aarch64 peer declares below 1000.
Cost: ESYSXLAT rows are emitted inline at every syscall site, so each ELF-aarch64 syscall site
grew 14 x 12 B = 168 B (the native aarch64 compiler +66 KB); x86 `cycc` is unaffected.
Mechanics and the ordering rule: field_notes/language/platform_abi.cyml
`raw_syscall_numbers_on_aarch64_and_the_darwin_at_flag_divergence`.

THE NATIVE-REGION EXEMPTION. The raw-literal warning (v6.5.51) runs in the parser, after the
preprocessor has already dropped the `#ifdef`, so a correct native call was told the x86 name:

    fn a(): i64 {
    #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64
        return syscall(8, path, name, buf, n);   # 6.6.11: "raw syscall 8 is x86_64 `lseek`;
    #endif                                       #  on ELF-aarch64 that number is `getxattr`.
        return 0;                                #  Use SYS_LSEEK ..." — 6.6.12: silent
    }

and spelling it through an `enum` folded to the same literal, so there was no way to mark a
number native. Since 6.6.12 the preprocessor writes `#@a+` / `#@a-` marker lines where a
TAKEN branch whose condition implies aarch64 opens and closes — `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64`,
`#ifplat aarch64`, `#ifndef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86`, and the `#else` of their opposites — and the
warning is skipped for a call inside one (a literal or an enum constant alike). Source text
that spells a marker is neutralised like a forged `#@file`. Outside such a region the warning
is unchanged.

⚠ SILENT IS NOT SAFE. The exemption removes a WRONG suggestion; it does not route anything.
A native number that is also a compat-row source is still rewritten before the `svc`, and now
without a word: measured on 6.6.12, `syscall(9, ...)` (native lgetxattr) inside
`#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` compiles silently and runs `mmap` under qemu. The guide's rule 3
stands: never write the aarch64-native number — use the peer's `SYS_*` name or the wrapper.

THE 3-ARG `kill` ARITY WARNING IS BACK ON x86-LINUX. 6.6.10 skipped the arity check for
Darwin's `kill(pid, sig, posix)` with no target test, and the Linux arity table runs on every
non-aarch64 backend, so `syscall(62, pid, sig, x)` compiled SILENTLY on x86-Linux from 6.6.10
on (6.6.9 warned). The skip is x86-macOS only now: x86-Linux warns `syscall arity mismatch`,
x86-macOS is silent for the 3-arg form and still warns for a 1-arg one (measured).

GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/xattr_statx_rlimit_fchown_wrappers.tcyr (62 rows; Linux, macOS,
PE, agnos), tests/gates/platform/esysxlat_row_order.sh (every alias the peer declares has its
row, in order), tests/gates/diagnostics/raw_syscall_native_exempt_and_kill_arity.sh (19 rows:
eight native shapes silent, six anti-vacuous rows still warn, the kill rows).


[[entries]]
name = "cx_tail_calls_and_cxvm_guest_traps_v6612"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.12 the cx backend compiles `return f(args);` as a real tail call (it was a call plus epilogue, so deep tail recursion overran cxvm's stacks and returned garbage), and cxvm TRAPS a guest fault instead of corrupting itself: every load/store, the syscall buffers, both VM stacks, the guest stack, a negative pc and an unknown opcode are checked (CVE-58). A trap prints `cxvm: <what> <value> at pc <N>` and exits 1. cxvm is still NOT a sandbox — the syscall opcode passes other numbers to the host raw"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cx", "cxvm", "bytecode", "tail-call", "sandbox", "CVE-58", "v6.6.12"]
---
TAIL CALLS. cx `ETAILJMP` emitted a normal call + epilogue, under a comment claiming a jump
broke when the arguments contained nested calls. They do not — the shared tail path has
popped every argument into r3.. before `ETAILJMP` — so it is now `mov sp, fp; popc fp;
jmp f`, the x86 `mov rsp, rbp; pop rbp; jmp` shape. Measured:

    fn tr(n, acc): i64 { if (n == 0) { return acc; } return tr(n - 1, acc + 1); }
    syscall(60, tr(2000000, 0) % 256);
    # 6.6.12 cycc_cx + cxvm: exit 128 (correct, 2.3 s interpreted)
    # 6.6.11 cycc_cx + cxvm: exit 1 — garbage from the 513th frame on

GUEST FAULTS TRAP (CVE-58). Before 6.6.12 `programs/cxvm.cyr` checked nothing a guest
controls: a guest address past the 1 MB data segment (or negative) read and wrote cxvm's own
heap — the register file, the stacks, the loaded code; guest address 0 (the in-memory copy of
the bytecode) stored silently; the 1024-entry call stack had no bound, so the 513th nested
frame overwrote the loaded code; `read` / `write` / `getrandom` / `clock_gettime` buffers ending
past 1 MB had the HOST kernel write cxvm's heap; an unknown opcode was a silent no-op. Now:

    fn f(): i64 { store64(2000000, 5); return 3; }
    # cxvm: guest address out of range: 2000000 at pc 44        (exit 1; 6.6.11 exited 3)
    store64(0, 5)          # cxvm: guest address out of range: 0 at pc 40
    load64(0 - 8)          # cxvm: guest address out of range: -8 at pc 48
    fn d(n) { return 1 + d(n + 1); }    # cxvm: guest stack overflow, sp 160 at pc 16

- every load / store is checked for its FULL width against `[8, _CX_MEM_SIZE)` — `[0, 8)` is
  the null page;
- the translated syscall buffers are checked for their full length (a path up to its NUL) and
  answer `-EFAULT` without reaching the host;
- both VM stacks hold 65536 entries (512 KiB each) and trap on overflow and underflow, so the
  guest's own 1 MB — not the host arrays — bounds recursion (non-tail depth 5000 returns the
  right value; it gave garbage past 512 before);
- `sub sp` traps before the guest stack reaches the loaded image (its globals and the
  `lib/alloc_cx.cyr` heap);
- a negative pc traps, an unknown opcode traps (`cxvm: unknown opcode 0xNN at pc <N>`).

No `.cyx` format bump: a newer `.cyx` on an older cxvm is refused only by the new cxvm's trap.

⚠ STILL NOT A SANDBOX. The syscall opcode (0x70) passes every number other than the
translated ones to the host kernel AS-IS, with the guest's raw register values — a guest can
issue any syscall the cxvm process may. Treat a `.cyx` you did not build like a native binary:
verify its origin, and run cxvm under the OS's own isolation when the input is not yours. The
contract is docs/platform-status.md "cyrius-x guest contract (cxvm)" in the cyrius repo.

ALSO cx at 6.6.12: a store to a narrow slot wrote 8 bytes (`EVSTORE_W` / `EFLSTORE_W`
ignored the width), clobbering packed narrow globals; both directions now use the sized
cxvm opcodes and a signed load sign-extends (see multi_width_types). And `thread_join` no
longer issues a FUTEX_WAIT with a guest offset (it spun on a Linux host): it returns -1 on
cx, where no thread can exist.

GATE: tests/gates/codegen/cx_tailcall_and_vm_traps.sh (axis A tail calls — a 2M-deep self
recursion, mutual recursion, nested-call arguments, a >6-arg call; axes B-D the traps; axis
E the narrow stores; every trap reddened by its own revert).

[[entries]]
name = "v6613_language_changes_builtins_array_sizes_global_layout"
description = "cyrius 6.6.13 language-visible changes: `f64_le` / `f64_ge` / `f64_trunc` are compiler BUILTINS (reserved names; no `lib/math.cyr` needed); `var a: T[N]` reserves N * sizeof(T) for EVERY element type; every global starts at its natural alignment; narrow globals initialise at their own width; a captured struct copies its bytes inside a closure; the Str -> `: cstring` warning sees call results, globals, fields, tail and method calls"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.6.13", "builtins", "f64_le", "f64_ge", "f64_trunc", "reserved", "typed arrays", "alignment", "globals", "closures", "Str", "cstring", "diagnostics"]
---
**Builtins (I5).** `f64_le(a, b)`, `f64_ge(a, b)` and `f64_trunc(x)` compile inline like `f64_lt` /
`f64_floor` (x86 `roundsd $3`, aarch64 `frintz`, cx opcode 0x6E). NaN semantics are unchanged: `f64_le`
and `f64_ge` are false when either side is NaN. The three names are now RESERVED (TOKNAME_BUILTIN holds
79 names), so a repo that still vendors a pre-6.6.13 `lib/math.cyr` (which defines them) fails with
`reserved keyword` at a pin bump until `cyrius deps` re-vendors it.

**Array sizes (M2).** `var a: T[N]` reserves `N * sizeof(T)` for every element type — f64 8, f32 4,
struct / union / generic instance STRUCTSZ, `*T` 8, vectors 16 / 32, bool / enum 8 — and the subscript
stride agrees. It used to reserve 8 bytes inside a fn for `f64[4]` or `Pt[2]` (N x 8 at top level for
structs). A generic `var a: T[N]` sizes its instance by STRUCTSZ. An unknown element type name is an
error. `var x[N]` (no type) is still N BYTES locally.

**Global layout (I9).** Every global starts at its natural alignment (1/2/4 for narrow scalars, 8 for
anything else). `var a: u8[N]` still gives N usable bytes; up to 7 bytes of padding may follow it. Code
that reached the NEXT global by adding to a global's address was always wrong; it is now visibly wrong.

**Narrow globals (M1).** `var a: u8 = 0;` (or a non-constant / call initialiser) stores 1 byte, not 8 —
it used to clobber the globals after it.

**Closures (M3).** Inside a closure, `b.v = p`, `var q: Pt = p` and struct literals copy a CAPTURED struct's
bytes, as outside a closure.

**Str -> `: cstring` (I11).** The warning fires for a `Str` named local or param, a call returning `Str`,
a `: Str` global (declared or inferred from its initializer), a `: Str` field, tail calls and method
calls, `x.data` and `str_data(x)` (a Str's data pointer is not NUL-terminated at its length). The fix is
`str_cstr(x)` (a NUL-terminated copy), `str_eq_cstr(x, "lit")` for comparisons, or a `: Str` parameter —
never `str_data(x)`. Warnings only: no binary changes.

[[entries]]
name = "indirect_calls_at_top_level_since_6_6_16"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.16 `callptr(f, ..)` and `fncall0..8(f, ..)` work at top level exactly as inside a fn, capturing closures included: a top-level `callptr` compiles (it was refused), and a top-level `fncallN` on a capturing closure that a fn built and returned no longer crashes (SIGSEGV; 0xC0000005 on Windows; 0 on cx)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["closures", "fnptr", "callptr", "fncall", "top-level", "cx", "v6.6.16"]
---
    include "lib/alloc.cyr"
    include "lib/fnptr.cyr"
    fn mk(b) { return |x| b + x; }      # captures b
    var g = mk(41);
    var a = fncall1(g, 1);              # 42 — top level, no wrapper fn needed (6.6.16+)
    var c = callptr(g, 1);              # 42 — a compile error before 6.6.16
    fncall1(g, 1);                      # statement position works too

Before 6.6.16 a top-level `fncallN` skipped the closure-aware lowering and called lib/fnptr.cyr,
which jumps to the closure's bit-63-tagged value; on cx it returned 0 for EVERY callee, plain fn
pointers too. The workaround was to call the closure from inside any fn
(`fn call1(f, a) { return fncall1(f, a); }`); it still works and is no longer needed.
A top-level indirect call opens its own small stack frame (on Windows with the usual stack
probe); inside a fn nothing changed. Still NOT closure-aware: an address-taken `&fncallN`
called through another indirect call runs lib/fnptr.cyr directly (and returns 0 on cx).
Compiler side: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
`top_level_has_no_frame_so_a_lowering_that_needs_one_must_refuse_or_bring_its_own`.

[[entries]]
name = "struct_assignment_handle_and_value_v6616"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.16 a struct ASSIGNMENT between a handle (a variable whose slot holds a plain struct's address: a pointer-mode local or global, a `*T` parameter) and a struct VALUE copies the struct — out of the pointee, or into it (`*a = q`). handle <- handle stays a pointer rebind at any pair of types; the DECLARATION `var q: T = a;` stays an alias from a pointer-mode local or global (from a parameter, a `*T` one included, it copies, as since 6.6.11)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["structs", "assignment", "copy", "pointer-mode", "handle", "v6.6.16"]
---
TERMS (plain structs over 8 bytes; Str / Result / Option / Tagged and <= 8 B structs are unchanged):
    HANDLE  one slot holding the struct's ADDRESS:
              var a: P3 = alloc(24);        # a pointer-mode local
              var G: P3 = 0; G = alloc(24); # a pointer-mode global
              fn f(p: *P3)                  # a `*T` parameter
    VALUE   the struct itself: an inline local or global (`var q: P3;`, `var G = P3{..}`), a
            by-value parameter (`q: P3`), a closure capture, a field `b.v`, a call / method /
            operator result.

THE RULE, for the assignment `dst = src;`:
    value  <- handle   copy the struct src points at            q = a;
    handle <- value    copy INTO the struct dst points at       a = q;  a = mk3(..);  a = b.v;
                       (`*a = q` in C) — a keeps its address    a = q.f();  a = l + r;  a = param;
    handle <- handle   REBIND (one word), at ANY struct types   b = a;  G = a;  p = q (two *T params)
    value  <- value    copy (unchanged)
    A source that is not a struct value — an untyped pointer, `alloc(..)`, a Str-returning call —
    still binds the handle. A struct value of a DIFFERENT type into a handle is refused by name
    (`cannot copy 'r' into a variable of a different struct/vector type: 'a'`).

WHAT IT WAS (every release before 6.6.16, x86 and aarch64 alike): each assignment arm stored ONE
word:
    q = a;      q.x = a's ADDRESS, q.y stale (printed 1397460178042889 where 12 is right)
    a = q;      a's slot = q.x's VALUE, the next a.x SIGSEGV'd; same for a = G, a = mk(..),
                a = b.v, a = q.dbl(), a = l + r, G = q
    a = q;      from a by-value PARAMETER: a pointed at the CALLER's struct (a.x = 9 wrote it)

THE ASYMMETRY IS DELIBERATE:
    var q: P3 = a;   # declaration: q takes its storage class FROM a. From a pointer-mode local
                     # or global -> a second pointer (alias). From a PARAMETER -> a COPY, and a
                     # `p: *P3` parameter counts (the 6.6.11 by-value parameter copy-init).
    var q: P3; q = a;# assignment: q is already inline -> a COPY
    The declaration chooses q's layout; an assignment cannot change it. For a private copy of a
    handle's struct, declare first and assign.

ONE SHAPE CHANGED MEANING: smuggling a handle through an inline struct variable —
    var w: P3; w = a; f(w);     # f(v) untyped, reads load64(v + 8)
now passes w's first FIELD (w is a real copy). Pass `a`, or `&w`. Nothing in the ecosystem did it.
Also: two `*T` parameters, `a = b;` — a rebind now (it copied `*b` into `*a`).

[[entries]]
name = "struct_parameter_is_a_copy_v6616"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.16 a parameter annotated with a plain struct is a VALUE at every width: over 8 bytes the struct still travels by address, but the callee copies it into its own frame on entry, so a write to the parameter never reaches the caller — whatever the argument (local, global, pointer-mode local, forwarded parameter, typed self, generic instance, fn pointer, field, top level). `p: *T` (called `f(&x)`) and an untyped self are the spellings for a callee that mutates. An `async fn` refuses such a parameter by name."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["structs", "by-value", "parameters", "copy", "self", "async", "abi", "v6.6.16"]
---
THE RULE (decided by the user, 2026-10-02):

    fn bump(p: P3): i64 { p.x = p.x + 1; return p.x; }
    var q: P3 = mk3(3, 0, 0);
    bump(q); bump(q);          # 4, 4 — and q.x is still 3

    fn bumpp(p: *P3): i64 { p.x = p.x + 1; return p.x; }
    bumpp(&q); bumpp(&q);      # 4, 5 — q.x is 5: a pointer parameter IS the caller's struct

  The same for a 16 B struct, an inline global (in a fn or at top level), a pointer-mode local
  (`var h: P3 = alloc(24);` — its heap object stays put), a parameter passed on, a typed
  `self: P3` (`q.bump()` == `P3_bump(q)`), a generic `bumpg<T>(p: T)` with T = P3, a call through
  `fncall1(&bump, &q)`, a field (`bump(b.v)`) and a top-level `take(BX.v)`. Untyped parameters,
  untyped `self`, `p: *T`, `Str` / `Result` / `Option` / `Tagged` (heap handles) and structs of
  8 bytes or less (values in a register) are unchanged.

WHAT IT WAS (every release before 6.6.16): the parameter was the CALLER's struct. `bump(q)` twice
gave 4 then 5 and left q.x at 5, for every argument form above; only a field argument inside a fn
was copied (6.6.12, caller-side — top level had no frame for it). The guide documented the alias.

HOW: the copy is made in the CALLEE's prologue (`_sptr_params_localize`, parse_fn.cyr), after
`_stkp_flush` has homed every parameter slot — a stack-passed pointer (SysV 7th+, aarch64 9th+,
Win64 5th+) is not in its slot before that. One site covers every caller, fn pointers included,
and the calling convention is unchanged. From there the parameter is an ordinary inline local.

CONSEQUENCES worth knowing:
  - A bare `q` where an untyped value is expected (`var k = q;`, `raw(q)` into an untyped
    parameter, `load64(q)`) is the struct's FIRST FIELD, as for any inline struct local. Before
    6.6.16 it was the caller's address. Write `&q` for the address (of the copy).
  - The copy happens on entry, so passing something that is not a struct's address (`take(0)`
    for a `q: P3`, e.g. to bind a generic's T) faults there even if the body never reads `q`.
  - An `async fn` with such a parameter is refused: "async fn parameter 'p' is a struct passed by
    value, which an `async fn` does not capture yet — pass a pointer to it (`p: *Pt`)". The body
    runs at force time, so an entry copy would read the caller's struct late.
  - Cost: one byte-exact copy per call (8/4/2/1-byte chunks) plus the struct's size in frame.
    cycc's own source declares no such parameter (self-compile unchanged).

GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/struct_arg_is_copy.tcyr, struct_param_byvalue_copy.tcyr,
struct_ptrmode_assign_copy.tcyr; tests/gates/frontend/coroutine_midbody_suspend.sh (the async
refusal). Compiler side: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
`a_cap_is_declared_in_one_place_and_believed_in_five_sweep_every_consumer` (the `*T` record moved
out of a 64-bit slot mask).

[[entries]]
name = "pointer_fields_returns_and_linked_lists_v6617"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.17 `*T` is a type wherever a type is written: a struct / union field `next: *Node`, a return type `fn f(): *Node` (and a multi-value return element), a local or global `var p: *Node`. A `*Struct` local, global, field or call result takes dot syntax like a `p: *Struct` parameter always did, and a field chain goes THROUGH a pointer field, so a linked list is written the obvious way"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["pointers", "typed-pointer", "structs", "field-chains", "linked-list", "v6.6.17"]
---
Before 6.6.17 `next: *Node` and `fn f(): *Node` were `expected identifier, got '*'`, a `var p: *Node`
local was `no struct type in scope` at `p.val`, and `a.next.val` was `expected ';', got '.'`.

    struct Node { val; next: *Node; }       # a field may point at its OWN struct
    fn link(n: *Node, v, head: *Node): *Node {
        n.val = v;
        n.next = head;
        return n;
    }
    fn push(head: *Node, v): *Node { return link(alloc(sizeof(Node)), v, head); }
    fn sum(head: *Node): i64 {
        var s = 0;
        var p: *Node = head;
        while (p != 0) { s = s + p.val; p = p.next; }   # the canonical walk
        return s;
    }
    var h: *Node = push(push(push(0, 1), 2), 3);
    h.next.next.val = 5;                    # chains through pointer fields, reads and writes
    var v = h.next.val + push(0, 7).val;    # ... and on a `: *Node` call result
    # (verified on the merged 6.6.17 tree: sum(h) == 10, v == 9, no warning)

`alloc(..)` returns an untyped word, so `var n: *Node = alloc(..);` warns `assigning non-pointer to
typed pointer`; hand it to a `*Node` parameter (as `push` does) or a `: *Node` fn instead.

RULES:
- `p = q` on two `*Node` REBINDS the pointer; it never copies the struct.
- T is resolved by name; an unknown T is refused (`unknown type 'X' as the target of pointer`).
  T may be the struct being declared or one declared BELOW (two structs can point at each other):
  such a field records T and is sized when it is used.
- `*Box<i32>` points at the INSTANCE (`sizeof(Box<i32>)`), not the base generic.
- The value a `*T` field load, a call, a method call or an explicit generic call yields is a
  `*T` — `p + n` steps `sizeof(T)` (see `typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617`).

⚠ NOT the same as a struct-typed local: `var p: Node = h;` with `h: *Node` COPIES the struct (a
walk written that way overwrites nodes), and for a struct of 8 B or less it stores the POINTER.
Bind vs copy for `Struct = *Struct` is an open v6.7.x question — write `var p: *Node`.

GATES: tests/tcyr/lang/pointer_field_return_types.tcyr (linked / chains / ptr_locals / call_ptr
groups, a linked-list walk), tests/gates/frontend/typed_pointer_warn_sign.sh axis 9. CHANGELOG
[6.6.17] (b3).

[[entries]]
name = "typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.17 `p + n`, `p - n`, `p += n` and `p -= n` on a `*T` step `sizeof(T)` bytes per element at EVERY site — local, parameter, closure capture, global in either zone, `*T` field, `: *T` result. `q - p` of two `*T` is the element count, a call result is a plain value unless the callee declares `: *T`, and `n + p` steps like `p + n`. Before, the step depended on where `p` was declared"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["pointers", "typed-pointer", "pointer-arithmetic", "sizeof", "behaviour-change", "v6.6.17"]
---
THE RULE (6.6.17, user decision):

    var buf: i64[8];
    var p: *i32 = &buf;
    p + 1          # &buf + 4      (sizeof(i32))
    var q: *Pt = arr;
    q + 2          # arr + 2 * sizeof(Pt)
    var e = (p + 3) - p;    # 3 — the ELEMENT count, a plain integer
    2 + p          # same as p + 2

- the same in a fn parameter `p: *T`, a closure capture of any depth, a global declared before
  or after the first statement, a `name: *T` field and a `fn f(): *T` result;
- `q - p` with different element sizes (`*i64 - *u8`) is refused by name — a pointer value
  carries only its step, so two types of one size cannot be told apart; `&a - &b` (untyped
  addresses) stays a byte difference;
- ⛔ a `*T` minus an UNTYPED var is `p - n`, NOT a byte difference: the var is read as
  an element COUNT. With `var base = &buf; var g: *i64 = &buf; g += 3;`, `g - base` is
  `g - base * 8`, a garbage address, with no diagnostic (measured 6.6.20: it equals
  `(base + 24) - base * 8` exactly, so its low byte moves run to run with ASLR). And
  `g - &buf` written directly is refused as `pointer difference of pointers with
  different element sizes`, even for an `i64[N]` array under a `*i64`. So the same
  subtraction gives an element count, a garbage address or a refusal depending on how
  the right side is spelled. To count ELEMENTS, subtract two `*T` (`var b: *i64 = &buf;
  g - b` == 3). For BYTES, bind BOTH sides to plain vars (`var x = g; x - base` == 24);
- a call's result is a plain value unless the callee declares `: *T`: with `p: *i64`,
  `f(p) + 1`, `load64(p) + 1`, `fncall1(&f, p) + 1` and `q.m(p) + 1` each used to add 8 (the
  expression scale was ambient state left by the last argument);
- `*p` still loads 8 bytes; a `*T` slot is always an 8-byte word.

WHAT IT REPLACED (measured on the 6.6.17 slot-open compiler): a local `*i8` / `*i16` / `*i32`
stepped 1 and every other local `*T` 8 (`*u8` and `*Pt` included); a parameter and a captured
`*T` 1 whatever T; a declaration-block global 8; a later global 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 by T; `p += n` 1
everywhere; `p + 1` on a `p: *f32` parameter was an f32 ADD of the pointer's bits and on a
`p: *Pt` it dispatched `Pt_add`. A `*T` parameter or capture also loaded as a non-pointer, so
`var q: *T = p;` warned `assigning non-pointer to typed pointer` — it no longer does.

MIGRATION: none measured — no `*T` declaration exists in the `src/` or `lib/` of 143 repos, so
every shape switched with no transition error (the user accepted that on 2026-10-05). The
teaching examples here use `*i64` locals, whose step is 8 under both rules.

`p[i]` on a `*T` is still refused (no element descriptor). GATES:
tests/tcyr/crossos/typed_pointer_step.tcyr, tests/gates/frontend/typed_pointer_warn_sign.sh axes
10-11. Supersedes the step table in field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml
`typed_pointer_local_slot_v6616`. CHANGELOG [6.6.17] (b7).

[[entries]]
name = "method_chains_v6617"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.17 a method call's result takes `.field` and `.method(..)`, chained to any depth, as a value and as a bare statement — `s.clone().cat(t)`, `p.bump().bump().sum()`, `mkp(3).bump().sum()`. Before, the `.` after a method call was a syntax error in every position"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["methods", "chains", "call-result", "Str", "structs", "v6.6.17"]
---
    var u: Str = s.clone().cat(t);       # a Str-returning chain
    var n = p.big().back().bump().sum(); # struct results of every return class
    p.bump().mu();                       # statement form; #must_use applies to the LAST method

Each link's result is held the way a free call's result has been since 6.6.12 (a > 16 B struct
in its retptr temp, 9-16 B in a temp, <= 8 B inline, a `Str` in a pointer-mode slot), so the
receiver is never touched.

REFUSED BY NAME: at TOP LEVEL a chain on a struct-returning method (`'T_m' returns a struct by
value, and a struct result needs storage in a fn's frame`); a chain on a method that returns no
struct (`cannot take a field of the result of 'T_m': it does not return a struct`).

STILL NOT SUPPORTED: a method call on a FIELD (`h.name.len()`, `b.v.sum()`) and compound
assignment on a field (`h.n += 1`) — bind the field first. An UNTYPED `var u = s.clone();`
does not take the method's struct type; write `var u: Str = ...`.

GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/method_chain.tcyr, tests/gates/frontend/call_result_field.sh C1-C3.
CHANGELOG [6.6.17] (b4).

[[entries]]
name = "nested_closure_capture_v6617"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.17 a closure nested inside another closure captures the variables of EVERY enclosing scope — the outermost fn's locals and params, the outer closure's params and locals, and what the outer closure itself captured — by value at construction, at any depth. The old guide rule `captured closures are flat` was a compiler limit written down as a rule, and is gone"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["closures", "capture", "nested", "scoping", "v6.6.17"]
---
    fn mk(b) {
        var g = |x| {
            var k = |y| { return y + b; };   # b: two levels out
            return callptr(k, x) + b;        # the outer keeps its own capture too
        };
        return g;
    }
    var add3 = |a| |b| |c| a + b + c;        # curried, three levels

- capture is BY VALUE at construction, as for one level (closures_v637_v638): a capture of a
  capture copies the word(s) out of the enclosing closure's env;
- a `var` declared in a block of a closure body (an `if`, a loop, a `for` header) hides an
  enclosing name only INSIDE that block — before 6.6.17 it hid it for the whole body, and a
  later read silently took a GLOBAL of the same name;
- a struct (by value or pointer-mode), a `cstring` and a `Str` capture keep their own type
  through every level, so dispatch on them is right;
- a nested `fn` inside a closure body is still refused by name (6.6.16) — define it at top level.

GATE: tests/tcyr/crossos/closure_nested_capture.tcyr. CHANGELOG [6.6.17] (b1). Compiler side:
the capture tables (`_cl_ncap`, `_cl_cap_slots`, `_cl_cap_w`) are per-closure state and are
saved / restored around each nested literal (`_cl_ctx_save` / `_cl_ctx_restore`).

[[entries]]
name = "nested_generic_type_arguments_v6617"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.17 type arguments nest wherever a type is written — `Vec<Box<i64>>` (the lexer's folded `>>` / `>>>` close two / three levels), `Box<Vec<i64>>`, `idv<Vec<i64>>(x)`, a field `items: Vec<Box<i64>>;` — `sizeof(Box<i32>)` is the instance's size in an expression and in `#assert`, and a multi-value return element takes type arguments, `(Box<i64>, i64)`"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["generics", "type-arguments", "sizeof", "multi-value-return", "v6.6.17"]
---
    struct Box<T> { v: T; w: T; }
    #assert sizeof(Box<i32>) == 8
    #assert sizeof(Box<Box<i32>>) == 16
    var x: Vec<Box<i64>> = vec_new();     # ran to end of file before 6.6.17
    fn two(a): (Box<i64>, i64) { return (a, 5); }   # was `expected ')', got '<'`

`sizeof(T<..>)` is exactly what `var b: T<..>` occupies (the instance, not the base generic).

STILL NOT SUPPORTED: a generic-struct FIELD `b: Box<i32>;` (`expected identifier, got '<'`);
`#derive` on a struct with a `Vec<Box<i64>>` field stops its field walk at the nested `<`.

GATE: tests/tcyr/frontend/nested_type_args.tcyr. CHANGELOG [6.6.17] (b5). Compiler side: every
`<..>` skipper must count levels and be able to OWE a closer to the list around it, because the
lexer folds `>>` into one token.

[[entries]]
name = "closeout_language_changes_v6620"
description = "What cycc 6.6.20 (the v6.6.x closeout) changed for a cyrius author: refusals that used to be silent miscompiles, a bare struct to a `*T` parameter, last-definition binding, reserved intrinsic names, preprocessor caps and TAB separators, and the manifest keys the CLI now confines."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.6.20", "closeout", "preprocessor", "pointers", "tail-calls", "manifest", "security"]
---
Language / compiler (each was a SILENT wrong value or memory corruption before 6.6.20):
- `continue` binds to its own loop when a `while` sits between two `for`s (was the outer loop's step).
- A `: f64` fn returns its f64 through a tail call, a bare `return;` and a fall-off (stale xmm0 on x86 / PE).
- `#inline` / small generic fns keep a `*T` parameter's sizeof(T) step; a generic `: *T` result steps sizeof(T).
- A bare struct passed to a `p: *T` parameter (or a `*S` operator operand) takes its ADDRESS at every size;
  a struct-valued call result there goes through a frame temp, refused by name at top level.
- A redefined fn binds EVERY call to its LAST definition, as the duplicate-fn warning says.
- A fn / var / param / enum variant / `use` alias named `sizeof`, `mulh64` or `fncall0..8` is refused by name.
- A tail call into a fn with a `*T` parameter keeps its `jmp` unless a frame address can reach the callee
  (inside a loop it still diverts) — deep `*Node` list walks no longer overflow the stack.
- aarch64: calls with 262+ arguments and frames past 64 KiB are encoded correctly.

Preprocessor: `#if`/`#ifdef`/`#ifndef`/`#ifplat` nest at most 64 levels (per file); at most 16
function-like macros (main source only; a redefinition or an included one is refused); `#define`
names count once (a redefinition's latest value wins); a macro's parameter list / one invocation's
arguments are at most 511 bytes and the argument count must match; TAB separates a directive's
words; a stray `#else` / `#endif` or an unclosed block is an error; `#ifplat` is evaluated in
included files. `use` aliases: at most 64.

CLI / manifest (security, CVE-79…CVE-102): `[package] cyrius` must be a version's shape (a leading
digit, `[0-9A-Za-z._-]`, no `..`); a manifest `[build] output` and `${file:PATH}` are confined to
the project by the `[embed]` path rules; `[deps.X] modules` entries may not leave the dep's tree;
project files are written through a symlink only to a file inside the project (never `.git`);
the crash-safe writers create their temp `O_EXCL|O_NOFOLLOW`; on Windows cmd.exe / certutil.exe
run by their System32 path.

GATES: see CHANGELOG [6.6.20] — each bullet names its test. Compiler side: the escape flag
`_fn_local_addr` + `_fnt_bvcp` (RPF-03), `_cont_vec` (RPF-01), `PP_PUSH_LEVEL` / `PP_MACRO_SLOT`.

[[entries]]
name = "traits_checked_v670"
description = "cyrius 6.7.0 traits: `trait` declarations with required and default methods, `impl X for T` checked against the trait, inherent `impl T {}`, the `T_Trait_m` qualified names and the collision rules, a typed `self` with arithmetic refused, and `var q: T = p` copying through a `*T`."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.0", "traits", "impl", "methods", "self", "dispatch", "ADR-007"]
---
DECLARE: `trait Show { fn show(self): i64; fn twice(self): i64 { return self.show() * 2; } }` — a
member ending in `;` is REQUIRED, one with a body is a DEFAULT. Only `fn` members. A trait or a member
declared twice is an error. Traits may be declared above or below their impls, in the declaration
section (before the first top-level statement), like `struct`.

IMPLEMENT: `impl Show for Point { fn show(self): i64 { .. } }` is CHECKED — an undeclared trait, a
method the trait does not declare, a different parameter count and a missing required method are
compile errors naming the trait. A default the impl does not define is instantiated for Point (parsed
from the trait's tokens with Point as the type; `self.show()` inside it calls Point's show).
Inherent methods with no trait: `impl Point { fn new(x, y): Point { .. } fn norm1(self) { .. } }`.

NAMES (static dispatch, ADR-004/ADR-007): a method is `Point_m`; every trait method is ALSO
`Point_Show_m`. Two traits giving Point an `m` make the plain `Point_m` / `p.m()` ambiguous — refused,
naming a qualified spelling; call `Point_A_m(p)`. Inside an impl/default of A, `self.m()` means A's.
An inherent `m` keeps `Point_m` (so `p.m()` picks it); the trait's is `Point_Trait_m`.

SELF: an untyped `self` in an impl is a `*T` — `self.x` reads and WRITES the receiver, and
`T_m(o)`, `T_m(&o)`, `o.m()` agree (before 6.7.0 `T_m(o)` on an 8-byte struct passed the value and
the method faulted). ⛔ Arithmetic on an untyped `self` (`self + 8`, `self[i]`, `self += n`) is
REFUSED: a `*T` steps sizeof(T), so the old `load64(self + 8)` idiom would have silently changed
meaning. Write `self.field`, or declare `self: *T` to step whole elements, or copy `var a = self;`
for a plain address.

COPY: `var q: T = p;` with `p: *T` copies `*p` at every size (it stored the pointer at <= 8 B and
aliased at > 8 B for a `*T` local); `var q: *T = p;` aliases.

CHAINS: `b.v.sum()`, `p.v.sum()`, `mk(3).v.sum()`, `c.w.v.scale(2);` — a method on a nested inline
field takes the field's address as `self`. `var u = s.clone();` types u from an 8-byte struct result.

Trait objects stay the `lib/trait.cyr` library pattern for now; compiler-CHECKED `dyn` (a 16-byte
{data, vtable} struct, the vtable built and verified from the impl) is decided for later in 6.7.x.
GATES: tests/gates/frontend/traits_checked.sh, impl_self_typed.sh; tests/tcyr/crossos/traits_checked,
impl_self_typed, impl_inherent, struct_from_pointer_copies, method_on_nested_field. ADR-007.

[[entries]]
name = "trait_bounds_v671"
description = "cyrius 6.7.1 trait-bounded generics: `fn f<T: Show + Eq>` and `struct Box<T: Show>` — a bound is a contract checked at the definition, every instantiation (the i64 base included) needs the impl, any type (a scalar too) satisfies a bound through its impl; plus a struct type argument in either slot of a two-parameter generic and a `b: Box<i32>` struct field."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.1", "generics", "traits", "bounds", "C3", "ADR-007"]
---
BOUND: `fn describe<T: Show>(v: T): i64 { return v.show(); }`; several: `<T: Show + Eq>`; on a generic
struct: `struct Shown<T: Show> { v: T; n; }`. A bound names a declared trait, takes no type arguments,
sits on one of the first two type parameters (only two are recorded), max 8 per parameter.

CONTRACT (user decision 2026-10-07): on a bounded `T`-typed parameter or local (`v: T`, `p: *T`,
`var q: T`), `v.m()` must be a method of a bound's trait — refused at the DEFINITION otherwise (a
method in no bound, or in two). The call is THAT trait's method for T's impl: with Pt giving `size`
through Size AND Other, `<T: Size>` calls Size's. Fields (`v.x`) are not in a trait — per instance.
An UNBOUNDED T keeps per-instance resolution (`fn f<T>(v: T) { return v.show(); }` works).

CHECKED AT EVERY INSTANTIATION, naming the trait and the `impl` it needs: `describe(r)`,
`describe<R>(r)`, and the i64 base — `describe(5)` needs `impl Show for i64`; without one the base is
the dead stub and every scalar path (call, tail call, `&describe`, an unbounded generic forwarding
its T) is refused. A struct's instances too: `Shown<R>`, a written `Shown<i64>`, a bare `Shown`.
`fn mk<T>(x: T): Shown<T>` / `struct W<T> { s: Shown<T>; }` are fine; only their i64 base is refused,
where used (`mk(5)`, a bare `W`).

SCALARS: `impl Show for i64 { fn show(self): i64 { return self + 1; } }` — a bounded call on an i64
T passes the value; `describe<i32>(7)` needs `impl Show for i32`. `self.m()` inside a scalar impl (a
trait default included) calls that type's own m (before 6.7.1: "no struct type in scope for 'self'").

ALSO 6.7.1: `g<Pt, i64>(p, 2)`, `g(5, p)`, `g(p, q)` — a struct in either slot of a two-parameter
generic (refused 6.6.10..6.7.0); `struct H { a: i64; b: Box<i32>; c: i64; }` (packed: Box<i32> is
4 + 8 = 12 B) and `w: Box<T>` inside `W<T>`; a field naming its own struct is refused. `#derive`
reads nested type arguments (`xs: Vec<Box<i64>>`); an inline generic instance in a #derive'd struct
hits its layout check, like any non-derived inline struct.

GATES: tests/gates/frontend/trait_bounds_checked.sh, generic_struct_field.sh,
generic_type_arg_unknown_refused.sh (K); tests/tcyr/crossos/trait_bounds, generic_two_param_struct,
generic_struct_field. ADR-007 decision 8. CHANGELOG [6.7.1].

[[entries]]
name = "const_and_const_fn_v672"
description = "cyrius 6.7.2 `const` (int / f64 / string, no storage, top level and in fns, any order) and `const fn` (runs at compile time in const contexts only, pure subset checked at the definition, bit-exact f64) — and the const contexts: array sizes, #assert, case labels, enum values."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.2", "v6.7.3", "const", "const fn", "comptime", "B1", "C1"]
---
CONST: `const LIMIT = 7;` `const PI = 3.14159;` `const NAME = "cyrius";` — NO STORAGE; each use is the
value (an int folds like an enum constant, an f64 is its literal, a string is its literal — passed to a
`Str` parameter it is wrapped like the literal). No type annotation (its type is its value's). `N = ..`,
`N += ..`, `&N` refused by name — and (6.7.3) a `for` step `for (..; ..; N = N + 1)`, which compiled in 6.7.2 and,
a top-level const's slot having size 0, stored over a neighbouring global (g0 7 → 100). Top-level consts: any order, forward use fine (a cycle is refused); one
top-level name is a const OR a global. Local consts: `const L = 3;` in a fn — visible to the rest of the
block, shadowed / shadowing by depth, a closure reads it by name (no frame slot, nothing captured).

CONST FN: `const fn fib(n) { .. }` is an ordinary fn; a CONST CONTEXT runs it at compile time, anywhere
else `fib(3)` is a plain runtime call (user decision: the disassembly shows the call you wrote). Body =
the pure subset, CHECKED AT THE DEFINITION even if never called: int and f64 arithmetic, comparisons,
&& / ||, shifts (`>>` logical, `>>>` arithmetic), `var x[: i64|i32|i16|i8|f64] = ..;` (narrow ones narrow
as at run time), local consts, `=` / `OP=`, if / elif / else, while, `for (;;)`-style with all three
clauses, `for i in a..b`, return / break / continue, const fn calls (recursion ok), exact f64 builtins
(f64_add..div, from, to, eq/lt/gt/le/ge, neg, abs, floor, ceil, trunc). NOT: load/store, arrays, `&x`,
fields, syscalls, globals, ordinary fn calls, transcendental builtins, generic const fns.

CONST CONTEXTS (any INTEGER const expression): a const's value, `var a[f(4)]` / `var a: i64[N * 2]` (top
level and in a fn), `#assert f(10) == 55 && N > 3` (any expression since 6.7.2), `case twice(N):`, enum
values `enum E { A = N; B = f(2); }`. ⚠ enum values and top-level array sizes are read in pass 1 in source
order: a const fn THEY call must be declared above them (a top-level const need not be).

SAME ANSWER AS RUN TIME: typing is the runtime's (f64 op iff the LEFT operand is f64; an int right
operand contributes its BITS). f64 is computed by an integer-only IEEE-754 module, bit-exact with x86-64
and AArch64. Refused by name: NaN (target-dependent bits), division by zero, f64_to out of range, a string
in arithmetic, > 10,000,000 steps, > 2,000 nested calls.

GATES: tests/gates/frontend/const_checked.sh; tests/tcyr/crossos/const_values, const_contexts,
const_fn_f64 (compile-time vs run-time BITS). CHANGELOG [6.7.2].

[[entries]]
name = "bool_true_false_not_v673"
description = "cyrius 6.7.3 `bool` / `true` / `false` / `!`: a WRITE into a bool (var, field, param, fn return, multi-return element, const, bool[N] list) takes only a boolean value; a bool READS as the integer 0 or 1; `!` is a real operator (it was silently dropped until 6.7.3)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.3", "bool", "true", "false", "!", "B2", "type check"]
---
VALUES: `true` = 1, `false` = 0, both reserved words; `bool` is an 8-byte word (sizeof 8, ADR-002).
`!e` = 1 when e's word is 0; binds like unary minus (its operand is a factor: `!x + 1` is `(!x) + 1`);
an f64 operand is tested by its raw bits (`!(-0.0)` is 0, as `if (-0.0)` is taken). ⚠ Until 6.7.3 the
lexer DROPPED a lone `!` — `!x` compiled as `x`, silently (CVE-104).

WRITES ARE CHECKED (user decision 2026-10-07): a bool local / global / field / parameter / `fn g(): bool`
return / multi-return element / const takes only a BOOLEAN value — true, false, a comparison (incl. the
f64_eq/lt/gt/le/ge builtins), `!x`, `&&` / `||`, another bool (var, field, param, capture, const, a
bool fn's call), or one in parens. Everything else is refused by name, `0` and `1` INCLUDED:
`var b: bool = 1;` `b = n;` `b += 1;` (every compound op) `p.on = 2;` `f(1)` for `on: bool`,
`return 1;` / bare `return;` in a bool fn. Arithmetic over a bool is an int (`ok + 0`, `-ok`,
`(a < b) + 1` refused). Untyped vars stay ints (`var x = a < b;` then `var c: bool = x;` refused).
Write `x != 0` to make a bool. Raw memory (store64, `*p = v`, ret2) is unchecked.

READS: a bool is 0 / 1 everywhere an integer is (`n + ok`, `ok * 4`, an i64 parameter). Conditions
still take any integer. Off the end of a bool fn returns `false`.

ELSEWHERE: consts are bool iff their value is (`const ON = !OFF;`, a `: bool` const fn) and every const
context takes them as 0 / 1; a `bool[N]` list takes true / false / bool consts; `#derive(Serialize)`
writes JSON true / false, the decoders store 1 for `true` and 0 otherwise, `#derive(accessors)` gives
`fn S_f(p): bool` and `fn S_set_f(p, v: bool)`. Checks are off under --syntax-only, not under
CYRIUS_TYPE_CHECK=0.

GATES: tests/gates/frontend/bool_checked.sh; tests/tcyr/crossos/bool_values.tcyr;
tests/tcyr/derive/derive_serialize_bool.tcyr. CHANGELOG [6.7.3].

[[entries]]
name = "struct_argument_type_checked_v673"
description = "Since cycc 6.7.3 a struct ARGUMENT whose static struct type differs from its parameter's is refused by name: every argument form, a generic instance against its base both ways, a by-value struct of 8 B or less and a Str handle parameter, an operator's two operands and a method's self. A typed pointer (`u: *Pt`), `&p` and an untyped value convert freely. Before, only a struct-typed field into an address-passed parameter was checked; every other mismatch compiled clean and the callee read the argument with its own layout."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["structs", "arguments", "type-check", "generics", "operators", "methods", "v6.7.3"]
---
The filing from the 6.7.1 open, fixed in the 6.7.3 repair lane. The user's rule: an argument
whose static struct type differs from its parameter's is refused, as the receive path refuses
`var q: Q = p;` (since 6.6.6).

    struct Pt { x; y; }
    struct Q  { a; b; c; }
    fn bq(b: Q): i64 { return b.c; }
    fn go(): i64 { var p: Pt; p.x = 3; p.y = 4; return bq(p); }
    # error: cannot pass 'p' to a parameter of a different struct type in a call to 'bq'

MEASURED on 6.7.3's slot-open cycc; every program compiled clean:
  - bq(p) gave 0: it read past the 16-byte Pt.
  - bs(mk1(p)), a Box<Pt> into `fn bs(b: Box)`, gave 4 (b.v.y) where n was 8.
  - A global Pt into bq gave 97. `q + p` with `fn Q_add(a: Q, b: Q)` gave 1.
  - `q + mkp()`, `q + p.dup()` and `sl(p)` into `s: Str` SIGSEGV'd.

CHECKED — the argument has a static struct VALUE type: a named inline or pointer-mode local, a global
(in a fn and at top level), a closure capture, a field, a free call (named by the fn), a method or
operator result at every size (named by the method fn) — through a call, a method's arguments and a
tail call, into a by-value parameter of any size, a `p: *T` or a `s: Str`; an operator's two operands
(parameters 0 and 1 of the operator fn) and a method's self.

A generic INSTANCE is its own struct: a Box<Pt> passed to `b: Box` is refused, and the reverse. So is
`gx(b)` for `fn gx<T>(b: Box<T>)`: T is not inferred through Box<T> (filed); write `gx<Pt>(b)`.

ACCEPTED: a TYPED POINTER of another struct (`u: *Pt` local, parameter, global, field or call result
into `*Q` or `Q`) converts freely, like `&p` and the receive path's `var q: *Q = u;` (user decision,
2026-10-08); an untyped variable; a literal; a call returning an integer or a pointer; a fn pointer.

HOW (internals): SFPSID also records a by-value struct of 8 B or less and a Str handle parameter
(`_psid_plain`) — neither has a struct-mask bit, so every ABI reader tests `_pm_struct` before GFPSID.
Such an argument is consumed by `_sarg_byval_small` (the caller's own PCMPE + EPUSHR). A 9-16 B method
or operator result nothing stores is recorded with `_sc_tmp` -4; a whole field by the passive `_fpk_*`
record. Nothing emits: a 649-file differential corpus compiled byte-identical.

GATES: tests/gates/frontend/struct_arg_type_refused.sh (41 refusal rows, 12 acceptances,
mutation-proven) and tests/tcyr/crossos/struct_arg_type_accepted.tcyr (38 rows). CHANGELOG [6.7.3].

[[entries]]
name = "if_expression_v674"
description = "cyrius 6.7.4 B3: `if (c) { a } elif (d) { b } else { e }` is an expression wherever an expression is parsed — one expression per branch, else required, only the taken branch runs, every branch one kind (integer / f64 / f32 / one struct of <= 8 B), const contexts and array-list elements included."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.4", "if expression", "conditional", "ternary", "B3", "const context"]
---
SYNTAX: `var v = if (n > 0) { 1 } elif (n < 0) { 0 - 1 } else { 0 };` — each branch ONE expression in
braces (no statements, no `;`); `else` REQUIRED; only the taken branch runs. C's `c ? a : b` is not
cyrius: `?` is the Result propagation operator. At the START of a statement `if` is the if statement.
It is a FACTOR: `if (c) {a} else {b} + 1` is `(if ..) + 1`.

KINDS: every branch an integer (pointers, bools included), an f64, an f32, or ONE struct type of
<= 8 B (the result keeps its type: `var z: S = ..`, struct arguments, operator overloads see it).
Refused by name: a kind mix ("if expression branches differ in type: an integer here, an f64 before
it"), a struct over 8 B, a vector, u128, a `: stack` pair (branch on `&x`). All-boolean branches make
a bool (B2). An untyped var holding a float is an INTEGER (ADR-002) — declare it `: f64` — and
`var t = if (c) {1.5} else {2.5};` stays untyped like `var t = 1.5;`.

CONST CONTEXTS: const values, local consts, array sizes, #assert, case labels, enum values, const fn
bodies — the evaluator runs the taken arm and WALKS the rest (an untaken `1 / 0` never faults). There
a string is its own kind (`const B = if (D) {"dbg"} else {"rel"};`); a string/int mix is refused; at
run time a string literal is a pointer word, so `if (c) {"a"} else {0}` compiles. Since 6.7.4 every
GLOBAL ARRAY-LIST ELEMENT is a const context too (comparisons, && / ||, !, const fn calls, f64 const
arithmetic are legal; a bool[N] list takes `{1 < 2, !false}`).

LIMITS: a call in a branch is never a tail call (deep recursion through one grows the stack); an
explicit #inline fn holding one keeps the "control flow" warning.
GATES: tests/gates/frontend/if_expr_checked.sh; tests/tcyr/crossos/if_expr_values.tcyr. CHANGELOG [6.7.4].

